const lessonSlides = {"lessons":[{"id":"u1-w01-l01","lesson":1,"title":"Plan a badge-cost program","prepare":"Whiteboard or paper; chapter open. No Python installation needed for this lesson.","slides":[{"title":"Plan a badge-cost program","body":["Unit 1 · Week 1 · Lesson 1","Explain the problem before writing code."],"minutes":2,"notes":"Introduce the week: plan, run, trace/test, then apply. Ask learners to explain what a successful badge calculator would do. This lesson is diagnostic and formative.","strategy":"Learning intention"},{"title":"What does a program need?","body":["A club sells badges for $3 each.","Think alone: what input do we need?","Compare with a partner: what should four badges cost?","Share your method, then try six badges."],"minutes":5,"notes":"Allow 45 seconds of silent thinking, one minute comparing, then invite two explanations. Expected: quantity is input, 4 × 3 = $12, 6 × 3 = $18. Accept diagrams or spoken explanations. If learners add 3 to the quantity, use equal groups to model the price per badge.","strategy":"Think, pair, share"},{"title":"Today’s goal","body":["I can identify input, processing and output.","I can write calculation steps in order.","I can predict a result before testing."],"minutes":6,"notes":"Have learners restate one criterion in their own words. Distinguish the variable quantity from the stated fixed price. Return to these criteria at the exit check.","strategy":"Success criteria"},{"title":"The badge requirement","body":["Display the cost for a valid badge quantity.","Each badge costs $3.","Assume a whole-number quantity of zero or more.","Input validation is taught later."],"minutes":10,"notes":"Think aloud as you underline quantity, circle the price and identify the required output. Model why zero is allowed by the stated requirement. Ask everyone to identify one valid input before continuing. If several learners choose a negative quantity, revisit the assumption. Validation comes later.","strategy":"Teacher modelling"},{"title":"A worked example: four badges","table":[["Stage","Badge example"],["Input","Quantity: 4"],["Processing","4 × 3 = 12"],["Output","Total: $12"]],"minutes":10,"notes":"Model input 4, processing 4 × 3 and output Total: $12, explaining each decision. Then learners create the same three rows for quantity 6 without copying the numbers. Ask everyone to show their processing row. Expected $18. If they write 6 + 3, model equal groups again before independent practice.","strategy":"Worked example and guided practice"},{"title":"An algorithm for any quantity","body":["Write the steps without looking at the example.","Trace your steps for 0, 1 and 6 badges.","Explain your sequence to a partner.","Revise any step your partner cannot follow."],"minutes":12,"notes":"Allow six minutes independently, three minutes of explanation in pairs, then three minutes to revise. Expected sequence: read quantity, multiply by 3, display total. Expected totals $0, $3, $18. Support: offer the three stage names Input, Processing, Output. Stretch: explain why displaying total before assigning it fails. Keep individual plans as evidence.","strategy":"Independent practice and peer explanation"},{"title":"Which rule matches the requirement?","body":["For six badges, choose a rule on paper.","A: quantity + 3     B: quantity × 3","Show your choice together.","Explain what the other rule gets wrong."],"minutes":5,"notes":"Collect a simultaneous response before discussion so every learner commits. Correct B gives $18. A gives $9 and adds a price once rather than charging for each badge. If several choose A, use a drawing of six groups of $3 and ask a fresh check for two badges before moving on.","strategy":"All-student misconception check"},{"title":"Exit check: seven badges","body":["Name the input.","State the processing step.","Give the expected displayed total.","Explain why an expected result is useful."],"minutes":5,"notes":"Collect an individual written response without partner help. Expected input quantity 7, processing multiply by 3, Total: $21. An independent expectation checks correctness. Sort responses into ready, input/processing confusion and calculation support, and use these groups to plan the next lesson starter.","strategy":"Independent exit ticket"}]},{"id":"u1-w01-l02","lesson":2,"title":"Run your first Python script","prepare":"School-approved Python 3 editor/IDE, writable practice folder and projector. Check the school’s Run command before class.","slides":[{"title":"Run your first Python script","body":["Unit 1 · Week 1 · Lesson 2","Turn the badge algorithm into a saved program."],"minutes":2,"notes":"Use the school-approved environment. Do not ask learners to install software or change school settings during the lesson.","strategy":"Learning intention"},{"title":"Retrieve the badge plan","body":["Close your notes for one minute.","Name the input and calculation step.","Predict the cost of six badges.","Compare with your notes and correct one detail."],"minutes":5,"notes":"Use one minute of individual retrieval, then compare and correct. Expected quantity, quantity × 3, $18. Ask everyone to show the calculation. If confusion remains between input and processing, briefly return to the three-row badge example before opening the editor.","strategy":"Retrieval practice"},{"title":"Today’s goal","body":["Save and run a Python script.","Enter suitable data when prompted.","Compare actual output with a prediction."],"minutes":6,"notes":"Success: the learner saves badge_cost.py, runs it independently, identifies where to enter the quantity and records a matching prediction/result.","strategy":"Success criteria"},{"title":"From demonstration to your own file","body":["Watch the teacher save badge_cost.py.","Point out the editor and output area.","Create your own file in this week’s folder.","Show your partner where Save and Run are."],"minutes":10,"notes":"Demonstrate in the school-approved editor for three minutes, then pause for learners to repeat each step. Ask partners to point to controls on their own screens, without taking over. Check everyone has a saved .py file before continuing. Save stores changes and Run executes them. Provide a prepared folder or printed instructions for setup support. Avoid filenames that shadow modules, such as random.py.","strategy":"Demonstration with guided rehearsal"},{"title":"The badge script","code":"BADGE_PRICE = 3\nquantity = int(input(\"Number of badges: \"))\ntotal = quantity * BADGE_PRICE\nprint(f\"Total: ${total}\")","minutes":10,"notes":"Read the code top to bottom and connect each line to the paper plan. Ask learners to point to the line that reads input before you explain it. input returns text and int converts suitable whole-number text. Pause before the total line and elicit a prediction for quantity 4. print displays the result. Uppercase BADGE_PRICE signals an intended fixed value, but Python does not enforce it. Model the reasoning rather than only typing.","strategy":"Code reading and teacher think-aloud"},{"title":"A prediction before each run","body":["Write your prediction for four badges.","Run the saved script and enter 4.","Compare expected and actual output with a partner.","Repeat for 1 and 0, predicting each time."],"minutes":12,"notes":"Use individual predictions before learners work in pairs. Driver runs the file and navigator checks the prediction and explains a line, then swap for the next quantity. Expected $12, $3, $0. If a result differs, inspect the saved file and input before changing code. Each learner retains a prediction/result record. Use suitable inputs only.","strategy":"Predict, run, investigate"},{"title":"One change, a new prediction","body":["Change BADGE_PRICE from 3 to 5.","Predict four badges before running.","Explain why the input line stays the same.","Return the price to 3 after checking."],"minutes":5,"notes":"Expected $20. Ask all learners to write why quantity still needs input while the fixed price changes. If the output stays $12, check saving and which file is running. Support: highlight the price line. Stretch: predict a new price and quantity before testing. Keep the original file structure.","strategy":"Controlled variation and explanation"},{"title":"Exit check: run independently","body":["Show your saved Python file.","Run it for two badges at $3 each.","State the expected output before running.","Explain the difference between Save and Run."],"minutes":5,"notes":"Each learner saves and runs their own file for quantity 2 at price 3, predicting Total: $6 first. Ask for a spoken or written explanation of Save versus Run. Record setup difficulties separately from conceptual errors. Start the next lesson with a short demonstration for learners who need environment support.","strategy":"Independent performance check"}]},{"id":"u1-w01-l03","lesson":3,"title":"Trace, modify and test a sequence","prepare":"Working badge_cost.py at price 3; paper trace table or worksheet; Python editor.","slides":[{"title":"Trace, modify and test","body":["Unit 1 · Week 1 · Lesson 3","Explain what changes as each instruction runs."],"minutes":2,"notes":"Learners should already be able to run the badge script. Keep a clean copy available so setup errors do not consume the tracing lesson.","strategy":"Learning intention"},{"title":"Retrieve the script","code":"BADGE_PRICE = 3\nquantity = int(input(\"Number of badges: \"))\ntotal = quantity * BADGE_PRICE\nprint(f\"Total: ${total}\")","body":["Predict the output for quantity 5."],"minutes":5,"notes":"Learners predict Total: $15 individually before running. Ask them to annotate which line reads input, which calculates and which displays. Compare annotations in pairs. If they treat print as the calculation, trace the total assignment explicitly before moving on.","strategy":"Retrieval and code annotation"},{"title":"Today’s goal","body":["Trace variable values in execution order.","Modify a calculation to match a requirement.","Record expected and actual results."],"minutes":6,"notes":"Success: complete a trace for a new input, add a fixed charge correctly, and explain at least one meaningful test. A trace records state after each instruction, not just the final answer.","strategy":"Success criteria"},{"title":"Trace four badges","table":[["After instruction","Value known"],["BADGE_PRICE = 3","BADGE_PRICE is 3"],["Read quantity 4","quantity is 4"],["Calculate total","total is 12"],["Display total","Output: Total: $12"]],"minutes":10,"notes":"Think aloud through the model for quantity 4. Cover the model or move off this slide and ask learners to trace quantity 2 using blank rows. Expected price 3, quantity 2, total 6 and output Total: $6. total has no assigned value before its calculation. Support: retain instruction labels. Stretch: explain which values remain unchanged at each line. Check every learner’s total row before the next task.","strategy":"Worked trace and faded support"},{"title":"Add a fixed packaging charge","code":"PACKAGING = 2\ntotal = quantity * BADGE_PRICE + PACKAGING","body":["The $2 charge applies to every order, including zero badges.","Predict the totals for quantities 0 and 3."],"minutes":10,"notes":"Model adding PACKAGING = 2 above the total calculation. Learners explain in pairs why packaging is added once, then independently predict quantity 0 ($2) and 3 ($11). Check answers before implementation. Contrast the stated zero-order rule with possible alternative requirements without changing this task. Multiplication precedes addition here.","strategy":"Guided prediction and code modification"},{"title":"Test the modified program","table":[["Quantity","Expected","Actual"],["0","$2","Record your run"],["1","$5","Record your run"],["3","$11","Record your run"]],"minutes":12,"notes":"Driver implements and runs the modification. Navigator checks the requirement and compares expected with actual output. Swap roles after the first case. Every learner keeps their own test record. Expected $2, $5, $11. Keep expected values unchanged when actual differs. Support: provide the correct input and output lines. Stretch: find a quantity where an incorrect packaging-per-item calculation accidentally passes (quantity 1).","strategy":"Paired testing with role changes"},{"title":"Two rules, different costs","body":["For quantity 3, which rule charges packaging once?","A: quantity × 3 + 2     B: quantity × (3 + 2)","Choose alone, then explain to a partner.","Why does a test with quantity 1 miss the error?"],"minutes":5,"notes":"Take simultaneous A/B responses before partner discussion. A gives $11 and B gives $15. Both are syntactically valid, so B is a logic error for this requirement. Quantity 1 produces $5 for both and cannot distinguish them. If several choose B, model one packaging charge separately and recheck quantity 2: $8 versus $10. A syntax error prevents Python parsing the instructions.","strategy":"Hinge question and error analysis"},{"title":"Exit check: a changed order","body":["Badges cost $3 each. Packaging is $2 per order.","Predict the cost of six badges.","Give the expected result for quantity 0.","Explain which test could reveal a packaging error."],"minutes":5,"notes":"Collect individual responses: quantity 6 gives $20 and quantity 0 gives $2. A zero test exposes the packaging-per-item fault because it gives $0. Look for a requirement-based explanation and an independent expected result. Use errors to choose a brief trace or fixed-charge starter next lesson.","strategy":"Independent exit ticket"}]},{"id":"u1-w01-l04","lesson":4,"title":"Build a book-cover calculator","prepare":"Python editor, a new cover_cost.py file and a test-record template. Keep the badge example available as a reference.","slides":[{"title":"Build a book-cover calculator","body":["Unit 1 · Week 1 · Lesson 4","Plan, implement, test and explain a new problem."],"minutes":2,"notes":"This is formative transfer practice, not an additional summative assessment. Let learners use their earlier notes while making their own design and test decisions.","strategy":"Learning intention and transfer"},{"title":"Retrieve a useful test habit","body":["Write why we predict before running.","Explain a fixed charge using the badge example.","Compare explanations with a partner.","Add one detail that makes your answer clearer."],"minutes":5,"notes":"Allow one minute of writing, two minutes comparing, then two minutes sharing corrections. Expected: an independent prediction checks correctness, a fixed charge applies once per order and a trace finds the first wrong value. Invite learners to use a small numerical example if language is a barrier.","strategy":"Retrieval with peer explanation"},{"title":"The book-cover requirement","body":["Each book cover costs $2.","Every order has a $4 handling charge.","The handling charge also applies to a zero-cover order.","Assume the quantity is a whole number of zero or more."],"minutes":6,"notes":"Learners underline the input, circle the two prices and mark the zero-order rule in their own notes. Ask everyone to predict whether quantity 0 costs $0 or $4 and explain. Correct $4 under this requirement. Success means an IPO plan, readable saved script, three predicted tests and an explanation of a line. Validation comes later.","strategy":"Requirement annotation"},{"title":"Design before coding","body":["Draft your own input, processing and output plan.","Write the calculation in words.","Ask a partner to follow the steps for two covers.","Revise your plan before coding."],"minutes":10,"notes":"Five minutes independent design, three minutes partner walkthrough, two minutes revision. Expected quantity × 2 + 4, so two covers cost $8. Partners ask questions without writing the solution for each other. Support: offer a blank IPO frame. Stretch: explain how a change to handling would affect every order. Suggested names COVER_PRICE and HANDLING.","strategy":"Independent design and peer critique"},{"title":"Expected results come from the rule","table":[["Covers","Your working","Expected total"],["0","Calculate on paper","Record prediction"],["1","Calculate on paper","Record prediction"],["5","Calculate on paper","Record prediction"]],"minutes":10,"notes":"Learners calculate all three cases individually before any program runs. Expected 0 × 2 + 4 = $4, 1 × 2 + 4 = $6 and 5 × 2 + 4 = $14. Ask for working, then compare in pairs. Keep answers in teacher notes so the slide does not give away the task. Support: model a different quantity, 2 gives $8. Stretch: find a test that distinguishes adding handling once from adding it per cover.","strategy":"Independent prediction with reduced support"},{"title":"Your own working calculator","body":["Create and save cover_cost.py.","Use your plan to write the calculation.","Display a labelled result and run your tests.","Record actual results beside your predictions."],"minutes":12,"notes":"Allow independent work, with short teacher conferences. Offer the badge example or a partial file only to learners who need it, then ask them to explain the changed lines. Reference: COVER_PRICE = 2; HANDLING = 4; quantity = int(input('Covers: ')); total = quantity * COVER_PRICE + HANDLING; print(f'Total: ${total}'). Stretch: change one fixed value, design a fresh test and justify it. Do not introduce input validation in this task.","strategy":"Independent practice with optional scaffolds"},{"title":"Feedback that leads to a revision","body":["Show a partner one prediction and actual result.","Explain why handling is added once.","Partner: ask one question about the evidence.","Revise one detail and name a limitation."],"minutes":5,"notes":"Each learner explains while the partner checks against the requirement. Expected test results $4, $6, $14. Feedback should identify a specific match or mismatch, not just say good work. Learners revise their own code or explanation. A suitable limitation is that invalid text and negative quantities are not handled. Keep the original expectation if a run fails.","strategy":"Peer feedback and self-explanation"},{"title":"Exit check: a fresh quantity","body":["Predict three covers without running the code.","Explain one input, processing or output line.","Which check helped you find or avoid an error?","Save your script and test evidence."],"minutes":5,"notes":"Collect individual responses before showing the answer: $10. Ask for a specific check such as predicting zero, tracing a total or comparing an actual result. Use responses to identify learners needing setup, arithmetic or tracing support before next week’s types and operators. The task remains formative.","strategy":"Independent exit ticket and reflection"}]},{"id":"u1-w10-l01","lesson":1,"title":"Integrating the session summary","chapter":"design","prepare":"Bring working quantity/input, calculation and storage modules from the Week 9 build workshops, synthetic data copies and project workbook. These slides support the existing learning project. Any graded use follows the separately issued school task and conditions.","slides":[{"title":"Integrating the session summary","strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Success means connect the session list to a summary, handle empty/tied cases and explain the distinction between current-session data and saved history. Assume the Week 9 implementation workshops have produced tested modules. Diagnose missing modules and use an isolated synthetic fixture for summary work rather than pretending integration is complete.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 10 · Lesson 1","Connect tested modules and check the complete workflow."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: the order contract","strategy":"Individual retrieval and paired explanation","notes":"Expected quantity zero is valid, cost is integer cents, saving must succeed before adding to the session list and the list contains this run’s accepted orders. Prior saved records remain in the file. Ask students to point to the relevant interface or requirement before discussing their implementation.","body":["When may a quantity enter the session list?","Why is zero a valid order?","Which values belong to this session’s summary?","Compare your answer with your module design."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Summary expectations","strategy":"Predict and justify","notes":"Students calculate before revealing/discussing. For [2,0,5], total 7, cost 2100 cents, first maximum index 2. For [5,5], total 10, cost 3000, index 0. Empty requires a clear message and no index access. Indices are zero-based. If presentation uses order number, distinguish index 0 from displayed order 1. Record expectations in workbook section 4.","table":[["Session quantities","Total quantity","Cost cents","First max index"],["[2, 0, 5]","7","2100","2"],["[5, 5]","10","3000","0"],["[]","Empty message","No total required","No index access"]],"minutes":6},{"title":"An empty list needs its own path","strategy":"Teacher modelling and paired trace","notes":"This language-neutral pseudocode isolates the empty case before initialising a best index. Strict greater-than preserves the first maximum on a tie. Learners trace [5,5] and then [] on paper. Agree a documented return/display interface with the mainline. The pseudocode names totals without supplying a complete implementation, so students use their own tested traversal or suitable taught operations.","body":["Trace [] and [5, 5] through your design.","Which operation must the empty branch avoid?"],"code":"IF quantities is empty\n    REPORT empty session\nELSE\n    CALCULATE quantity and cost totals\n    FIND first largest-order index\n    REPORT totals and index","minutes":8},{"title":"The integration boundary","strategy":"Failure scenario and think-pair-share","notes":"Scenario: quantity 4 is valid but the append fails. Expected existing session [2,0,5] remains unchanged, totals still refer to saved orders, clear failure message and an agreed retry/cancel route. An integration test checks what happens between modules, beyond individual calculation checks. Ask students to locate the exact point their list changes. Work only with disposable data; simulate a failure deliberately through a test stub or teacher-controlled disposable setup.","body":["The quantity is valid, but saving the order fails.","Should the session list change? Explain why.","Where does the mainline learn whether saving succeeded?","Plan a safe test using disposable data."],"minutes":7},{"title":"Integration workshop","strategy":"Independent implementation with peer explanation","notes":"Students connect summary to their existing mainline, keeping the agreed interfaces. Run normal [2,0,5], tied [5,5], empty, single zero and save-failure cases. Record actual evidence and corrections. Partner explains data movement, but each learner owns their implementation. Support: provide a synthetic list so summary logic can be tested separately. Extension: compare an isolated summary test with the complete workflow. If graded, apply the issued collaboration rules.","body":["Integrate the summary using your own module design.","Execute the normal, tie and empty-session cases.","Check that a failed save leaves session data unchanged.","Record actual results and explain one interface."],"minutes":17},{"title":"Session or saved history?","strategy":"All-student hinge question","notes":"Correct B. The R5 summary concerns the current session list; R4 preserves the file across runs. Reading all historical quantities into today’s session would change the requirement. Ask for a two-run test that checks both properties independently. A different client requirement could permit a history report, but it would need explicit agreement.","body":["The file contains yesterday’s orders and today’s order.","R5 asks for a summary of the current session.","A: Include every saved order.   B: Use this run’s list.","Choose and justify the data source."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Checkpoint: an integrated workflow","strategy":"Independent exit ticket","notes":"Retain one normal result, an empty/tie result and one safe failure result linked to requirements. A prediction alone is insufficient execution evidence. Ask students to identify remaining gaps honestly. Carry open failures to the next testing workshop, prioritised by which required behaviours they block.","body":["Show actual normal and empty/tie results.","Explain one module-to-mainline data transfer.","Record the outcome of a save-failure check.","Name the highest-priority fault still open."],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w10-l02","lesson":2,"title":"Testing, correction and retesting","chapter":"design","prepare":"Bring an integrated practice program, a disposable folder, synthetic test fixtures and workbook section 4. Preserve a clean starting copy for each destructive or failure test.","slides":[{"title":"Testing, correction and retesting","strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Success means execute a requirement-linked test set, record expected and actual results honestly, isolate failures and verify repairs with regression checks. This is the existing project testing workshop, not an extra weighted test.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 10 · Lesson 2","Use test evidence to decide what still needs repair."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: useful test evidence","strategy":"Individual retrieval and peer explanation","notes":"A desk-check predicts state; an executed test records actual behaviour. The expected result comes from the brief/independent calculation. A repair requires rerunning the failed case and earlier relevant cases. Distinguish unit tests of one module from integration tests of cooperating modules.","body":["What separates a planned test from an executed test?","Why calculate the expected result first?","Which earlier tests should run after a repair?","Give a unit test and an integration test."],"minutes":5},{"title":"A test set follows requirements","strategy":"Test selection and prioritisation","notes":"Students assign IDs and precise expectations. R1 needs integer text conversion, range and retry cases, including zero. R2 ordinary/zero costs. R3 list order and zero retention. R4 separate records across two runs and a safe failure. R5 empty, single, several and tied maxima. This overview is not the complete test log. Ask for invalid ID cases: empty, comma and line break.","table":[["Requirement","A useful test family"],["R1–R2","Invalid input, zero and ordinary costs"],["R3","Accepted quantities remain in entry order"],["R4","Two runs, separate records, write failure"],["R5","Empty, one, several and tied largest"]],"minutes":6},{"title":"Boundary and recovery cases","strategy":"Guided prediction and partner challenge","notes":"For the non-negative integer quantity rule, -1 rejects, 0 accepts, 1 accepts. three and 2.5 reject as integer text. A retry sequence three,-1,0 should finish with accepted zero and no rejected values saved. Ask students to add a blank ID and a comma-containing ID test, with a later valid value. Define the response before executing.","body":["Quantity boundary: -1, 0 and 1.","Conversion recovery: three, then -1, then 0.","ID checks: empty or comma-containing text.","Write expected responses before executing each case."],"minutes":8},{"title":"Reproducing a failure","strategy":"Teacher think-aloud and minimal-case debugging","notes":"Use the chapter tie case [5,5] returning index 1 instead of 0. Reproduce with the smallest two-item fixture, trace remembered best index, identify >= versus >, then correct and rerun the tie case. Also rerun [2,0,5], a single item and empty. Students must identify the cause rather than changing several unrelated lines. The example is a debugging illustration, not claimed evidence about their own program.","body":["A tied session returns index 1 instead of index 0.","Reduce the case to [5, 5].","Trace the remembered index before changing code.","Repair the cause, then rerun the relevant earlier tests."],"minutes":7},{"title":"Evidence workshop","strategy":"Independent testing with teacher conferences","notes":"Students execute their planned tests using resettable fixtures, log results, repair one priority fault and retest. Columns: test ID, requirement, setup/input, expected, actual, pass/fail, correction and retest evidence. Do not mark unexecuted cases as passed. Support: teacher models one record and provides a trace template. Extension: inject a known failure in a private copy to see whether the tests expose it. Use only disposable files for file-mode and failure trials.","body":["Execute the requirement-linked tests in your workbook.","Record actual outputs and evidence locations.","Isolate and repair one priority fault.","Retest it and check for regressions."],"minutes":17},{"title":"An honest result record","strategy":"All-student hinge question","notes":"Correct B. The learner has only predicted or desk-checked, so the log should show planned/not executed. A screenshot of code is not evidence that the particular test ran. Discuss why an honest open failure is more informative than a false pass. Ask for the exact additional evidence needed to change the status.","body":["You predicted that a second run keeps both records.","You have not yet performed the second run.","A: Mark pass.   B: Record planned / not executed.","Choose and state the missing evidence."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Checkpoint: readiness for a user trial","strategy":"Independent readiness decision","notes":"Retain completed logs and explain whether the core workflow can be trialled safely. If core defects remain, specify them and fix before an unassisted trial, or clearly scope the trial and record the limitation. Do not invent successful results. Teacher checks that saved sample files use synthetic data and tests cover the agreed requirements.","body":["Show a completed test with independent expectations.","Show one correction and its retest.","Identify any unresolved requirement or failure.","Decide whether the core workflow is ready for a trial."],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w10-l03","lesson":3,"title":"The organiser acceptance trial","chapter":"design","prepare":"Prepare a working practice build and clean synthetic file, an organiser scenario card and workbook section 5. Partner roles represent a fictional organiser; record a role label rather than a real name.","slides":[{"title":"The organiser acceptance trial","strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Success means observe whether a user can complete agreed tasks, collect evidence without leading the user and prioritise improvements against requirements. Use the existing learning project and issued collaboration rules if it forms part of a school assessment.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 10 · Lesson 3","Observe whether the organiser can use the solution."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: who judges what?","strategy":"Individual retrieval and comparison","notes":"A unit test checks a small module, integration checks cooperation, and acceptance checks the agreed client workflow. Passing calculations alone does not show whether the organiser can recover from bad input or recognise a failed save. Ask students to choose one acceptance criterion already written in week 8.","body":["What does a unit test establish?","What extra question does an acceptance trial ask?","Choose one agreed organiser acceptance criterion.","Explain how you would observe it."],"minutes":5},{"title":"A scenario with observable outcomes","strategy":"Worked trial plan","notes":"Provide a clean practice folder and one synthetic ID per order. Script: create order of 2, enter invalid quantity then recover with 0, inspect the records and request the summary. Current-session totals are 2 and 600 cents; first largest index 0. For persistence, restart and add quantity 1 under a new ID; previous records remain while new-session summary is 1 and 300 cents, index 0. Empty and tied cases were tested previously and may be included if time permits. Agree exact success criteria without giving click-by-click prompts.","table":[["Organiser action","Observe"],["Create a synthetic order","Prompts and accepted result"],["Recover from unsuitable quantity","Message and successful retry"],["Inspect saved record","Correct separate fields/record"],["Request the session summary","Correct totals and clear output"]],"minutes":6},{"title":"Roles and observation","strategy":"Role rehearsal and teacher modelling","notes":"One learner acts as organiser, another as developer/observer. The organiser has a goal and synthetic data; the observer has the acceptance criteria. Record actions, output, hesitation and requests for help without collecting unnecessary personal details. A screenshot or short written record of the relevant output is sufficient if school rules allow it. If the developer intervenes, record the intervention honestly. In a controlled assessment, follow permitted collaboration conditions.","body":["Organiser: attempt the goal using the supplied data.","Observer: record actions, outputs and requests for help.","Let the organiser try before giving guidance.","Record any intervention and its reason."],"minutes":8},{"title":"Feedback linked to a requirement","strategy":"Classify evidence and discuss","notes":"Compare vague praise with a useful observation. “R1: after three, the message appeared, but the prompt did not return” identifies a failed recovery. “R5: totals correct, but cents label absent” identifies an interpretation/usability issue. Record whether each concerns a requirement failure, clarity issue or optional enhancement. Do not let requested extras such as avatars expand the assessed scope automatically.","body":["“Looks good” gives little evidence.","“After three, I could not enter a new quantity” is specific.","Link feedback to a requirement or clarity issue.","Separate required repairs from optional extra features."],"minutes":7},{"title":"Acceptance workshop","strategy":"Paired user trial and role exchange","notes":"Use the scenario from slide 3 with synthetic inputs, allow an initial attempt without coaching and record the result for each acceptance criterion. Exchange roles so each learner observes their own implementation being used. Support: give a short goal card and observation table. Extension: ask the organiser to explain the session/history distinction in their own words. If software is incomplete, record a scoped trial and its limits rather than claim full acceptance. Allocate about eight minutes per direction and one minute to identify priorities.","body":["Run the agreed scenario without leading the organiser.","Record outcomes and specific feedback.","Swap roles and trial the second solution.","Identify the most important required improvement."],"minutes":17},{"title":"A change needs another check","strategy":"All-student hinge question","notes":"Correct B. Record what changed, retest relevant functional cases, then repeat the affected acceptance action. A clearer prompt should not accidentally change validation or stored data. Learners explain whether the change affects just the interface or the underlying behaviour. Keep both observations as evidence of improvement.","body":["You change a confusing quantity prompt after feedback.","A: Assume acceptance now passes.","B: Retest the affected behaviour and repeat the user action.","Choose and explain which evidence to retain."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Checkpoint: an acceptance record","strategy":"Independent exit ticket","notes":"Retain the scenario, role label, criterion-level observations, interventions, feedback and one prioritised action. Include the repeated trial if a repair is already complete; otherwise record the next action. Use this evidence in the final evaluation, avoiding claims that every requirement was met if the trial did not examine it.","body":["Keep the scenario and observed outcomes.","Link one observation to an agreed requirement.","Record one justified change or remaining limitation.","Carry the evidence into your evaluation."],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w10-l04","lesson":4,"title":"Demonstration, evaluation and retrospective","chapter":"design","prepare":"Bring code, synthetic data, project workbook and honest test/acceptance records. Use the existing learning-project checkpoint. Any grading, authorship checks or submission rules come from the separately issued school task.","slides":[{"title":"Demonstration, evaluation and retrospective","strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Success means demonstrate required behaviour, judge requirements using actual evidence, explain limitations and reflect on the development process. No new marks, weights or assessment conditions are introduced by these slides.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 10 · Lesson 4","Demonstrate the solution and evaluate the evidence."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: a claim needs evidence","strategy":"Individual retrieval and paired challenge","notes":"An evaluation refers to the requirement and actual test/observation. “All tests passed” is insufficient if missing tests leave a requirement unexamined. Predicted results are planning. A retrospective concerns the development process, whereas product evaluation judges the solution. Ask for one actual example from each learner’s workbook.","body":["What evidence supports “R4 is met”?","Can an unexecuted test justify a success claim?","How does product evaluation differ from a retrospective?","Share one claim and let a partner ask for evidence."],"minutes":4},{"title":"A focused demonstration","strategy":"Teacher modelling of evidence selection","notes":"Students plan a short demonstration: ordinary order, unsuitable input recovery, retained records across a restart and empty/tied summaries. Use synthetic values and prepared resettable fixtures. The demonstration supplements the test log; it cannot cover every possible case. Identify the actual version shown. Where time is tight, use two-minute peer demos and selected teacher conferences rather than a whole-class presentation by every student.","body":["Identify the version and synthetic test data.","Show the core workflow and input recovery.","Demonstrate persistence and empty/tied behaviour.","Point to the matching workbook evidence."],"minutes":6},{"title":"Evaluation against requirements","strategy":"Worked evidence comparison","notes":"These are illustrative statements, not evidence about a student’s work. R2 met: recorded 0,300,1200 cents for quantities 0,1,4. R4 partly met: two-run persistence passed but save-failure recovery remains unhandled. R5 not yet evidenced if empty/tie cases are unexecuted. Distinguish not met from not tested. Students use met/partly met/not met and explicitly record evidence gaps without converting untested into a pass.","table":[["Requirement","Judgement","Evidence / limitation"],["R2: badge cost","Met in tested cases","0, 1, 4 gave 0, 300, 1200"],["R4: save records","Partly met","Two runs pass; failure recovery open"],["R5: summary","Evidence incomplete","Empty/tie tests not executed"]],"minutes":8},{"title":"The developer retrospective","strategy":"Individual reflection with paired challenge","notes":"Ask students to compare planned and actual milestones, identify a concrete delay and support one improvement with evidence. Example: mixed file writing with input logic made unit testing difficult; next time separate those responsibilities earlier. Retrospective concerns how the learner worked, not another generic product feature list. Include source/assistance log and explain own contribution under school rules.","body":["Compare the planned schedule with actual progress.","Explain one decision that helped or delayed testing.","Propose one process change and justify it.","State your own contribution and acknowledge assistance."],"minutes":7},{"title":"Demonstration and evaluation workshop","strategy":"Peer demonstration, independent writing and teacher conference","notes":"Allow about six minutes for paired demonstrations and questions, nine minutes for individual evaluation/retrospective, three minutes to assemble evidence. Each learner retains their own code, data and reasoning. Teacher conferences prioritise unresolved authorship or requirement questions; apply school conditions if assessed. Support: requirement, judgement, evidence, limitation sentence frame. Extension: explain a design trade-off and the test that could evaluate an improvement.","body":["Demonstrate your solution using a small scenario.","Write requirement-by-requirement evaluation.","Complete the retrospective and source log.","Assemble code, synthetic data and the evidence workbook."],"minutes":18},{"title":"An honest handover","strategy":"Peer checklist and independent verification","notes":"Check actual files exist, open in the intended environment and correspond to the version demonstrated. The handover includes code, synthetic saved data and workbook with tests, acceptance, evaluation and sources. Record known limitations and how to start the program. Do not publish student data, accounts or a peer’s work to the public textbook. These are learning handover checks, not additional school submission rules.","body":["Does the delivered version match the demonstration?","Can another person find the start instructions?","Are tests, acceptance evidence and sources included?","Are remaining faults and limitations clearly recorded?"],"minutes":5},{"title":"Exit ticket: one justified next step","strategy":"Independent synthesis","notes":"Collect a final requirement claim with its evidence, a limitation and one justified process improvement. Teacher uses patterns to plan feedback and future recovery work. The textbook moves to networking next; unfinished practice tasks need a realistic agreed recovery action rather than silently adding new project deadlines to the exam block.","body":["Name one requirement and its actual evidence.","State one remaining limitation.","Explain one process improvement for the next project.","Keep the completed workbook for future reference."],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w11-l01","lesson":1,"title":"Protocols and shared resources","chapter":"networks","source":"https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#networks","prepare":"Open the networks chapter and its learning resources. Use paper or a local editor as appropriate, with fictional data and disposable practice copies. The suggested 55-minute lesson is flexible.","slides":[{"title":"Protocols and shared resources","body":["Unit 1 · Week 11 · Lesson 1","Explain why networked devices need shared rules."],"minutes":2,"strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Explain the goal and the expected independent outcome. Explain why networked devices need shared rules. This is ungraded textbook learning. Suggested times can be adjusted to the class. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#networks. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Retrieval and explanation","body":["Which school resources do devices share?","What could go wrong if message formats differ?"],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual retrieval, then partner comparison","notes":"Examples include files, printers and a web service. Shared formats and responses let devices interpret a request. A cable alone does not specify the message meaning. Allow silent thinking before discussion. Ask a learner to justify an answer before revealing the explanation. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#networks. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"A network request","table":[["Part","Library example"],["Client","Laptop requesting a page"],["Resource","Library web service"],["Protocol","Rules for request and response"],["Connection","The links carrying the data"]],"minutes":6,"strategy":"Worked table and teacher explanation","notes":"Distinguish a service from its communication rules and physical connection. Use the fictional library scenario. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#networks. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"A useful request","body":["One device sends a request for a library page.","The service interprets it and returns a response.","Name a rule both ends need to agree on."],"minutes":10,"strategy":"Predict, model and explain","notes":"Accept message format, request meaning or response interpretation. HTTP is an application protocol. More layers handle transport and local delivery. Pause for predictions before discussing the expected result. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#networks. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Protocol sorting","body":["Match web requests, email transfer and name lookup.","Use HTTP, SMTP and DNS.","Explain the job of each choice."],"minutes":10,"strategy":"Guided paired practice","notes":"HTTP handles web messages, SMTP transfers email, DNS provides name-related lookup information. HTTPS protects HTTP using TLS. Swap explaining and checking roles so both learners articulate the reasoning. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#networks. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Independent practice","body":["Choose a shared resource.","Describe its client, request and response.","Explain why a connection and agreed rules matter."],"minutes":12,"strategy":"Independent application with optional support","notes":"Pairs draft first, then each learner explains a changed resource independently. Accept a printer example if its service and rules are distinguished clearly. Offer the relevant worked example if needed, then use a changed case to check independent understanding. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#networks. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"A choice to explain","body":["Two devices share a cable connection.","A: That alone defines every service message.","B: They still need shared communication rules."],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Whole-class response and justification","notes":"B. Connectivity and agreement about message meaning are different requirements. Give thinking time and collect reasons, without recording scores. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#networks. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Exit explanation","body":["Define protocol in your own words.","Apply the definition to a new resource."],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual reflection and fresh explanation","notes":"A useful answer names agreed rules and links them to interpreting an actual request or response. Use the response to select the next explanation or practice activity. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#networks. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"}]},{"id":"u1-w11-l02","lesson":2,"title":"The four TCP/IP layers","chapter":"networks","source":"https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#networks","prepare":"Open the networks chapter and its learning resources. Use paper or a local editor as appropriate, with fictional data and disposable practice copies. The suggested 55-minute lesson is flexible.","slides":[{"title":"The four TCP/IP layers","body":["Unit 1 · Week 11 · Lesson 2","Match each layer to a communication responsibility."],"minutes":2,"strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Explain the goal and the expected independent outcome. Match each layer to a communication responsibility. This is ungraded textbook learning. Suggested times can be adjusted to the class. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#networks. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Retrieval and explanation","body":["What job does HTTP perform?","Which other jobs must carry its messages?"],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual retrieval, then partner comparison","notes":"HTTP describes application messages. Transport, IP routing and a local link have separate responsibilities. Allow silent thinking before discussion. Ask a learner to justify an answer before revealing the explanation. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#networks. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Four responsibilities","table":[["DoD layer","Main job","Example"],["Application","Service messages","HTTP, DNS"],["Transport","Endpoint communication","TCP, UDP"],["Internet","IP addressing and routing","IPv4, IPv6"],["Network access","Local frames and link","Ethernet, Wi-Fi"]],"minutes":6,"strategy":"Worked table and teacher explanation","notes":"The course may call the bottom layer Network. It is not the OSI Network layer. Explain the model label before comparing answers. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#networks. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"A TCP web request","body":["Identify the application message.","Locate transport, IP and local-link information.","Read the layer order from sender to receiver."],"minutes":10,"strategy":"Predict, model and explain","notes":"For this explicit TCP example: application data is carried by TCP, IP and a local frame. This is a conceptual layering trace, not a Cisco topology drawing. Pause for predictions before discussing the expected result. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#networks. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Layer explanations","body":["Match routing, a web request and a local frame.","Explain the responsibility behind each match."],"minutes":10,"strategy":"Guided paired practice","notes":"Routing belongs to Internet, web messaging to Application, local frames to Network access. Ask for the responsibility rather than memorised position alone. Swap explaining and checking roles so both learners articulate the reasoning. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#networks. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Independent practice","body":["Make a four-layer table from memory.","Add one job and example per layer.","Compare with the chapter, then correct one gap."],"minutes":12,"strategy":"Independent application with optional support","notes":"Use retrieval, compare, correction and a second closed-book attempt. Keep the model names consistent. Offer the relevant worked example if needed, then use a changed case to check independent understanding. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#networks. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"A choice to explain","body":["Which layer handles routing IP packets?","A: Internet","B: Network access"],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Whole-class response and justification","notes":"A. The bottom network-access layer handles the local link. Acknowledge that OSI uses different layer names. Give thinking time and collect reasons, without recording scores. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#networks. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Exit explanation","body":["Explain why HTTP alone cannot deliver a page.","Name the other layer responsibilities."],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual reflection and fresh explanation","notes":"Look for service, transport, routing and local-link roles rather than a list without explanations. Use the response to select the next explanation or practice activity. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#networks. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"}]},{"id":"u1-w11-l03","lesson":3,"title":"A web request and transport choices","chapter":"networks","source":"https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#networks","prepare":"Open the networks chapter and its learning resources. Use paper or a local editor as appropriate, with fictional data and disposable practice copies. The suggested 55-minute lesson is flexible.","slides":[{"title":"A web request and transport choices","body":["Unit 1 · Week 11 · Lesson 3","Trace a request and explain TCP and UDP guarantees."],"minutes":2,"strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Explain the goal and the expected independent outcome. Trace a request and explain TCP and UDP guarantees. This is ungraded textbook learning. Suggested times can be adjusted to the class. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#networks. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Retrieval and explanation","body":["Which layer handles IP routing?","What does DNS help a client look up?"],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual retrieval, then partner comparison","notes":"Internet handles IP addressing/routing. DNS supplies name-related records, often including an address. It does not carry the later web page content. Allow silent thinking before discussion. Ask a learner to justify an answer before revealing the explanation. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#networks. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Service and transport roles","table":[["Mechanism","Job","Limit"],["DNS","Name-related lookup","Does not serve the web page"],["TCP","Ordered reliable byte stream","No promise of service availability"],["UDP","Datagram transport","No built-in delivery/order guarantee"],["TLS","Connection protection","Endpoints still need protection"]],"minutes":6,"strategy":"Worked table and teacher explanation","notes":"Choose a web request using TCP for the worked trace. Modern HTTP versions can use other transport arrangements, so do not state all HTTPS uses TCP. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#networks. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Encapsulation in this example","body":["Application data enters a TCP segment.","IP carries that segment in a packet.","A local frame carries the packet across a link."],"minutes":10,"strategy":"Predict, model and explain","notes":"Explain encapsulation as adding control information for each job. The receiver processes the information up the stack. Pause for predictions before discussing the expected result. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#networks. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"A transport decision","body":["A transfer needs an ordered stream.","Another app handles late or missing updates itself.","Discuss the transport responsibilities in each case."],"minutes":10,"strategy":"Guided paired practice","notes":"TCP supplies ordered reliable stream behaviour. UDP leaves delivery/order handling to the application where needed. Avoid claiming UDP always wins on speed. Swap explaining and checking roles so both learners articulate the reasoning. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#networks. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Independent practice","body":["Trace a fictional library request through four layers.","Explain where DNS and TLS contribute.","State one guarantee and one limit of TCP."],"minutes":12,"strategy":"Independent application with optional support","notes":"Learners annotate a paper trace. Do not generate live traffic. Require an explicit assumption that this example uses TCP. Offer the relevant worked example if needed, then use a changed case to check independent understanding. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#networks. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"A choice to explain","body":["A TCP connection exists.","A: The application must always be available.","B: The application can still fail or reject a request."],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Whole-class response and justification","notes":"B. Transport guarantees concern the stream, not the whole service or its application permissions. Give thinking time and collect reasons, without recording scores. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#networks. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Exit explanation","body":["Explain segment, packet and frame.","Give one reason a web request can still fail."],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual reflection and fresh explanation","notes":"Each term identifies a layer unit. DNS, application state or permissions can fail independently of a healthy local connection. Use the response to select the next explanation or practice activity. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#networks. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"}]},{"id":"u1-w11-l04","lesson":4,"title":"Network evidence and diagnosis","chapter":"networks","source":"https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#networks","prepare":"Open the networks chapter and its learning resources. Use paper or a local editor as appropriate, with fictional data and disposable practice copies. The suggested 55-minute lesson is flexible.","slides":[{"title":"Network evidence and diagnosis","body":["Unit 1 · Week 11 · Lesson 4","Use test results to choose a sensible next check."],"minutes":2,"strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Explain the goal and the expected independent outcome. Use test results to choose a sensible next check. This is ungraded textbook learning. Suggested times can be adjusted to the class. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#networks. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Retrieval and explanation","body":["Does one successful ping prove a website works?","What part of communication does DNS support?"],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual retrieval, then partner comparison","notes":"Ping tests ICMP echo behaviour at a moment. It does not test web-page service or prove all application functions work. Allow silent thinking before discussion. Ask a learner to justify an answer before revealing the explanation. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#networks. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Observation and next check","table":[["Observation","Useful investigation"],["Name lookup fails","DNS configuration and records"],["No local connection","Link, device and interface state"],["Only one web service fails","Service and access configuration"],["Ping receives no reply","Several causes remain possible"]],"minutes":6,"strategy":"Worked table and teacher explanation","notes":"The table proposes investigations, not confirmed diagnoses. A device or policy may ignore ICMP while other traffic still works. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#networks. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"A name-resolution clue","body":["The name lookup fails.","The known server address replies to ICMP.","Separate the two observations before proposing a cause."],"minutes":10,"strategy":"Predict, model and explain","notes":"Investigate DNS first. An ICMP reply supports reachability for that test but does not show HTTPS is functional. Do not bypass certificate checks by substituting an IP. Pause for predictions before discussing the expected result. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#networks. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Evidence pairs","body":["Write an observation for a slow service.","Suggest two possible causes.","Name a check that distinguishes them."],"minutes":10,"strategy":"Guided paired practice","notes":"Accept congestion versus an overloaded service if the proposed observations separate client/link load from service behaviour. Swap explaining and checking roles so both learners articulate the reasoning. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#networks. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Independent practice","body":["Explain two supplied failure scenarios.","State what is known and what remains uncertain.","Choose the next check and justify it."],"minutes":12,"strategy":"Independent application with optional support","notes":"Use paper scenarios only. Partners challenge any unsupported certainty, then learners write their own final reasoning. Offer the relevant worked example if needed, then use a changed case to check independent understanding. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#networks. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"A choice to explain","body":["A client receives no ping reply.","A: The server is certainly switched off.","B: Link, routing, policy or host causes remain possible."],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Whole-class response and justification","notes":"B. A failed test narrows the question only when its assumptions and limits are considered. Give thinking time and collect reasons, without recording scores. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#networks. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Exit explanation","body":["Correct the claim “ping works, so the website works”.","Give one useful follow-up check."],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual reflection and fresh explanation","notes":"Expected: ICMP and application service are different. A relevant follow-up checks name resolution, service response or access configuration. Use the response to select the next explanation or practice activity. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#networks. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"}]},{"id":"u1-w12-l01","lesson":1,"title":"IP addresses and network interfaces","chapter":"addressing","source":"https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#addressing","prepare":"Open the addressing chapter and its learning resources. Use paper or a local editor as appropriate, with fictional data and disposable practice copies. The suggested 55-minute lesson is flexible.","slides":[{"title":"IP addresses and network interfaces","body":["Unit 1 · Week 12 · Lesson 1","Interpret IPv4 and IPv6 addresses and their roles."],"minutes":2,"strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Explain the goal and the expected independent outcome. Interpret IPv4 and IPv6 addresses and their roles. This is ungraded textbook learning. Suggested times can be adjusted to the class. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#addressing. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Retrieval and explanation","body":["Which device role moves packets between networks?","Which layer uses IP?"],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual retrieval, then partner comparison","notes":"A router performs IP forwarding between networks. IP belongs to the Internet layer in the DoD model. Allow silent thinking before discussion. Ask a learner to justify an answer before revealing the explanation. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#addressing. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Address families","table":[["Feature","IPv4","IPv6"],["Length","32 bits","128 bits"],["Common notation","Four decimal groups","Hexadecimal groups"],["Example context","192.168.40.12","2001:db8::12"],["Identifies","Network interface","Network interface"]],"minutes":6,"strategy":"Worked table and teacher explanation","notes":"An interface can have multiple addresses. Do not describe an IP as a permanent unique label for an entire person or device. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#addressing. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"An interface address","body":["Read 192.168.40.12 as four decimal groups.","Each group ranges from 0 to 255.","The /24 describes the network-prefix length."],"minutes":10,"strategy":"Predict, model and explain","notes":"Keep address and prefix separate. The larger IPv6 space follows from its larger address length, not from hexadecimal notation alone. Pause for predictions before discussing the expected result. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#addressing. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Address claims","body":["Explain why 192.168.40.300 is invalid IPv4 text.","Compare a numeric address with a website name."],"minutes":10,"strategy":"Guided paired practice","notes":"300 exceeds one octet’s range. A website name is not an IP address, though DNS may provide address records for it. Swap explaining and checking roles so both learners articulate the reasoning. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#addressing. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Independent practice","body":["Compare IPv4 and IPv6 without copying the table.","Explain address, interface and prefix.","Give one invalid IPv4 example and its reason."],"minutes":12,"strategy":"Independent application with optional support","notes":"Check 32 versus 128 bits, correct common notation and a reasoned invalid-octet example. Use only synthetic examples. Offer the relevant worked example if needed, then use a changed case to check independent understanding. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#addressing. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"A choice to explain","body":["IPv6 has a larger address space mainly because it uses","A: 128 bits instead of 32.","B: letters instead of decimal digits."],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Whole-class response and justification","notes":"A. Notation represents values. Address length determines the number of available bit patterns. Give thinking time and collect reasons, without recording scores. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#addressing. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Exit explanation","body":["Explain the role of an IP address.","State why its subnet information also matters."],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual reflection and fresh explanation","notes":"It identifies an interface for IP communication. The sender uses its mask/prefix to judge whether a destination is local. Use the response to select the next explanation or practice activity. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#addressing. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"}]},{"id":"u1-w12-l02","lesson":2,"title":"Masks and local destinations","chapter":"addressing","source":"https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#addressing","prepare":"Open the addressing chapter and its learning resources. Use paper or a local editor as appropriate, with fictional data and disposable practice copies. The suggested 55-minute lesson is flexible.","slides":[{"title":"Masks and local destinations","body":["Unit 1 · Week 12 · Lesson 2","Compare network addresses under a /24 mask."],"minutes":2,"strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Explain the goal and the expected independent outcome. Compare network addresses under a /24 mask. This is ungraded textbook learning. Suggested times can be adjusted to the class. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#addressing. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Retrieval and explanation","body":["What does /24 identify?","How many bits are in an IPv4 address?"],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual retrieval, then partner comparison","notes":"The first 24 of 32 bits form the prefix here. The corresponding mask is 255.255.255.0. Allow silent thinking before discussion. Ask a learner to justify an answer before revealing the explanation. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#addressing. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"A host at 192.168.40.12/24","table":[["Destination","Network under /24","Decision"],["192.168.40.80","192.168.40.0","Local"],["192.168.41.80","192.168.41.0","Routing needed"],["192.168.40.200","192.168.40.0","Local"]],"minutes":6,"strategy":"Worked table and teacher explanation","notes":"Apply the sender’s mask to its own address and the destination. Comparing the first three groups is a shortcut specific to /24, not every mask. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#addressing. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"A local-versus-remote comparison","body":["Write the sender’s network: 192.168.40.0.","Find the destination network using the same mask.","Compare the two network addresses."],"minutes":10,"strategy":"Predict, model and explain","notes":"For destination 192.168.41.80, network 192.168.41.0 differs. The ordinary host sends off-subnet traffic towards its configured local gateway. Pause for predictions before discussing the expected result. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#addressing. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Three destinations","body":["Classify 192.168.40.20 and 192.168.41.20. Also check 192.168.40.250.","Write the full address and network each time."],"minutes":10,"strategy":"Guided paired practice","notes":"Under the stated /24: local, remote, local. Do not accept a decision with no mask or assumption. Swap explaining and checking roles so both learners articulate the reasoning. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#addressing. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Independent practice","body":["Create two local and two remote examples.","Use the same sender and /24 mask.","Explain one decision in a complete sentence."],"minutes":12,"strategy":"Independent application with optional support","notes":"Check unique examples and full network values. Extension: explain why a different mask changes the comparison method, without adding unintroduced subnet arithmetic. Offer the relevant worked example if needed, then use a changed case to check independent understanding. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#addressing. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"A choice to explain","body":["Two addresses share their first three groups.","A: They must be local under every mask.","B: The configured mask is still needed."],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Whole-class response and justification","notes":"B. The simple comparison was based on /24. Other prefixes can make a different boundary. Give thinking time and collect reasons, without recording scores. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#addressing. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Exit explanation","body":["Is 192.168.41.12 local to 192.168.40.12/24?","State the two network addresses."],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual reflection and fresh explanation","notes":"No. They are 192.168.41.0 and 192.168.40.0 under the sender’s /24 mask. Use the response to select the next explanation or practice activity. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#addressing. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"}]},{"id":"u1-w12-l03","lesson":3,"title":"Devices and connection media","chapter":"addressing","source":"https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#addressing","prepare":"Open the addressing chapter and its learning resources. Use paper or a local editor as appropriate, with fictional data and disposable practice copies. The suggested 55-minute lesson is flexible.","slides":[{"title":"Devices and connection media","body":["Unit 1 · Week 12 · Lesson 3","Choose equipment by the job it needs to perform."],"minutes":2,"strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Explain the goal and the expected independent outcome. Choose equipment by the job it needs to perform. This is ungraded textbook learning. Suggested times can be adjusted to the class. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#addressing. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Retrieval and explanation","body":["What happens when the destination is off-subnet?","What links wireless clients to the LAN?"],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual retrieval, then partner comparison","notes":"The host uses its local gateway. A wireless access point links wireless clients to the network. A router handles crossing IP networks. Allow silent thinking before discussion. Ask a learner to justify an answer before revealing the explanation. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#addressing. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Device responsibilities","table":[["Device","Role"],["Switch","Forward local frames"],["Router","Forward IP packets between networks"],["Access point","Connect wireless clients"],["Firewall","Apply traffic rules"],["Modem","Adapt signals for provider technology"]],"minutes":6,"strategy":"Worked table and teacher explanation","notes":"One physical home device can combine roles. This course table describes basic roles, not every product configuration. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#addressing. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"A fixed desk and a mobile tablet","body":["A desk needs a reliable nearby wired connection.","A tablet needs mobility around the room.","Explain media and device choices for each."],"minutes":10,"strategy":"Predict, model and explain","notes":"UTP and a switch can fit a nearby fixed desk. Wi-Fi and an access point support mobility. Judge distance, interference and capacity before choosing. Pause for predictions before discussing the expected result. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#addressing. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"A second building","body":["Compare copper, fibre and wireless.","Ask about distance and electromagnetic interference.","State a missing fact before choosing."],"minutes":10,"strategy":"Guided paired practice","notes":"Fibre can suit longer inter-building links and resists electromagnetic interference. Installation and supported distances still matter. Do not invent distances or prices. Swap explaining and checking roles so both learners articulate the reasoning. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#addressing. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Independent practice","body":["Recommend connections for a library and nearby building.","Name each device’s job.","Explain a trade-off and an assumption."],"minutes":12,"strategy":"Independent application with optional support","notes":"Separate the local switch, routing boundary and access point roles. A media recommendation must fit stated distance, mobility and reliability requirements. Offer the relevant worked example if needed, then use a changed case to check independent understanding. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#addressing. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"A choice to explain","body":["A wireless client reaches a wired server on its LAN.","A: An access point provides the wireless link.","B: A modem replaces every local device."],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Whole-class response and justification","notes":"A. A provider-connection modem performs a different role. Give thinking time and collect reasons, without recording scores. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#addressing. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Exit explanation","body":["Distinguish switch, router and access point.","Choose one medium and justify its use."],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual reflection and fresh explanation","notes":"Look for local frames, between-network packets and wireless access, followed by a scenario-specific media justification. Use the response to select the next explanation or practice activity. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#addressing. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"}]},{"id":"u1-w12-l04","lesson":4,"title":"A consistent address plan","chapter":"addressing","source":"https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#addressing","prepare":"Open the addressing chapter and its learning resources. Use paper or a local editor as appropriate, with fictional data and disposable practice copies. The suggested 55-minute lesson is flexible.","slides":[{"title":"A consistent address plan","body":["Unit 1 · Week 12 · Lesson 4","Allocate usable addresses without duplicates."],"minutes":2,"strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Explain the goal and the expected independent outcome. Allocate usable addresses without duplicates. This is ungraded textbook learning. Suggested times can be adjusted to the class. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#addressing. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Retrieval and explanation","body":["Which address is the network for 192.168.40.12/24?","Why must interface allocations be unique?"],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual retrieval, then partner comparison","notes":"The network is 192.168.40.0. Duplicate addresses on the network make delivery and ownership ambiguous. Allow silent thinking before discussion. Ask a learner to justify an answer before revealing the explanation. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#addressing. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Library practice plan","table":[["Role","Address"],["Network","192.168.40.0/24"],["Gateway","192.168.40.1"],["Server","192.168.40.10"],["Desktop range","192.168.40.20–31"],["Broadcast","192.168.40.255"]],"minutes":6,"strategy":"Worked table and teacher explanation","notes":"For this ordinary /24, do not allocate .0 or .255 to hosts. The twelve desktops need twelve distinct addresses. This is a paper plan, not a deployment instruction. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#addressing. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"A gateway mismatch","body":["Client: 192.168.40.12/24","Configured gateway: 192.168.41.1","Explain the mismatch before correcting the plan."],"minutes":10,"strategy":"Predict, model and explain","notes":"The gateway is outside the client’s stated /24 local subnet. In this ordinary directly connected example, choose the intended reachable local gateway .40.1. Pause for predictions before discussing the expected result. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#addressing. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Allocating mobile clients","body":["Keep server and gateway addresses reserved.","Add invented wireless-client allocations.","Check every address against the table."],"minutes":10,"strategy":"Guided paired practice","notes":"Use distinct unused host addresses, such as .40.40 onwards. Note that operational DHCP needs configuration; a paper allocation does not configure it. Swap explaining and checking roles so both learners articulate the reasoning. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#addressing. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Independent practice","body":["Plan addresses for 12 desks, 18 clients and a server.","State the common mask and gateway.","Check uniqueness and special addresses."],"minutes":12,"strategy":"Independent application with optional support","notes":"Accept a different coherent allocation. Count all required interfaces and document reservations. Avoid changing any school device configuration. Offer the relevant worked example if needed, then use a changed case to check independent understanding. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#addressing. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"A choice to explain","body":["The server already uses 192.168.40.10.","A: A desktop can reuse it because names differ.","B: The desktop needs a different host address."],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Whole-class response and justification","notes":"B. Different labels do not resolve a duplicate interface address on the same subnet. Give thinking time and collect reasons, without recording scores. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#addressing. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Exit explanation","body":["Give a valid server and client allocation.","Explain the role of the gateway and mask."],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual reflection and fresh explanation","notes":"Check distinct usable host addresses, local gateway and the /24 network comparison. Use the response to select the next explanation or practice activity. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#addressing. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"}]},{"id":"u1-w13-l01","lesson":1,"title":"LAN, WLAN and WAN","chapter":"network-design","source":"https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-design","prepare":"Open the network-design chapter and its learning resources. Use paper or a local editor as appropriate, with fictional data and disposable practice copies. The suggested 55-minute lesson is flexible.","slides":[{"title":"LAN, WLAN and WAN","body":["Unit 1 · Week 13 · Lesson 1","Distinguish network scale from connection method."],"minutes":2,"strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Explain the goal and the expected independent outcome. Distinguish network scale from connection method. This is ungraded textbook learning. Suggested times can be adjusted to the class. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-design. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Retrieval and explanation","body":["How does a wireless client join a LAN?","What job does a router perform?"],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual retrieval, then partner comparison","notes":"An access point provides wireless access. Routing connects IP networks. Wireless describes a connection method, not necessarily geographic scale. Allow silent thinking before discussion. Ask a learner to justify an answer before revealing the explanation. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-design. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Network descriptions","table":[["Term","Meaning","Example"],["LAN","Local area network","A library site"],["WLAN","Wireless local area network","Library Wi-Fi"],["WAN","Larger-area network links","Connections between distant sites"]],"minutes":6,"strategy":"Worked table and teacher explanation","notes":"A WLAN can be part of a site LAN. Do not make these three exclusive categories for every installation. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-design. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"One site, several connections","body":["Fixed desktops use wired local links.","Visiting devices use a wireless access point.","The site also has a provider connection."],"minutes":10,"strategy":"Predict, model and explain","notes":"Identify local wired and wireless components, then the wider connection. The presence of Wi-Fi does not make the site a WAN. Pause for predictions before discussing the expected result. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-design. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Physical and logical views","body":["Describe where devices sit.","Then describe how they communicate.","Explain what each drawing helps you understand."],"minutes":10,"strategy":"Guided paired practice","notes":"A physical view focuses on locations and links. A logical view focuses on communication organisation and addressing. They answer related but different questions. Swap explaining and checking roles so both learners articulate the reasoning. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-design. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Independent practice","body":["Describe a school site using LAN and WLAN.","Add a justified larger-area connection.","Explain scale and media separately."],"minutes":12,"strategy":"Independent application with optional support","notes":"Use the chapter’s scenario rather than real infrastructure details. Require an example and a reason for each term. Offer the relevant worked example if needed, then use a changed case to check independent understanding. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-design. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"A choice to explain","body":["A room uses wireless laptops.","A: Wireless alone makes it a WAN.","B: It can be a WLAN within the local site network."],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Whole-class response and justification","notes":"B. Geographic reach and the transmission method are different characteristics. Give thinking time and collect reasons, without recording scores. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-design. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Exit explanation","body":["Give a setting that contains both LAN and WLAN.","Explain when WAN is a useful description."],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual reflection and fresh explanation","notes":"Accept local site with wireless access plus wider-area connectivity. Avoid treating every Internet-connected client itself as a WAN. Use the response to select the next explanation or practice activity. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-design. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"}]},{"id":"u1-w13-l02","lesson":2,"title":"A logical network design","chapter":"network-design","source":"https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-design","prepare":"Open the network-design chapter and its learning resources. Use paper or a local editor as appropriate, with fictional data and disposable practice copies. The suggested 55-minute lesson is flexible.","slides":[{"title":"A logical network design","body":["Unit 1 · Week 13 · Lesson 2","Connect device roles, addresses and course symbols."],"minutes":2,"strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Explain the goal and the expected independent outcome. Connect device roles, addresses and course symbols. This is ungraded textbook learning. Suggested times can be adjusted to the class. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-design. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Retrieval and explanation","body":["Which device connects wireless clients?","Which device connects different IP networks?"],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual retrieval, then partner comparison","notes":"Access point and router respectively. Ask learners to label roles before choosing the course symbols. Allow silent thinking before discussion. Ask a learner to justify an answer before revealing the explanation. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-design. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Diagram checklist","table":[["Include","Check"],["Devices","Correct role and course symbol"],["Links","Connected to the intended device"],["Addresses","Unique host allocations and mask"],["Gateway","Router interface on the LAN"],["Boundary","Course firewall convention"]],"minutes":6,"strategy":"Worked table and teacher explanation","notes":"Open the linked SCSA support booklet. Follow its Cisco-style symbol conventions. For the course perimeter diagram, the firewall sits outside the trusted network without an IP label; real deployment addressing varies. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-design. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"A desktop request to a local server","body":["Identify desktop, switch and server.","Trace the local communication path.","Then contrast a remote destination."],"minutes":10,"strategy":"Predict, model and explain","notes":"A basic switch handles local frames. A remote IP destination needs the gateway/router path. The conceptual trace does not replace the required symbol diagram. Pause for predictions before discussing the expected result. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-design. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Diagram peer review","body":["Trace a wireless client’s route to the server.","Check the access point connection.","Check the router interface and mask."],"minutes":10,"strategy":"Guided paired practice","notes":"Use the actual support booklet for symbols. Partners should name one precise correction, not simply say the diagram looks good. Swap explaining and checking roles so both learners articulate the reasoning. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-design. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Independent practice","body":["Draw the fictional library’s logical network.","Use the supplied course symbol reference.","Annotate links, addresses and a traffic trace."],"minutes":12,"strategy":"Independent application with optional support","notes":"No copied real network configuration is needed. Check server placement, wireless access, local switching and external routing. Teacher checks visual conventions. Offer the relevant worked example if needed, then use a changed case to check independent understanding. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-design. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"A choice to explain","body":["A text chain lists the devices in order.","A: It replaces every required course symbol drawing.","B: It helps plan the later symbol drawing."],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Whole-class response and justification","notes":"B. A conceptual chain supports reasoning, while the course diagram still needs the specified symbols and labels. Give thinking time and collect reasons, without recording scores. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-design. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Exit explanation","body":["Explain one complete local path.","Identify one remaining symbol or address correction."],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual reflection and fresh explanation","notes":"Check the explanation agrees with the learner’s drawing rather than a memorised generic chain. Use the response to select the next explanation or practice activity. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-design. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"}]},{"id":"u1-w13-l03","lesson":3,"title":"Access controls in a network design","chapter":"network-design","source":"https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-design","prepare":"Open the network-design chapter and its learning resources. Use paper or a local editor as appropriate, with fictional data and disposable practice copies. The suggested 55-minute lesson is flexible.","slides":[{"title":"Access controls in a network design","body":["Unit 1 · Week 13 · Lesson 3","Match controls to unauthorised-access risks."],"minutes":2,"strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Explain the goal and the expected independent outcome. Match controls to unauthorised-access risks. This is ungraded textbook learning. Suggested times can be adjusted to the class. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-design. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Retrieval and explanation","body":["Should reading resources also permit changing staff files?","What does a firewall rule decide?"],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual retrieval, then partner comparison","notes":"Read access does not imply write permission. A firewall applies configured traffic rules, while application/file permissions control other operations. Allow silent thinking before discussion. Ask a learner to justify an answer before revealing the explanation. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-design. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Threat and control links","table":[["Need or risk","Useful control","Limit"],["Students read resources","Limited individual permissions","Configuration must be checked"],["Guest isolation","Separate network and policy","Separation alone is insufficient"],["Unwanted traffic","Narrow firewall rules","Allowed traffic can still be harmful"],["Software flaws","Managed updates","New flaws may remain"]],"minutes":6,"strategy":"Worked table and teacher explanation","notes":"Introduce least privilege as giving only required access. Distinguish permitted connectivity from permission to edit a resource. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-design. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"A shared administrator login","body":["Several learners use one administrator account.","Explain excessive privilege and weak accountability.","Propose a narrower access arrangement."],"minutes":10,"strategy":"Predict, model and explain","notes":"Separate limited accounts reduce unnecessary power and improve activity attribution. Logs still need integrity and context. Pause for predictions before discussing the expected result. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-design. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"A rule with a purpose","body":["State a required connection.","State an unwanted operation.","Choose controls at the relevant boundaries."],"minutes":10,"strategy":"Guided paired practice","notes":"A web connection rule does not grant export or file-edit permission. Ask learners where each decision is enforced. Swap explaining and checking roles so both learners articulate the reasoning. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-design. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Independent practice","body":["Choose two controls for the library.","Explain the threat each addresses.","Write one allowed and one refused test."],"minutes":12,"strategy":"Independent application with optional support","notes":"Use paper predictions. Keep legitimate student reading available while protecting staff changes. No live rule deployment. Offer the relevant worked example if needed, then use a changed case to check independent understanding. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-design. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"A choice to explain","body":["Guest devices use a separate network.","A: No further access policy is needed.","B: Rules must still enforce the intended boundary."],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Whole-class response and justification","notes":"B. Separation structures communication but its configured forwarding and permissions determine access. Give thinking time and collect reasons, without recording scores. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-design. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Exit explanation","body":["Explain least privilege using the library.","State a limitation of one proposed control."],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual reflection and fresh explanation","notes":"Require a particular role, operation and resource, not merely the word security. Use the response to select the next explanation or practice activity. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-design. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"}]},{"id":"u1-w13-l04","lesson":4,"title":"Bottlenecks and network performance","chapter":"network-design","source":"https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-design","prepare":"Open the network-design chapter and its learning resources. Use paper or a local editor as appropriate, with fictional data and disposable practice copies. The suggested 55-minute lesson is flexible.","slides":[{"title":"Bottlenecks and network performance","body":["Unit 1 · Week 13 · Lesson 4","Use evidence to distinguish capacity, rate and delay."],"minutes":2,"strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Explain the goal and the expected independent outcome. Use evidence to distinguish capacity, rate and delay. This is ungraded textbook learning. Suggested times can be adjusted to the class. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-design. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Retrieval and explanation","body":["What might make Wi-Fi slower at busy times?","Does a faster switch fix every slow download?"],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual retrieval, then partner comparison","notes":"Shared airtime, interference and service/link load are possibilities. A change helps only if it addresses the limiting resource. Allow silent thinking before discussion. Ask a learner to justify an answer before revealing the explanation. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-design. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Three performance measures","table":[["Measure","Meaning","Example question"],["Bandwidth","Capacity","What rate can this link support?"],["Throughput","Achieved transfer rate","What rate do users actually get?"],["Latency","Delay","How long does a response take?"]],"minutes":6,"strategy":"Worked table and teacher explanation","notes":"These measures are related but not interchangeable. Do not treat a latency measurement as a bulk-throughput test. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-design. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"An ideal transfer","body":["Data size: 80 megabits.","Available constant rate: 20 megabits per second.","Calculate time, then state the assumptions."],"minutes":10,"strategy":"Predict, model and explain","notes":"80 / 20 = 4 seconds in the ideal model. Real transfers include overhead, delay and competing demand. A byte has eight bits. Pause for predictions before discussing the expected result. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-design. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"A busy wireless room","body":["Collect client count and signal observations.","Compare wired and wireless experiences.","Explain which result would support your hypothesis."],"minutes":10,"strategy":"Guided paired practice","notes":"A wireless-only slowdown suggests investigating the radio link, but does not prove a single cause. A busy remote service could affect both. Swap explaining and checking roles so both learners articulate the reasoning. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-design. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Independent practice","body":["Calculate 120 megabits at 20 megabits per second.","Investigate a fictional slowdown with two hypotheses.","Justify a targeted improvement."],"minutes":12,"strategy":"Independent application with optional support","notes":"Ideal time is 6 seconds. Check units and whether the proposed evidence distinguishes local wireless, uplink or service limitations. Offer the relevant worked example if needed, then use a changed case to check independent understanding. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-design. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"A choice to explain","body":["An overloaded remote service limits a download.","A: A new local switch must double its speed.","B: The remote limit may remain unchanged."],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Whole-class response and justification","notes":"B. Increasing capacity outside the bottleneck need not improve the end-to-end result. Give thinking time and collect reasons, without recording scores. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-design. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Exit explanation","body":["Distinguish bandwidth, throughput and latency.","Link one improvement to one observed constraint."],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual reflection and fresh explanation","notes":"A valid recommendation connects evidence, cause and effect without promising universal speed gains. Use the response to select the next explanation or practice activity. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-design. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"}]},{"id":"u1-w14-l01","lesson":1,"title":"Traffic paths and network explanations","chapter":"network-review","source":"https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-review","prepare":"Open the network-review chapter and its learning resources. Use paper or a local editor as appropriate, with fictional data and disposable practice copies. The suggested 55-minute lesson is flexible.","slides":[{"title":"Traffic paths and network explanations","body":["Unit 1 · Week 14 · Lesson 1","Explain a path using addressing and device functions."],"minutes":2,"strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Explain the goal and the expected independent outcome. Explain a path using addressing and device functions. This is ungraded textbook learning. Suggested times can be adjusted to the class. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-review. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Retrieval and explanation","body":["Which job belongs to a switch?","When does a client use its gateway?"],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual retrieval, then partner comparison","notes":"A basic switch forwards local frames. The client ordinarily uses the gateway for off-subnet destinations under its mask. Allow silent thinking before discussion. Ask a learner to justify an answer before revealing the explanation. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-review. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Two library destinations","table":[["Client","Destination","Path requirement"],["192.168.40.20/24","192.168.40.10","Local switching"],["192.168.40.20/24","198.51.100.20","Local gateway and routing"],["Wireless local client","192.168.40.10","Access point plus local network"]],"minutes":6,"strategy":"Worked table and teacher explanation","notes":"Use full addresses in the spoken explanation. A remote example such as 198.51.100.20 is fictional documentation data, not a live target. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-review. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"A complete explanation","body":["Need: retrieve a file from a remote network.","Address comparison: destination is not local.","Role: the gateway forwards towards that network."],"minutes":10,"strategy":"Predict, model and explain","notes":"Model the link between the requirement, mask comparison and routing action. A device name alone does not explain the outcome. Pause for predictions before discussing the expected result. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-review. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Local and remote traces","body":["Trace one wired request to the local server.","Trace one wireless request to a remote service.","Compare the roles that differ."],"minutes":10,"strategy":"Guided paired practice","notes":"The wireless trace adds the access point. The remote destination adds the gateway/routing role. Keep the physical drawing consistent with the explanation. Swap explaining and checking roles so both learners articulate the reasoning. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-review. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Independent practice","body":["Write two traffic-path explanations.","State addresses, mask and relevant devices.","Have a partner identify an unsupported step."],"minutes":12,"strategy":"Independent application with optional support","notes":"Use need, device, action and outcome as a writing scaffold. Learners then revise independently. Offer the relevant worked example if needed, then use a changed case to check independent understanding. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-review. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"A choice to explain","body":["Two hosts are on the same stated subnet.","A: Basic local communication can use switching.","B: Every local frame must cross the Internet."],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Whole-class response and justification","notes":"A. Do not confuse the presence of a router with a requirement to route every local exchange. Give thinking time and collect reasons, without recording scores. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-review. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Exit explanation","body":["Explain why one destination is remote.","Name the routing action that follows."],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual reflection and fresh explanation","notes":"Require the actual network comparison and gateway role. Use the response to select the next explanation or practice activity. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-review. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"}]},{"id":"u1-w14-l02","lesson":2,"title":"The library design workshop","chapter":"network-review","source":"https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-review","prepare":"Open the network-review chapter and its learning resources. Use paper or a local editor as appropriate, with fictional data and disposable practice copies. The suggested 55-minute lesson is flexible.","slides":[{"title":"The library design workshop","body":["Unit 1 · Week 14 · Lesson 2","Combine addressing, media and access decisions."],"minutes":2,"strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Explain the goal and the expected independent outcome. Combine addressing, media and access decisions. This is ungraded textbook learning. Suggested times can be adjusted to the class. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-review. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Retrieval and explanation","body":["Which /24 addresses have special roles?","What must remain unique in an address plan?"],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual retrieval, then partner comparison","notes":"For the ordinary practice /24, .0 is network and .255 broadcast. Host interface allocations must be distinct, including the gateway and server. Allow silent thinking before discussion. Ask a learner to justify an answer before revealing the explanation. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-review. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Library requirements","table":[["Requirement","Evidence to produce"],["12 desks and 18 mobile clients","Device and address plan"],["Local server access","Traffic traces"],["Neighbouring building","Justified media choice"],["Protect staff files","Role and permission tests"],["Busy-time slowdown","Hypothesis and measurement plan"]],"minutes":6,"strategy":"Worked table and teacher explanation","notes":"Use the chapter’s unchanged fictional brief. Do not invent a precise building distance or price. State assumptions where the scenario lacks facts. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-review. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"A server allocation","body":["Reserve 192.168.40.1 for the gateway.","Reserve 192.168.40.10 for the server.","Check proposed desktop addresses against both."],"minutes":10,"strategy":"Predict, model and explain","notes":"An allocation such as .40.20–31 gives twelve desktop addresses without reusing .1 or .10. Other coherent plans are acceptable. Pause for predictions before discussing the expected result. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-review. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"A design critique","body":["Check a partner’s wireless connection and gateway.","Trace a local and remote request.","Challenge one untested performance claim."],"minutes":10,"strategy":"Guided paired practice","notes":"Require specific feedback about a link, address, access rule or evidence gap. Teacher checks the course diagram symbols. Swap explaining and checking roles so both learners articulate the reasoning. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-review. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Independent practice","body":["Complete the library design and address table.","Explain two controls and a media choice.","Plan one test of a slowdown hypothesis."],"minutes":12,"strategy":"Independent application with optional support","notes":"Look for complete coverage of the brief. Allow alternate justified designs. This is ungraded practice with public explained answers. Offer the relevant worked example if needed, then use a changed case to check independent understanding. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-review. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"A choice to explain","body":["A proposal gives fast equipment but no staff-file controls.","A: Speed alone meets every requirement.","B: The access requirement still needs evidence."],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Whole-class response and justification","notes":"B. Performance and access protection are separate requirements. Give thinking time and collect reasons, without recording scores. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-review. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Exit explanation","body":["Name one requirement your design supports.","Show the drawing, address or test that supports it."],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual reflection and fresh explanation","notes":"Check concrete evidence against the brief rather than a general statement that the design works. Use the response to select the next explanation or practice activity. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-review. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"}]},{"id":"u1-w14-l03","lesson":3,"title":"Network scenario reasoning","chapter":"network-review","source":"https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-review","prepare":"Open the network-review chapter and its learning resources. Use paper or a local editor as appropriate, with fictional data and disposable practice copies. The suggested 55-minute lesson is flexible.","slides":[{"title":"Network scenario reasoning","body":["Unit 1 · Week 14 · Lesson 3","Support each recommendation with a mechanism and limit."],"minutes":2,"strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Explain the goal and the expected independent outcome. Support each recommendation with a mechanism and limit. This is ungraded textbook learning. Suggested times can be adjusted to the class. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-review. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Retrieval and explanation","body":["What evidence supports an access-control claim?","What does a ping reply establish?"],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual retrieval, then partner comparison","notes":"Use a specific permitted/refused operation test. Ping establishes an ICMP response for that test, not web-service correctness. Allow silent thinking before discussion. Ask a learner to justify an answer before revealing the explanation. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-review. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"An explanation structure","table":[["Part","What to write"],["Need","The required outcome"],["Mechanism","Device, protocol or control action"],["Evidence","Observation or predicted test"],["Limit","What remains uncertain"]],"minutes":6,"strategy":"Worked table and teacher explanation","notes":"Replace the old assessment slot with independent scenario practice. No mark allocation, submission condition or new graded task is introduced. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-review. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"An access recommendation","body":["Need: students read resources without editing staff files.","Control: individual roles and narrow permissions.","Evidence: a read succeeds and an unauthorised write fails."],"minutes":10,"strategy":"Predict, model and explain","notes":"Explain where the rule operates. A firewall alone may not control a permitted application’s file operations. Pause for predictions before discussing the expected result. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-review. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Three short scenarios","body":["Correct a gateway outside its client’s /24 subnet.","Explain an ICMP reply with a failed web service.","Investigate slower wireless transfers at busy times."],"minutes":10,"strategy":"Guided paired practice","notes":"Expected: check intended local gateway; investigate service/name/access factors separately; gather radio and load evidence. Keep alternative explanations visible. Swap explaining and checking roles so both learners articulate the reasoning. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-review. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Independent practice","body":["Answer two unfamiliar network scenarios.","Use need, mechanism, evidence and limit.","Compare with the explained answers and revise."],"minutes":12,"strategy":"Independent application with optional support","notes":"Support with a writing frame, then remove it for a fresh scenario. Accept equivalent technically coherent reasoning. Offer the relevant worked example if needed, then use a changed case to check independent understanding. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-review. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"A choice to explain","body":["A download is slow and the cause is unknown.","A: Buy the fastest router immediately.","B: Gather evidence about the limiting link or service."],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Whole-class response and justification","notes":"B. A diagnosis should justify the location and purpose of a change. Give thinking time and collect reasons, without recording scores. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-review. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Exit explanation","body":["Write a recommendation for a fresh slowdown.","State one observation that could change your mind."],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual reflection and fresh explanation","notes":"Look for a testable hypothesis and a genuine alternative rather than unsupported certainty. Use the response to select the next explanation or practice activity. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-review. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"}]},{"id":"u1-w14-l04","lesson":4,"title":"Network corrections and fresh transfer","chapter":"network-review","source":"https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-review","prepare":"Open the network-review chapter and its learning resources. Use paper or a local editor as appropriate, with fictional data and disposable practice copies. The suggested 55-minute lesson is flexible.","slides":[{"title":"Network corrections and fresh transfer","body":["Unit 1 · Week 14 · Lesson 4","Use feedback to improve an explanation independently."],"minutes":2,"strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Explain the goal and the expected independent outcome. Use feedback to improve an explanation independently. This is ungraded textbook learning. Suggested times can be adjusted to the class. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-review. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Retrieval and explanation","body":["Which network error have you corrected this week?","What evidence showed that it was wrong?"],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual retrieval, then partner comparison","notes":"Let students choose an addressing, device-role, access or performance error. An exact correction is more useful than a confidence rating alone. Allow silent thinking before discussion. Ask a learner to justify an answer before revealing the explanation. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-review. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Common corrections","table":[["Claim","More accurate reasoning"],["Wi-Fi means WAN","Media and geographic scale differ"],["Switch removes broadcasts","Basic switches still forward broadcasts"],["Ping proves web access","ICMP and application tests differ"],["More capacity fixes delay","Find the actual constraint"]],"minutes":6,"strategy":"Worked table and teacher explanation","notes":"Use only errors present in learners’ work. Broadcasts consume resources within a broadcast domain, while routers ordinarily separate those domains. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-review. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"A corrected explanation","body":["Original: a new switch fixes all slow downloads.","Correction: locate the bottleneck first.","Transfer: what if only wireless users are affected?"],"minutes":10,"strategy":"Predict, model and explain","notes":"The transfer suggests a wireless-specific investigation, while other evidence may still change the hypothesis. Pause for predictions before discussing the expected result. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-review. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Explain the correction","body":["Show your original reasoning.","Name the exact mistaken rule.","Try changed data without the worked answer."],"minutes":10,"strategy":"Guided paired practice","notes":"Partners explain the rule rather than copy corrected sentences. Example: change a destination network while keeping the sender mask fixed. Swap explaining and checking roles so both learners articulate the reasoning. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-review. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Independent practice","body":["Complete a fresh address and traffic-path case.","Add an access-control test and a performance limit.","Choose one priority for consolidation."],"minutes":12,"strategy":"Independent application with optional support","notes":"Collect a short independent explanation for teacher planning. It remains an ungraded practice record. Offer the relevant worked example if needed, then use a changed case to check independent understanding. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-review. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"A choice to explain","body":["You copied the explained answer accurately.","A: That alone shows transfer to a new situation.","B: A changed example is needed to check understanding."],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Whole-class response and justification","notes":"B. Accurate copying does not show that the underlying decision rule can be applied independently. Give thinking time and collect reasons, without recording scores. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-review. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"},{"title":"Exit explanation","body":["Explain one corrected rule from memory.","Write a changed example to revisit next week."],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual reflection and fresh explanation","notes":"Check the new example exercises the same concept with different data or conditions. Use the response to select the next explanation or practice activity. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#network-review. Technical/course references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918 https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1090875/Year-11_12_Computer-Science_ATAR_Additional-syllabus-support-booklet-.PDF"}]},{"id":"u1-w15-l01","lesson":1,"title":"Unit 1 retrieval and practice priorities","chapter":"consolidation","source":"https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#consolidation","prepare":"Open the consolidation chapter and its learning resources. Use paper or a local editor as appropriate, with fictional data and disposable practice copies. The suggested 55-minute lesson is flexible.","slides":[{"title":"Unit 1 retrieval and practice priorities","body":["Unit 1 · Week 15 · Lesson 1","Identify a specific skill to practise using evidence."],"minutes":2,"strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Explain the goal and the expected independent outcome. Identify a specific skill to practise using evidence. This is ungraded textbook learning. Suggested times can be adjusted to the class. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#consolidation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"Retrieval and explanation","body":["Trace one comparison and one loop update.","Explain a local versus remote destination."],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual retrieval, then partner comparison","notes":"Use brief mixed prompts. Ask for reasoning before showing answers. The purpose is to choose practice, not rank students. Allow silent thinking before discussion. Ask a learner to justify an answer before revealing the explanation. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#consolidation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"Consolidation stations","table":[["Station","Focus"],["A","Selection and boundaries"],["B","Array traversal and extrema"],["C","Debugging and correction"],["D","Files, project and source use"],["E","Networking and design"]],"minutes":6,"strategy":"Worked table and teacher explanation","notes":"Use the existing consolidation chapter. Choose stations from actual errors rather than attempting every activity superficially. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#consolidation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"A useful priority","body":["Broad label: I am bad at arrays.","Specific priority: I confuse index and value.","Practice: trace a first maximum with a tie."],"minutes":10,"strategy":"Predict, model and explain","notes":"Model a small, observable learning need. An explanation with a correct changed example provides better evidence than a broad self-label. Pause for predictions before discussing the expected result. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#consolidation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"A short mixed check","body":["Try an index question and a boundary question.","Explain your reasoning with a partner.","Compare with the chapter’s explanations."],"minutes":10,"strategy":"Guided paired practice","notes":"Use [8,3,6] index 1 equals 3, and total >= 50 includes 50. Ask students to explain why, then change the values. Swap explaining and checking roles so both learners articulate the reasoning. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#consolidation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"Independent practice","body":["Choose two evidence-based practice priorities.","Select matching chapter stations.","Write an expected outcome for each activity."],"minutes":12,"strategy":"Independent application with optional support","notes":"Support students to separate knowledge gaps, tracing errors and unclear explanations. Keep goals small enough for the remaining lessons. Offer the relevant worked example if needed, then use a changed case to check independent understanding. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#consolidation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"A choice to explain","body":["A test answer is wrong.","A: Practise every topic for the same time.","B: Identify the mistaken step and choose matching practice."],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Whole-class response and justification","notes":"B. Targeted practice responds to the evidence while mixed retrieval still checks other areas. Give thinking time and collect reasons, without recording scores. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#consolidation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"Exit explanation","body":["Name one priority and the evidence for it.","State the activity you will try next."],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual reflection and fresh explanation","notes":"A useful plan connects the particular error, the practice task and an observable corrected result. Use the response to select the next explanation or practice activity. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#consolidation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."}]},{"id":"u1-w15-l02","lesson":2,"title":"Programming traces and repairs","chapter":"consolidation","source":"https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#consolidation","prepare":"Open the consolidation chapter and its learning resources. Use paper or a local editor as appropriate, with fictional data and disposable practice copies. The suggested 55-minute lesson is flexible.","slides":[{"title":"Programming traces and repairs","body":["Unit 1 · Week 15 · Lesson 2","Trace pseudocode and verify a targeted correction."],"minutes":2,"strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Explain the goal and the expected independent outcome. Trace pseudocode and verify a targeted correction. This is ungraded textbook learning. Suggested times can be adjusted to the class. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#consolidation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"Retrieval and explanation","body":["Where does an accumulator start?","How does a first-maximum tie rule work?"],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual retrieval, then partner comparison","notes":"A running sum normally starts at zero before the loop. For first maximum, start from a real element after an empty check and update only for a greater value. Allow silent thinking before discussion. Ask a learner to justify an answer before revealing the explanation. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#consolidation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"Trace a first maximum","table":[["Step for [6, -2, 6]","Best index","Best value"],["Start","0","6"],["Inspect index 1","0","6"],["Inspect index 2","0","6"]],"minutes":6,"strategy":"Worked table and teacher explanation","notes":"Use a strict greater-than comparison. Equality keeps the earlier index. The total is 10, a separate calculation. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#consolidation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"A reset inside the loop","body":["Predict the output for [2, 0, 5].","Find the first loss of an earlier contribution."],"code":"for value in [2, 0, 5]:\n    total = 0\n    total += value\nprint(total)","minutes":10,"strategy":"Predict, model and explain","notes":"The faulty result is 5 because total is reset each pass. Move initialisation before the loop and retest to obtain 7. Pause for predictions before discussing the expected result. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#consolidation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"Pseudocode transfer","body":["Trace load, display and total algorithms.","Translate the loop endpoints into Python range.","Check zero-based indices and stop exclusion."],"minutes":10,"strategy":"Guided paired practice","notes":"Use station B’s supplied pseudocode. Do not silently assume all pseudocode conventions use identical endpoint rules. Swap explaining and checking roles so both learners articulate the reasoning. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#consolidation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"Independent practice","body":["Repair one faulty traversal from station C.","Test empty, one-item, repeated and negative data.","Explain the cause and the retest results."],"minutes":12,"strategy":"Independent application with optional support","notes":"Keep the failed trace and changed example. A correct maximum on positive data alone will not expose an initial-zero bug. Offer the relevant worked example if needed, then use a changed case to check independent understanding. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#consolidation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"A choice to explain","body":["A maximum search starts with value 0 on [-8, -3].","A: It can report a value absent from the list.","B: Zero is always a safe largest-value baseline."],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Whole-class response and justification","notes":"A. The correct maximum is -3. An index initialised to zero is a different idea from a maximum value initialised to zero. Give thinking time and collect reasons, without recording scores. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#consolidation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"Exit explanation","body":["Explain one loop-boundary or initialisation correction.","Give a new input that would expose the old fault."],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual reflection and fresh explanation","notes":"Require a causal explanation and discriminating test, not merely a rewritten line. Use the response to select the next explanation or practice activity. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#consolidation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."}]},{"id":"u1-w15-l03","lesson":3,"title":"Files, source use and network practice","chapter":"consolidation","source":"https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#consolidation","prepare":"Open the consolidation chapter and its learning resources. Use paper or a local editor as appropriate, with fictional data and disposable practice copies. The suggested 55-minute lesson is flexible.","slides":[{"title":"Files, source use and network practice","body":["Unit 1 · Week 15 · Lesson 3","Connect operational requirements to useful evidence."],"minutes":2,"strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Explain the goal and the expected independent outcome. Connect operational requirements to useful evidence. This is ungraded textbook learning. Suggested times can be adjusted to the class. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#consolidation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"Retrieval and explanation","body":["What happens when a file opens in w mode?","Does public source visibility grant reuse rights?"],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual retrieval, then partner comparison","notes":"Write mode truncates existing contents. Visible source alone does not grant a licence. Use the current textbook licence guidance and recorded permissions. Allow silent thinking before discussion. Ask a learner to justify an answer before revealing the explanation. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#consolidation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"Requirement and evidence","table":[["Requirement","Useful check"],["Retain records","Second-run file inspection"],["Handle invalid input","Invalid text followed by valid input"],["Use borrowed material properly","Source and licence record"],["Protect staff files","Read allowed, write refused"],["Route remote traffic","Mask and gateway explanation"]],"minutes":6,"strategy":"Worked table and teacher explanation","notes":"These are independent evidence checks. A screenshot that a screen opened cannot establish all of them. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#consolidation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"The second-run failure","body":["First run saves quantity 2.","Second run saves quantity 5.","Only 5 remains. Explain the likely code path to inspect."],"minutes":10,"strategy":"Predict, model and explain","notes":"Inspect truncating mode and when the file is opened. Correct in a disposable copy and verify two separate records persist. Pause for predictions before discussing the expected result. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#consolidation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"A library explanation","body":["State one access requirement.","Match a control and a positive/negative test.","Identify a useful network diagram correction."],"minutes":10,"strategy":"Guided paired practice","notes":"Use station E and the existing support booklet. Keep the traffic policy and application/file permissions distinct. Swap explaining and checking roles so both learners articulate the reasoning. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#consolidation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"Independent practice","body":["Complete one station D and one station E activity.","Record expected and actual or justified paper results.","Explain one source or evidence limitation."],"minutes":12,"strategy":"Independent application with optional support","notes":"Provide the project workbook and course symbol reference. Written network reasoning does not replace the required drawing practice. Offer the relevant worked example if needed, then use a changed case to check independent understanding. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#consolidation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"A choice to explain","body":["A tutorial is credited but its reuse terms are unclear.","A: Credit alone settles redistribution permission.","B: Check the licence or permission conditions."],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Whole-class response and justification","notes":"B. Attribution and permission answer different questions. Avoid inventing a blanket rule about every copyright exception. Give thinking time and collect reasons, without recording scores. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#consolidation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"Exit explanation","body":["Show one corrected file or network explanation.","State one remaining check before you trust it."],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual reflection and fresh explanation","notes":"Look for a precise test or source check connected to the claimed result. Use the response to select the next explanation or practice activity. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#consolidation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."}]},{"id":"u1-w15-l04","lesson":4,"title":"Fresh transfer and a revision plan","chapter":"consolidation","source":"https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#consolidation","prepare":"Open the consolidation chapter and its learning resources. Use paper or a local editor as appropriate, with fictional data and disposable practice copies. The suggested 55-minute lesson is flexible.","slides":[{"title":"Fresh transfer and a revision plan","body":["Unit 1 · Week 15 · Lesson 4","Apply a corrected idea using changed data."],"minutes":2,"strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Explain the goal and the expected independent outcome. Apply a corrected idea using changed data. This is ungraded textbook learning. Suggested times can be adjusted to the class. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#consolidation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"Retrieval and explanation","body":["What was your priority at the start of this week?","Which independent result now supports improvement?"],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual retrieval, then partner comparison","notes":"Return to the selected error, correction and changed example. Do not use a copied answer as the only evidence. Allow silent thinking before discussion. Ask a learner to justify an answer before revealing the explanation. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#consolidation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"A small revision plan","table":[["Part","Example"],["Priority","First minimum with negative values"],["Method","Trace, then implement"],["Check","Changed values and repeated minimum"],["Later retry","Revisit without the answer"]],"minutes":6,"strategy":"Worked table and teacher explanation","notes":"The dates and priorities belong to the learner. Spaced retry supports checking whether understanding lasts. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#consolidation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"A changed minimum example","body":["Use [-4, 2, -7, -7].","Predict the first minimum’s value and index.","Explain the comparison rule."],"minutes":10,"strategy":"Predict, model and explain","notes":"The first minimum is -7 at index 2 using a strict less-than update. An empty list needs a separate no-minimum rule. Pause for predictions before discussing the expected result. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#consolidation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"A changed network case","body":["Sender: 192.168.40.30/24.","Destination: 192.168.41.10.","Explain the network comparison and next device role."],"minutes":10,"strategy":"Guided paired practice","notes":"Networks .40.0 and .41.0 differ under /24, so ordinary off-subnet traffic uses the local gateway/router. Swap explaining and checking roles so both learners articulate the reasoning. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#consolidation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"Independent practice","body":["Try one fresh programming and one network problem.","Check with the explained answers afterwards.","Plan a later independent retry for one weak point."],"minutes":12,"strategy":"Independent application with optional support","notes":"Use changed context or values. Students keep their corrections; this is not a new graded task. Offer the relevant worked example if needed, then use a changed case to check independent understanding. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#consolidation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"A choice to explain","body":["A changed example still produces the same mistake.","A: Copy the answer and stop.","B: Revisit the decision rule and try another example."],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Whole-class response and justification","notes":"B. Diagnose the repeated error before adding more undirected practice. Give thinking time and collect reasons, without recording scores. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#consolidation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"Exit explanation","body":["Write a priority, practice method and later retry.","Name the result that would show a correction."],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual reflection and fresh explanation","notes":"A clear plan identifies a skill and an observable outcome rather than a general promise to study more. Use the response to select the next explanation or practice activity. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#consolidation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."}]},{"id":"u1-w16-l01","lesson":1,"title":"Written practice and clear reasoning","chapter":"exam-preparation","source":"https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#exam-preparation","prepare":"Open the exam-preparation chapter and its learning resources. Use paper or a local editor as appropriate, with fictional data and disposable practice copies. The suggested 55-minute lesson is flexible.","slides":[{"title":"Written practice and clear reasoning","body":["Unit 1 · Week 16 · Lesson 1","Explain your method on an original revision sample."],"minutes":2,"strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Explain the goal and the expected independent outcome. Explain your method on an original revision sample. This is ungraded textbook learning. Suggested times can be adjusted to the class. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#exam-preparation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"Retrieval and explanation","body":["What do explain and trace ask you to show?","How can you predict an output before running code?"],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual retrieval, then partner comparison","notes":"Explain links a mechanism to an outcome. Trace records changing state in order. Use the supplied task instructions and current course guidance. Allow silent thinking before discussion. Ask a learner to justify an answer before revealing the explanation. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#exam-preparation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"A written response plan","table":[["Prompt","Useful response"],["Predict","Expected output with assumptions"],["Trace","Ordered variable states"],["Explain","Mechanism and consequence"],["Justify","Choice linked to requirements"]],"minutes":6,"strategy":"Worked table and teacher explanation","notes":"Use the original Unit 1 practice paper as optional revision. It is not an official paper or a grade predictor. No marks need recording. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#exam-preparation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"The Q3 maximum trace","body":["Read [6, -2, 6].","Track first-maximum index, value and sum.","State what happens for empty input."],"minutes":10,"strategy":"Predict, model and explain","notes":"Index 0, maximum 6 and total 10. Equality keeps the first maximum. Empty input requires checking before reading element zero. Pause for predictions before discussing the expected result. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#exam-preparation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"Reading a compound question","body":["Underline each requested result.","Separate calculation, trace and explanation.","Predict the result before viewing the answer."],"minutes":10,"strategy":"Guided paired practice","notes":"Q3 requests more than a largest value. Model checking every requested part without implying a marking allocation. Swap explaining and checking roles so both learners articulate the reasoning. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#exam-preparation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"Independent practice","body":["Attempt a chosen section of the original practice paper.","Show reasoning and use the chapter examples if needed.","Keep unanswered steps visible for the next lesson."],"minutes":12,"strategy":"Independent application with optional support","notes":"Use questions-only file first and explained answers afterwards. Timing is optional and should fit class needs. No scoring or submission requirement. Offer the relevant worked example if needed, then use a changed case to check independent understanding. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#exam-preparation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"A choice to explain","body":["A maximum value is correct but its position is missing.","A: Every part of a position question is answered.","B: The requested index still needs explaining."],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Whole-class response and justification","notes":"B. Answer the actual requirement. A value and its location are different outputs. Give thinking time and collect reasons, without recording scores. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#exam-preparation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"Exit explanation","body":["Identify one response you can explain confidently.","Name one step you want to revisit."],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual reflection and fresh explanation","notes":"Use the specific question and missing reasoning step to plan feedback, not a numeric ranking. Use the response to select the next explanation or practice activity. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#exam-preparation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."}]},{"id":"u1-w16-l02","lesson":2,"title":"Explained answers and corrections","chapter":"exam-preparation","source":"https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#exam-preparation","prepare":"Open the exam-preparation chapter and its learning resources. Use paper or a local editor as appropriate, with fictional data and disposable practice copies. The suggested 55-minute lesson is flexible.","slides":[{"title":"Explained answers and corrections","body":["Unit 1 · Week 16 · Lesson 2","Use explanations to identify why a response needs repair."],"minutes":2,"strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Explain the goal and the expected independent outcome. Use explanations to identify why a response needs repair. This is ungraded textbook learning. Suggested times can be adjusted to the class. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#exam-preparation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"Retrieval and explanation","body":["How can a correct result have an unclear explanation?","Why should you preserve your first attempt?"],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual retrieval, then partner comparison","notes":"A first attempt shows the original reasoning. A correction should identify what changed and why, not simply replace it with the answer. Allow silent thinking before discussion. Ask a learner to justify an answer before revealing the explanation. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#exam-preparation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"A correction record","table":[["Record","Purpose"],["Original reasoning","Shows the starting misconception"],["Explained comparison","Locates the mistaken step"],["Corrected rule","States the repair"],["Fresh example","Checks independent transfer"]],"minutes":6,"strategy":"Worked table and teacher explanation","notes":"Use the public explained answers. Equivalent valid reasoning is acceptable. Avoid scores and grade predictions. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#exam-preparation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"The boundary at 50","body":["The rule gives free delivery for totals at least 50.","Compare > 50 with >= 50.","Test 49.99, 50 and 50.01."],"minutes":10,"strategy":"Predict, model and explain","notes":"Correct charges are 5, 0, 0. The strict greater-than version fails only the exact-boundary case among these three. Pause for predictions before discussing the expected result. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#exam-preparation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"A response with missing evidence","body":["Q9 asks for a layer and its role or example.","Compare a name-only response with a fuller explanation."],"minutes":10,"strategy":"Guided paired practice","notes":"A suitable example pairs Application with a web protocol or service role. A label alone may leave part of the prompt unanswered. Swap explaining and checking roles so both learners articulate the reasoning. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#exam-preparation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"Independent practice","body":["Finish the chosen practice questions.","Compare with the explained answers.","Correct two reasoning gaps and try changed examples."],"minutes":12,"strategy":"Independent application with optional support","notes":"Use teacher feedback for course diagram symbols and coherent alternative designs. Self-checking cannot certify every drawing convention. Offer the relevant worked example if needed, then use a changed case to check independent understanding. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#exam-preparation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"A choice to explain","body":["A learner copies the full model answer.","A: A changed example can check understanding.","B: Copying alone proves the skill is secure."],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Whole-class response and justification","notes":"A. Ask the learner to explain the rule in their own words before trying fresh data. Give thinking time and collect reasons, without recording scores. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#exam-preparation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"Exit explanation","body":["Explain one original error and the corrected rule.","Show a fresh example that uses the rule."],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual reflection and fresh explanation","notes":"A good response identifies the cause and demonstrates the repair with changed values or conditions. Use the response to select the next explanation or practice activity. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#exam-preparation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."}]},{"id":"u1-w16-l03","lesson":3,"title":"Targeted practice with fresh examples","chapter":"exam-preparation","source":"https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#exam-preparation","prepare":"Open the exam-preparation chapter and its learning resources. Use paper or a local editor as appropriate, with fictional data and disposable practice copies. The suggested 55-minute lesson is flexible.","slides":[{"title":"Targeted practice with fresh examples","body":["Unit 1 · Week 16 · Lesson 3","Test a repaired idea beyond the original question."],"minutes":2,"strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Explain the goal and the expected independent outcome. Test a repaired idea beyond the original question. This is ungraded textbook learning. Suggested times can be adjusted to the class. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#exam-preparation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"Retrieval and explanation","body":["Which correction needs a fresh test?","What feature should change while the rule stays the same?"],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual retrieval, then partner comparison","notes":"Keep the underlying concept and change data or context. Examples include different negative values, a changed threshold or a new destination subnet. Allow silent thinking before discussion. Ask a learner to justify an answer before revealing the explanation. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#exam-preparation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"Changed-data choices","table":[["Concept","Fresh example"],["First maximum","[-9, -2, -2]"],["Input boundary","Accept 0 through 10 inclusive"],["Append records","A second run with quantity 4"],["Local network","192.168.40.8/24 to 192.168.41.8"]],"minutes":6,"strategy":"Worked table and teacher explanation","notes":"Expected first maximum index 1/value -2; boundary checks -1,0,10,11; previous file records remain; remote subnet needs routing. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#exam-preparation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"A first-maximum transfer","body":["Trace [-9, -2, -2] using a strict > update.","Keep the index separate from the stored value."],"minutes":10,"strategy":"Predict, model and explain","notes":"Best index changes from 0 to 1 and stays 1 at equality. Starting maximum value at zero would fail for this all-negative list. Pause for predictions before discussing the expected result. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#exam-preparation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"The new boundary","body":["The allowed whole-number range is 0 to 10.","Choose tests just outside and at both ends."],"minutes":10,"strategy":"Guided paired practice","notes":"Useful tests are -1,0,1,9,10,11 and invalid text. Distinguish conversion failure from range rejection. Swap explaining and checking roles so both learners articulate the reasoning. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#exam-preparation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"Independent practice","body":["Choose two earlier weak ideas.","Create fresh tests and predict answers.","Complete them independently, then check explanations."],"minutes":12,"strategy":"Independent application with optional support","notes":"A partner can change values after the learner’s first prediction. Keep a concrete error/correction record for any remaining gap. Offer the relevant worked example if needed, then use a changed case to check independent understanding. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#exam-preparation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"A choice to explain","body":["Only positive lists were used to test a maximum search.","A: That proves an initial-zero algorithm is correct.","B: An all-negative case can reveal another fault."],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Whole-class response and justification","notes":"B. Test families should challenge different assumptions, not repeat the same ordinary case. Give thinking time and collect reasons, without recording scores. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#exam-preparation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"Exit explanation","body":["Give one changed case and its result.","Explain why it checks your earlier misconception."],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual reflection and fresh explanation","notes":"The explanation should connect the new data to the original faulty assumption or boundary. Use the response to select the next explanation or practice activity. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#exam-preparation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."}]},{"id":"u1-w16-l04","lesson":4,"title":"Unit 1 review and future practice","chapter":"exam-preparation","source":"https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#exam-preparation","prepare":"Open the exam-preparation chapter and its learning resources. Use paper or a local editor as appropriate, with fictional data and disposable practice copies. The suggested 55-minute lesson is flexible.","slides":[{"title":"Unit 1 review and future practice","body":["Unit 1 · Week 16 · Lesson 4","Explain key ideas and choose a realistic next step."],"minutes":2,"strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Explain the goal and the expected independent outcome. Explain key ideas and choose a realistic next step. This is ungraded textbook learning. Suggested times can be adjusted to the class. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#exam-preparation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"Retrieval and explanation","body":["Explain one function result and one network path.","Name a reliable method for checking your reasoning."],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual retrieval, then partner comparison","notes":"Use short independent explanations. Accept traces, tests and requirement-based comparisons with clear assumptions. Allow silent thinking before discussion. Ask a learner to justify an answer before revealing the explanation. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#exam-preparation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"Evidence across the unit","table":[["Area","A useful demonstration"],["Programming","Trace and test a changed case"],["Files","Retain records across two runs"],["Design","Connect requirement, implementation and test"],["Networks","Explain path, control and limitation"]],"minutes":6,"strategy":"Worked table and teacher explanation","notes":"These examples support learning review. They do not certify complete syllabus coverage or predict a result. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#exam-preparation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"A realistic next action","body":["Priority: distinguish local and remote traffic.","Action: trace two changed /24 destinations.","Later check: explain the gateway decision unaided."],"minutes":10,"strategy":"Predict, model and explain","notes":"A small specific action is easier to follow than revising everything. Use evidence from the earlier practice to choose it. Pause for predictions before discussing the expected result. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#exam-preparation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"Peer explanation","body":["Explain one corrected misconception without notes.","Invite a partner to change an assumption.","Revise the explanation if the change matters."],"minutes":10,"strategy":"Guided paired practice","notes":"Example: change a mask, a tie rule or a file mode. Check the learner notices which conclusion depends on that assumption. Swap explaining and checking roles so both learners articulate the reasoning. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#exam-preparation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"Independent practice","body":["Complete a short mixed retrieval activity.","Write one independent programming explanation.","Write one network explanation and a later practice plan."],"minutes":12,"strategy":"Independent application with optional support","notes":"Use the textbook’s current practice questions. Any official examination instructions or school scheduling must come from the current authorised source, not this slide deck. Offer the relevant worked example if needed, then use a changed case to check independent understanding. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#exam-preparation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"A choice to explain","body":["One successful sample means every skill is mastered.","A: Further changed examples can reveal gaps.","B: No further checking is useful."],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Whole-class response and justification","notes":"A. Sampling gives limited evidence. Practical implementation, diagrams and later retrieval check different aspects. Give thinking time and collect reasons, without recording scores. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#exam-preparation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"Exit explanation","body":["Name one idea you can explain clearly.","Choose one next practice action and when to retry it."],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual reflection and fresh explanation","notes":"End with concrete progress and a realistic plan. Weeks 17–18 remain the planner’s examination block, with no new textbook lessons added here. Use the response to select the next explanation or practice activity. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#exam-preparation. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."}]},{"id":"u1-w02-l01","lesson":1,"title":"Values, types and input","chapter":"values","source":"https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#values","prepare":"Approved Python 3 editor, paper for type sorting and a saved Week 1 badge script.","slides":[{"title":"Values, types and input","strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Explain that learners will choose a type by meaning, convert suitable input and justify a prediction. This is formative practice.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 2 · Lesson 1","Choose a type that fits what a value means."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: the badge program","strategy":"Individual retrieval and pair comparison","notes":"Allow one minute without notes, then compare with a partner. Expected quantity is input, quantity × 3 is processing, print displays output. input returns text and int converts suitable text. If learners think input returns a number, demonstrate type(input(...)) with 4 before continuing.","body":["Without notes, name the badge program’s input.","Which line calculates the cost?","Why did we use int(input(...))?","Compare with a partner, then correct your explanation."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Four useful Python types","strategy":"Type sorting and explanation","notes":"Model the first row, then learners suggest another example for each type. A count is int, a measurement can be float, an identifier stays str and a two-state value is bool. Capitalisation matters in True and False. Avoid presenting float as an exact decimal representation.","table":[["Type","Example","Meaning"],["int","24","Count of badges"],["float","2.5","Length in metres"],["str","\"0042\"","Identifier"],["bool","True","Two-state value"]],"minutes":6},{"title":"The same digits can mean different things","strategy":"Teacher think-aloud and guided prediction","notes":"Think aloud: the first line joins strings and the second adds integers. Expected 123 then 15. Learners predict first, run, then annotate which values change type. input always returns str here. int(\"12.5\") raises ValueError while float(\"12.5\") gives 12.5. Demonstrate the failure in a separate interpreter, explaining that error handling comes later.","body":["Predict the first two outputs before running.","Explain why they differ."],"code":"print(\"12\" + \"3\")\nprint(int(\"12\") + int(\"3\"))\nmeasurement = float(\"12.5\")","minutes":10},{"title":"A type depends on its purpose","strategy":"Think, pair, share","notes":"Two minutes individually choosing types, three comparing, then share justifications. Expected locker label str, student count int, temperature float and consent flag bool. A number-looking identifier does not require arithmetic. Keep a label such as 0042 as a string to preserve its zeros. Support: supply the four type names. Stretch: explain why converting an identifier may lose information.","body":["Choose a type for each value and explain why.","Locker label \"0042\" and student count 28.","Temperature 18.5 and permission True.","Compare with a partner before changing your answers."],"minutes":10},{"title":"A measurement calculator","strategy":"Predict, run, investigate","notes":"Learners predict for length 2.5 and width 4: Area: 10.0, then run using suitable numeric input. Driver runs and navigator explains conversions, then swap for 3 and 2: 6.0. Each learner records predictions and results. Support: identify which lines read, calculate and display. Stretch: explain why int(input(...)) would reject the text 2.5.","body":["Predict for length 2.5 and width 4.","Run, compare and explain one conversion."],"code":"length = float(input(\"Length: \"))\nwidth = float(input(\"Width: \"))\narea = length * width\nprint(f\"Area: {area}\")","minutes":12},{"title":"Which conversion fits?","strategy":"All-student misconception check","notes":"Collect A/B/C responses simultaneously. Correct B preserves 0042. A produces 42 and C produces 42.0. If several choose A, revisit identifier versus quantity and recheck a phone extension with a leading zero. Do not imply bool(\"False\") parses a Boolean word; non-empty strings are truthy, which is beyond today’s input task.","body":["An identifier must keep its leading zeros.","A: int(\"0042\")     B: \"0042\"     C: float(\"0042\")","Choose alone, then justify your answer.","What information could conversion remove?"],"minutes":5},{"title":"Exit ticket: a type and a reason","strategy":"Independent exit ticket","notes":"Expected str for 0075, float for 1.75 metres, int for 6 items and bool for an on/off flag. \"6\" + \"2\" gives \"62\" and int(\"6\") + 2 gives 8. Collect without partner help. Use errors to group a brief type/conversion refresher next lesson.","body":["Choose types for label \"0075\" and length 1.75.","Choose types for a count and an on/off flag.","Predict \"6\" + \"2\" and int(\"6\") + 2.","Give a reason for one choice."],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w02-l02","lesson":2,"title":"Operators and changing values","chapter":"values","source":"https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#values","prepare":"Python editor, paper trace tables and counters or a sketch of objects in groups.","slides":[{"title":"Operators and changing values","strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Link to Week 1 calculations. Success means predict arithmetic, explain precedence, trace an update and interpret quotient/remainder for non-negative quantities.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 2 · Lesson 2","Predict calculations before you run them."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: text or number?","strategy":"Individual retrieval and peer explanation","notes":"Expected \"45\", 9 and float for a fractional measurement. Give one minute individually, then compare explanations. If string concatenation still causes confusion, show the two expressions side by side before introducing additional operators.","body":["Predict \"4\" + \"5\" without running it.","Predict int(\"4\") + 5.","Which type suits a measurement with a fraction?","Explain one answer to a partner."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Arithmetic operators","strategy":"Worked examples","notes":"Model the first four operators briefly, then use counters for 17 objects packed four per group. / returns a float for these operands, so 17 / 4 is 4.25. // floors the quotient, giving 4, and % gives remainder 1. Restrict grouping tasks to non-negative integers and positive group sizes. A zero divisor raises ZeroDivisionError.","table":[["Operator","Example","Result"],["+ and -","7 + 2, 7 - 2","9, 5"],["* and /","3 * 4, 17 / 4","12, 4.25"],["//","17 // 4","4 full groups"],["%","17 % 4","1 remaining"]],"minutes":6},{"title":"Order changes the result","strategy":"Teacher think-aloud and guided practice","notes":"Model multiplication before addition for 2 + 3 * 4 = 14. Parentheses first gives (2 + 3) * 4 = 20. Learners then independently predict 18 - 6 / 3 = 16.0. Multiplication and division share precedence and evaluate left to right here. Check all responses before moving on. Support: underline the first operation.","body":["2 + 3 * 4 gives 14.","(2 + 3) * 4 gives 20.","Predict 18 - 6 / 3 and show your working.","Compare the first operation with a partner."],"minutes":10},{"title":"Assignment updates the stored value","strategy":"Worked trace and faded support","notes":"Model count = 4, then the right side count + 1 uses the old 4 and assigns 5, then multiplying by 2 assigns 10. Learners annotate the value after each line and predict print output 10. Repeat independently from count 7, giving 7, 8, 16. = assigns and == compares. Python assignment is not a mathematical claim that count equals count plus one.","body":["Write the value of count after each assignment.","Try again with a starting value of 7."],"code":"count = 4\ncount = count + 1\ncount = count * 2\nprint(count)","minutes":10},{"title":"Cones packed in boxes","strategy":"Paired coding with role changes","notes":"For 29 cones and six per box, expected full boxes 4 and remaining 5. Learners complete and run the code, then swap driver/navigator roles for tests 0, 6 and 7. Expected (0,0), (1,0), (1,1). Each learner keeps a test record. Support: give the operator meanings. Stretch: verify full_boxes * BOX_SIZE + remaining equals cones.","body":["Replace each __ with the correct operator.","Predict and test for 0, 6 and 7 cones."],"code":"cones = 29\nBOX_SIZE = 6\nfull_boxes = cones __ BOX_SIZE\nremaining = cones __ BOX_SIZE\nprint(full_boxes, remaining)","minutes":12},{"title":"Division gives different information","strategy":"Hinge question and error analysis","notes":"Correct B is 2 full boxes. A gives 2.3333333333333335 and C gives remainder 2. Ask for simultaneous responses, then an explanation. If learners confuse quotient and remainder, draw two groups of six and circle the two leftovers. Recheck with 13 cones: quotient 2, remainder 1.","body":["Fourteen cones go into boxes of six.","Which expression gives the number of full boxes?","A: 14 / 6     B: 14 // 6     C: 14 % 6","Explain what each result tells you."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Exit ticket: predict and justify","strategy":"Independent exit ticket","notes":"Expected 3 + 2 * 5 = 13, (3 + 2) * 5 = 25, 23 // 5 = 4 and 23 % 5 = 3. count = 8 then count = count + 1 gives 9. Collect working rather than output copied from Python. Use errors to revisit precedence or grouping.","body":["Predict 3 + 2 * 5 and (3 + 2) * 5.","Find 23 // 5 and 23 % 5.","Trace count = 8, then count = count + 1.","Explain one answer using the operation’s meaning."],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w02-l03","lesson":3,"title":"Binary, decimal and hexadecimal","chapter":"representation","source":"https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#representation","prepare":"Paper place-value grids, Python editor for checking after hand calculations.","slides":[{"title":"Binary and hexadecimal","strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Explain that the base changes notation while the value stays the same. Success means hand conversions of small non-negative integers and a second-method check.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 2 · Lesson 3","Use place values to explain the same number in different bases."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: decimal place value","strategy":"Think, pair, share","notes":"Expected 247 = 2 × 100 + 4 × 10 + 7 × 1. The left position is ten times the next position to the right. Let learners explain with a place-value grid. This bridge prepares doubling for binary without assuming prior binary knowledge.","body":["Write 247 as hundreds, tens and ones.","Explain what the 4 contributes.","How does a position change as you move left?","Compare your explanation with a partner."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Binary place values","strategy":"Worked example and guided practice","notes":"Binary uses only 0 and 1, with each position twice the value to its right. Model 10110₂ by summing 16 + 4 + 2 = 22 decimal. Read the table from left to right. Then learners use the same headings for 11001₂: 16 + 8 + 1 = 25. Support: circle positions with 1. Ask all learners for the decimal result.","table":[["Place","16","8","4","2","1"],["Digit","1","0","1","1","0"],["Contribution","16","0","4","2","0"]],"minutes":6},{"title":"Decimal 22 written in binary","strategy":"Teacher think-aloud and faded support","notes":"Model choosing 16 from 22, leaving 6, skipping 8, choosing 4 then 2, leaving zero. Result 10110₂. Learners convert decimal 13 independently: 01101₂ or 1101₂. Leading zeros preserve value. Support: provide place headings 16,8,4,2,1. Stretch: convert 31 and explain why adding 1 needs a new position.","body":["Choose place values that add to 22: 16 + 4 + 2.","Write 1 for chosen places and 0 for the others.","22 decimal becomes 10110 in binary.","Now convert decimal 13 and check by adding."],"minutes":10},{"title":"Hexadecimal uses sixteen digits","strategy":"Worked example and paired explanation","notes":"Explain digits 0–9 then A=10 through F=15. For 2F hexadecimal, 2 × 16 + 15 = 47 decimal. Each hex digit maps to four bits: 2 is 0010 and F is 1111, giving 00101111. Ask pairs to explain why F is one digit rather than a variable. A bit is a binary digit, and eight bits form a byte.","table":[["Hex digit","Decimal value","Four binary digits"],["2","2","0010"],["A","10","1010"],["F","15","1111"]],"minutes":10},{"title":"Conversion practice before Python","strategy":"Independent practice and peer checking","notes":"Allow seven minutes individually, three comparing working, then two using Python. Expected 11001₂ = 25; 3C₁₆ = 60 and 00111100₂; decimal 37 = 100101₂ and 25₁₆. Python bin(37), hex(37), int(\"100101\",2), int(\"25\",16) verify. Support: supply place grids and A–F values. Stretch: compare two methods for converting 3C. Do not accept Python output alone as hand-working evidence.","body":["Convert binary 11001 to decimal.","Convert hexadecimal 3C to decimal and eight-bit binary.","Convert decimal 37 to binary and hexadecimal.","Show working, compare, then check with Python."],"minutes":12},{"title":"Leading zeros and number bases","strategy":"All-student misconception check","notes":"Correct B: both equal decimal 5. Leading zeros change written width, not value. A common misconception is treating the digits 101 as decimal one hundred and one. If several choose A, return to place values 4,2,1, then recheck binary 0011 versus 11. Always state the base when notation could be ambiguous.","body":["Compare binary 00101 and binary 101.","A: different values     B: the same value","Choose alone and justify with place values.","How many bits form one byte?"],"minutes":5},{"title":"Exit ticket: one value, three notations","strategy":"Independent exit ticket","notes":"Expected decimal 26 is binary 11010 and hexadecimal 1A. Working: 16 + 8 + 2 and 1 × 16 + 10. A–F are hexadecimal digits for 10–15. Collect individual work. Use errors to decide whether to revisit binary positions or hexadecimal digit values in Lesson 4.","body":["Write decimal 26 in binary and hexadecimal.","Show a calculation that checks each answer.","What does A mean as a hexadecimal digit?","Explain why the value stays the same."],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w02-l04","lesson":4,"title":"Characters, code points and bytes","chapter":"representation","source":"https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#representation","prepare":"Python 3 editor capable of UTF-8, paper matching cards and Week 2 conversion notes.","slides":[{"title":"Characters, code points and bytes","strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Success means distinguish a character, a Unicode code point and encoded bytes. Learners revisit base conversion while investigating text representation. Keep examples to A and é to avoid implying all visible characters use exactly one code point.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 2 · Lesson 4","Explain how a character connects to a number and encoded bytes."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: the number 65","strategy":"Individual retrieval and pair explanation","notes":"Expected decimal 65 is binary 1000001 or 01000001 as eight bits, and hexadecimal 41. Working 64 + 1 and 4 × 16 + 1. Allow silent working before pairs compare. If needed, model the 64 position. Use this same value for the character A next.","body":["Write decimal 65 in binary and hexadecimal.","Show place-value working for one conversion.","Compare with a partner.","What changes when we write a number in another base?"],"minutes":5},{"title":"A character and its code point","strategy":"Worked example and matching","notes":"Explain Unicode assigns A code point 65, written U+0041 in hexadecimal. These are representations of the code-point number. UTF-8 encodes A as the single byte 41 hexadecimal. Learners match the rows to character, number and bytes. A code point identifies a character in this example; some visible characters combine multiple code points.","table":[["Representation","Example for A"],["Character","A"],["Code point","65 decimal = U+0041"],["Code-point number in binary","1000001"],["UTF-8 encoded byte","41 hexadecimal"]],"minutes":6},{"title":"Python can inspect a code point","strategy":"Predict, run, investigate","notes":"Predict 65, 0b1000001, 0x41 and A, then run. ord returns a single character’s code point, chr maps a code point back to a character. bin and hex return strings with notation prefixes. Ask learners to annotate each output as number, notation or character. This code does not show encoded bytes yet.","body":["Predict each line of output before running.","Which line returns the character?"],"code":"point = ord(\"A\")\nprint(point)\nprint(bin(point))\nprint(hex(point))\nprint(chr(point))","minutes":10},{"title":"An encoding determines the bytes","strategy":"Teacher modelling and contrast","notes":"Show A: ord 65, UTF-8 hex 41, byte length 1. Show é: ord 233 (U+00E9), UTF-8 hex c3a9, byte length 2. Run the snippet with A, then change to the single precomposed é character shown. len(encoded) counts bytes. .hex() displays those bytes in hex. Explain that a code-point number is different from the encoded byte sequence. Do not generalise one visible character to one byte.","body":["Predict whether é uses the same byte count as A.","Run and compare the two examples."],"code":"text = \"é\"\nencoded = text.encode(\"utf-8\")\nprint(ord(text))\nprint(encoded.hex())\nprint(len(encoded))","minutes":10},{"title":"A representation record","strategy":"Paired investigation with role changes","notes":"Learners complete A then swap roles for precomposed é. Expected A: code point 65/U+0041, UTF-8 41, one byte. é: 233/U+00E9, UTF-8 c3a9, two bytes. Navigator explains which column comes from ord and which from encode. Each learner records an explanation. Support: use the previous snippet. Stretch: explain why decimal 233 alone does not identify a file’s byte sequence without an encoding.","table":[["Character","Code point","UTF-8 hex","Byte count"],["A","Predict, check","Predict, check","Predict, check"],["é","Predict, check","Predict, check","Predict, check"]],"minutes":12},{"title":"How many bytes does a character use?","strategy":"Hinge question and peer explanation","notes":"Correct B. é has code point 233 but uses two UTF-8 bytes c3 a9. A uses one. Collect simultaneous responses, then ask pairs to explain using the record. If several choose A, point to the code-point and encoded-byte columns and re-run len(encoded). A byte is eight bits, but an encoded character can occupy several bytes.","body":["“Every Unicode character takes one byte in UTF-8.”","A: agree     B: disagree","Choose alone, then use A and é as evidence.","What information does a code point identify?"],"minutes":5},{"title":"Exit ticket: explain the distinction","strategy":"Independent exit ticket and reflection","notes":"Expected A has decimal code point 65 and U+0041, encoded as one UTF-8 byte 41. é uses two UTF-8 bytes. Unicode provides code points and UTF-8 supplies encoding rules. Learners explain in their own words and identify a check that helped. Use remaining base-conversion or encoding confusion for a short retrieval starter before Week 3 selection.","body":["Explain character, code point and encoded bytes.","Give A’s code point in decimal and hexadecimal.","Use é to challenge the one-byte claim.","Which prediction or check changed your understanding?"],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w03-l01","lesson":1,"title":"Comparisons and two-way decisions","chapter":"selection","prepare":"Python editor, paper trace grids and the selection chapter.","slides":[{"title":"Comparisons and two-way decisions","strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Success: predict a Boolean comparison, trace an if/else branch and justify a boundary test. Connect selection to Week 1 sequence. This lesson assumes suitable non-negative numeric input.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 3 · Lesson 1","Use a condition to choose which instructions run."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: assignment and types","strategy":"Individual retrieval and pair comparison","notes":"One minute without notes, then compare. Expected count becomes 5 and input returns str. = assigns while == compares. Ask everyone to identify which symbol tests equality. If confusion remains, show count = 4 followed by count == 4, which evaluates True without updating count.","body":["Trace count = 4, then count = count + 1.","What type does input() return?","What does = do in Python?","Compare your answers before opening your notes."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Comparisons produce Boolean results","strategy":"Worked examples and guided prediction","notes":"Model the first two rows, then learners explain why 50 >= 50 is True while 50 > 50 is False. Cover the result column or use a fresh example: 12 \u003c= 12 is True. = assigns, == compares. True and False have capital initial letters in Python.","table":[["Comparison","Meaning","Example result"],["== and !=","Equal / not equal","5 == 5 is True"],["\u003c and >","Less / greater than","5 > 8 is False"],["\u003c=","Less than or equal","5 \u003c= 5 is True"],[">=","Greater than or equal","50 >= 50 is True"]],"minutes":6},{"title":"A delivery rule with a boundary","strategy":"Think, pair, share","notes":"Read the rule together, then allow two minutes of silent predictions. Expected delivery charges: 35 gives $5, 50 gives $0, 80 gives $0. Invite pairs to explain why the boundary belongs to the free-delivery branch. Assume order total is a valid number of zero or more. This is a teaching example, not a robust payment application.","body":["Orders of $50 or more have free delivery.","Smaller orders have a $5 delivery charge.","Predict the charge for totals 35, 50 and 80.","Explain which comparison matches “$50 or more”."],"minutes":10},{"title":"Two branches, one chosen path","strategy":"Teacher think-aloud and code annotation","notes":"Trace order_total 35: condition False, else assigns 5, final print runs and displays Delivery: $5.00. Repeat for 50: True, if assigns 0, output Delivery: $0.00. Colon introduces a suite and indentation groups its instructions. Ask learners to mark the two branches and circle the final print outside them. Invalid input handling comes later.","body":["Trace once for 35, then for 50.","Which line runs after either branch?"],"code":"order_total = float(input(\"Order total: \"))\nif order_total >= 50:\n    delivery = 0\nelse:\n    delivery = 5\nprint(f\"Delivery: ${delivery:.2f}\")","minutes":10},{"title":"A trace before each run","strategy":"Paired prediction, run and explanation","notes":"Driver enters an input while navigator checks the prior prediction and explains the selected branch. Swap after the first run. Expected 49.99 False/else/$5.00; 50 True/if/$0.00; 50.01 True/if/$0.00. Every learner records their own trace. Support: supply condition and branch headings. Stretch: explain which test detects > instead of >=.","table":[["Order total","Condition","Branch and charge"],["49.99","Predict first","Trace, then run"],["50.00","Predict first","Trace, then run"],["50.01","Predict first","Trace, then run"]],"minutes":12},{"title":"Which condition includes the boundary?","strategy":"All-student hinge question","notes":"Take simultaneous responses. Correct B is >= 50. A excludes exactly 50 and C excludes all larger orders. If several choose A, mark 50 on a number line and retest with exactly 50 before moving on. Ask a learner choosing B to explain the equality part.","body":["Which condition matches “at least 50”?","A: total > 50     B: total >= 50     C: total == 50","Choose alone and show your answer together.","Name an input that exposes each wrong choice."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Exit ticket: a new decision","strategy":"Independent transfer check","notes":"Museum rule: under 16 costs $8, otherwise $12. Expected age 15 gives $8, 16 gives $12, 17 gives $12. Correct condition age \u003c 16 for the cheaper branch. Collect the condition and one trace, without partner help. Revisit equality or branch tracing next lesson if needed.","body":["A museum charges $8 for ages under 16.","Everyone else pays $12.","Write the condition for the cheaper branch.","Predict prices at ages 15, 16 and 17."],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w03-l02","lesson":2,"title":"Combining Boolean conditions","chapter":"selection","prepare":"Python editor, paper response cards and a four-row truth-table frame.","slides":[{"title":"Combining Boolean conditions","strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Success: explain and/or/not, evaluate a combined rule and use parentheses to make grouping explicit. Use supplied Boolean values rather than parsing yes/no input.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 3 · Lesson 2","Explain when a combined condition becomes True."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: the equality boundary","strategy":"Individual retrieval with correction","notes":"Expected False, True and True. Ask for one minute of individual work, then paired explanations. If > and >= remain confused, return to the number-line boundary before combining conditions.","body":["Predict 50 > 50 and 50 >= 50.","Predict 15 \u003c 16.","Explain one answer to a partner.","Correct any disagreement using the comparison’s meaning."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Both, either and the opposite","strategy":"Worked truth table","notes":"Model the first row and ask learners to predict the remaining rows before revealing them or checking with Python. and requires both True, or needs at least one True and includes both, not reverses a Boolean. Here P and Q stand for Boolean values. A common error is interpreting or as exactly one.","table":[["P","Q","P and Q","P or Q"],["True","True","True","True"],["True","False","False","True"],["False","True","False","True"],["False","False","False","False"]],"minutes":6},{"title":"An eligibility rule","strategy":"Teacher modelling and paired variation","notes":"Rule: eligibility requires age at least 16 AND permission. With supplied values age 16 and has_permission False, result is False. Ask learners to predict changing permission to True, then age to 15. Expected True then False. has_permission is already bool. bool(\"False\") is True because the string is non-empty, so do not use it to parse input.","body":["Predict before running.","Change one value, then explain the new result."],"code":"age = 16\nhas_permission = False\neligible = age >= 16 and has_permission\nprint(eligible)","minutes":10},{"title":"Grouping changes which rule you test","strategy":"Guided prediction and peer explanation","notes":"Use the stated values to evaluate both expressions. A is True because and binds more tightly than or. B is False because the parenthesised or is True but not suspended is False. Explain Python Boolean operator precedence: not, then and, then or. Comparisons here evaluate before these Boolean combinations. Ask learners to annotate sub-results. If confusion remains, replace each sub-expression with True/False on paper.","body":["member = True, guest = False, suspended = True","A: member or guest and not suspended","B: (member or guest) and not suspended","Predict each result and explain the grouping."],"minutes":10},{"title":"Testing every Boolean combination","strategy":"Paired investigation with role changes","notes":"Use supplied booleans rather than text input. Learners implement allowed = (member or guest) and not suspended and choose cases before running. Expected True/False/False gives True; False/True/False gives True; True/True/True gives False; False/False/False gives False. These cover key paths but not all eight combinations. Stretch: test all eight and justify coverage. Support: add intermediate (member or guest) results.","body":["Rule: a member or guest may enter if not suspended.","Write the condition with explicit parentheses.","Choose cases for each route to entry and refusal.","Predict, run and compare, then swap roles."],"minutes":12},{"title":"What does or allow?","strategy":"All-student misconception check","notes":"Collect responses before discussion. Correct B: True or True is True. Explain inclusive or allows both. If learners choose A, use the truth-table first row and recheck True or False. Ask them to contrast and, which requires both.","body":["Predict True or True.","A: False     B: True","Show your choice, then explain it.","How would and differ when only one value is True?"],"minutes":5},{"title":"Exit ticket: a combined decision","strategy":"Independent exit ticket","notes":"Rule: both age >= 16 and has_permission, reversed permission with not. Expected age 16/permission False gives False for eligibility, age 15/permission True gives False and age 16/permission True gives True. not False is True. Collect without Python first. Group follow-up support by comparison, conjunction or negation errors.","body":["Rule: age >= 16 and has_permission.","Predict for (16, False), (15, True), (16, True).","Predict not False.","Explain why one unsuccessful case fails."],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w03-l03","lesson":3,"title":"Several choices with if and elif","chapter":"selection","prepare":"Python editor and paper trace tables. Use the chapter’s supplied score-band rule.","slides":[{"title":"Several choices with if and elif","strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Success: implement a three-way decision, trace the first matching branch and explain why order matters. This lesson uses the supplied score-band example from the chapter.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 3 · Lesson 3","Choose the first matching branch in an ordered decision."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: two-way selection","strategy":"Individual retrieval and peer explanation","notes":"Ask learners to sketch if/else structure and mark indentation. Expected one of the two branches runs and a print after the decision runs either way. == compares while = assigns. Revisit structural errors before adding elif.","body":["Sketch an if/else decision from memory.","What determines which branch runs?","Where could you print a result after either branch?","Compare the indentation with a partner."],"minutes":5},{"title":"A supplied score-band rule","strategy":"Requirement annotation","notes":"Assume a suitable whole-number score from 0 to 100. Model 85 as High. Ask learners independently for 65 Pass and 40 Developing. Have them underline the inclusive boundary at 80 and 50. These are example program categories, not official ATAR grade boundaries.","table":[["Score","Band"],["80 to 100","High"],["50 to 79","Pass"],["0 to 49","Developing"]],"minutes":6},{"title":"An ordered decision in Python","strategy":"Teacher think-aloud and guided trace","notes":"For 80 the first comparison is True, so High runs and the remaining branches are skipped. For 65 the first is False, the elif is True and Pass runs. For 40 both comparisons are False and else runs. elif belongs to the same chain. Colon and indentation matter. Learners annotate the checked conditions, not only the output.","body":["Trace scores 80, 65 and 40."],"code":"score = 80\nif score >= 80:\n    band = \"High\"\nelif score >= 50:\n    band = \"Pass\"\nelse:\n    band = \"Developing\"\nprint(band)","minutes":10},{"title":"Why the order matters","strategy":"Hinge question and error analysis","notes":"Take predictions before running. With 85, the first >= 50 comparison is True, so band becomes Pass and the elif is skipped. This is a logic error for the stated rule. Learners reorder the >= 80 check before >= 50 and test 85 again. If several still expect High automatically, trace line by line and cross out skipped branches.","body":["Which band does this code assign?","Explain the fault, then correct the branch order."],"code":"score = 85\nif score >= 50:\n    band = \"Pass\"\nelif score >= 80:\n    band = \"High\"\nelse:\n    band = \"Developing\"","minutes":10},{"title":"Your own implementation and test record","strategy":"Independent coding with optional scaffolds","notes":"Learners implement the correct ordered rule and record predictions before runs. Expected 49 Developing, 50 Pass, 79 Pass, 80 High. Support: provide a skeleton with blank conditions, then ask learners to explain their choices. Stretch: compare an ordered chain with two independent if statements and investigate whether later assignments overwrite a result. Keep the supplied 0–100 valid-input assumption.","body":["Implement the supplied score-band rule.","Predict bands for 49, 50, 79 and 80.","Run each case and retain your expected results.","Explain any correction using a trace."],"minutes":12},{"title":"One chain or separate decisions?","strategy":"Paired prediction and whole-class check","notes":"Trace this deliberate fault at score 85: first if assigns High; second independent if is also True and overwrites band with Pass. Output Pass. Contrast with the correct if/elif chain, which prints High. If learners expect High, ask which assignment runs last. This is about separate if statements, not a claim that all if statements should use elif.","body":["Predict the output before discussing it.","How does a second if differ from elif?"],"code":"score = 85\nif score >= 80:\n    band = \"High\"\nif score >= 50:\n    band = \"Pass\"\nprint(band)","minutes":5},{"title":"Exit ticket: branch and reason","strategy":"Independent exit ticket","notes":"Expected 79 Pass, 80 High and 50 Pass. 80 belongs to High because the >= 80 branch is checked first and includes equality. Use responses to select a short first-match tracing starter for Lesson 4.","body":["State the bands for scores 79, 80 and 50.","Which condition matches first for score 80?","Explain why checking >= 50 first causes a fault.","Save your code and test evidence."],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w03-l04","lesson":4,"title":"Boundary tests and debugging","chapter":"selection","prepare":"Python editor, a clean delivery example and a blank expected/actual test log.","slides":[{"title":"Boundary tests and debugging","strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Success: derive tests from a requirement, identify a boundary fault, correct it and rerun the relevant tests. Expected results must come from the stated rule rather than the faulty code.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 3 · Lesson 4","Use expected results to find and explain a decision fault."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: where do results change?","strategy":"Individual retrieval and pair comparison","notes":"Ask learners to identify the two score boundaries 50 and 80, then the delivery boundary 50. Rehearse below/at/above with valid integer scores: 49,50,51 and 79,80,81. For delivery totals to cents use 49.99,50.00,50.01. Distinguish the input domains rather than assuming every task uses the same step.","body":["Where do the score-band decisions change?","Where does the free-delivery decision change?","Choose a value just below one boundary.","Compare your reasoning with a partner."],"minutes":5},{"title":"A test record keeps expectation separate","strategy":"Worked example and guided completion","notes":"Delivery rule stays $0 for totals >= 50 and $5 otherwise. Model 49.99 with condition False and expected $5.00. Learners complete expected values for 50 and 50.01 before running. Actual and pass/fail remain blank until execution. Expected $0.00 for both. If learners copy actual into expected, revisit the requirement and preserve an independent prediction.","table":[["Input total","Expected charge","Actual / result"],["49.99","$5.00","Record after run"],["50.00","Predict from rule","Record after run"],["50.01","Predict from rule","Record after run"]],"minutes":6},{"title":"A fault that ordinary tests can miss","strategy":"Predict, run, investigate","notes":"Run this deliberately faulty example with 10 and 100: both match the stated rule. Then 50 prints Delivery: $5.00 but expectation is $0.00. The condition > excludes the boundary. Ask learners to record the mismatch before editing. Keep suitable non-negative input; invalid input handling is separate.","body":["Predict from the requirement for 10, 100 and 50.","Run each case and locate the mismatch."],"code":"order_total = float(input(\"Order total: \"))\nif order_total > 50:\n    delivery = 0\nelse:\n    delivery = 5\nprint(f\"Delivery: ${delivery:.2f}\")","minutes":10},{"title":"A correction needs an explanation","strategy":"Guided debugging and peer explanation","notes":"Learners explain that >= includes exactly 50, change the faulty comparison and rerun 49.99,50,50.01,10,100. Expected charges 5,0,0,5,0. Keep the earlier failed result alongside the corrected run. A successful test is evidence for tested cases, not proof for every possible input. Support: trace only the comparison at 50 first.","body":["Use a trace to explain the wrong charge at 50.","Correct the comparison and predict again.","Rerun below, at and above the boundary.","Retest ordinary cases and record the correction."],"minutes":10},{"title":"Transfer: museum ticket prices","strategy":"Independent design and testing","notes":"Rule age \u003c 16 costs $8, otherwise $12; valid non-negative integer ages. Learners choose 15,16,17 plus an ordinary younger/older case before coding. Model solution: age = int(input(\"Age: \")); if age \u003c 16: ticket = 8; else: ticket = 12; print(ticket), with proper indentation on separate lines. Expected 8,12,12. Support: supply an if/else skeleton. Stretch: show a \u003c= fault and identify the detecting input. Partners may question the test evidence after independent work.","body":["Under 16 costs $8. Everyone else pays $12.","Design boundary tests before writing the program.","Implement, run and record expected versus actual.","Ask a partner to challenge one test choice."],"minutes":12},{"title":"What do passing tests show?","strategy":"All-student misconception check","notes":"Correct B: passing 10 and 100 does not establish that equality is handled. The > fault passes those two cases but fails 50. Collect responses before showing the faulty code again. If learners choose A, ask them to supply the missing boundary expectation. No finite small set proves all possible behaviours.","body":["Delivery tests at 10 and 100 both pass.","“This proves the boundary is correct.”","A: agree     B: disagree","Choose, then give a test that strengthens the evidence."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Exit ticket: justify a test set","strategy":"Independent exit ticket and reflection","notes":"For museum ages 15,16,17 expected 8,12,12. Age 16 detects \u003c= 16 used for the cheaper branch, because that fault gives 8 instead of 12. Learners explain how they derived expectations and one correction. Collect evidence to identify comparison, branch-order or test-design support before Week 4 loops.","body":["Give museum tests just below, at and above 16.","Write the expected prices from the rule.","Which test detects age \u003c= 16 as a fault?","Explain one check you will reuse in future programs."],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w04-l01","lesson":1,"title":"Fixed repetition with for and range","chapter":"loops","prepare":"Python editor, paper trace grids and the loops chapter. Use whole-number donations for the example.","slides":[{"title":"Fixed repetition with for and range","strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Success means identify what repeats, list range values and trace a fixed loop. Connect Week 3 selection, which chooses a path, with iteration, which repeats a body. Introduce the three-donation task from the chapter.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 4 · Lesson 1","Repeat a task a known number of times."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: sequence and selection","strategy":"Individual retrieval and pair comparison","notes":"One minute individually, then compare explanations. Expected sequence executes instructions in order, selection chooses a branch, >= includes equality and total = total + 5 uses the old total to calculate a new total. Revisit assignment if learners interpret it as mathematical equality.","body":["What does an if/else decision choose?","Why does >= include a boundary value?","Explain total = total + 5 in words.","Compare your explanation with a partner."],"minutes":5},{"title":"What repeats in the donation task?","strategy":"Teacher modelling and requirement annotation","notes":"Model reading and adding one donation, then ask what should repeat for three donations. Initialise total once before repetition and display once afterwards. The loop body reads and adds. Assume suitable non-negative whole-number amounts. Use three counters or slips to act out passes before code.","body":["The club records three whole-number donations.","Read one amount and add it to a running total.","Repeat those steps for each donation.","Display the final total after all three."],"minutes":6},{"title":"range leaves out the stop value","strategy":"Worked examples and guided prediction","notes":"Model range(3) as 0,1,2 and range(1,4) as 1,2,3. Both produce three values. Ask everyone to predict range(2,6): 2,3,4,5, then check. In these positive-step examples stop is excluded. range(0) produces no values. Python for iterates over these values and assigns each to the loop variable.","table":[["Expression","Values","Passes"],["range(3)","0, 1, 2","3"],["range(1, 4)","1, 2, 3","3"],["range(2, 6)","Predict first","Count the values"],["range(0)","No values","0"]],"minutes":10},{"title":"A running total across three passes","strategy":"Teacher think-aloud and code annotation","notes":"Think aloud through 5,7,3: total starts 0, then becomes 5,12,15. donation_number labels passes 1,2,3. Highlight the two indented lines as the body. final print is outside the loop and displays Total: $15 once. Ask learners to point to initialisation and the repeated update. The range ends repetition after the third value.","body":["Trace donations 5, 7 and 3.","Which lines repeat and which run once?"],"code":"total = 0\nfor donation_number in range(1, 4):\n    donation = int(input(\"Donation: \"))\n    total = total + donation\nprint(f\"Total: ${total}\")","minutes":10},{"title":"Prediction before paired runs","strategy":"Paired programming with role changes","notes":"Driver runs the example while navigator checks the prior prediction and explains a pass, then swap roles for a second set. First set 5,7,3 gives 15; second 0,4,6 gives 10. Every learner records totals after each pass. Support: provide total-before and total-after columns. Stretch: change to four donations, predict a new input set and explain the changed range stop.","body":["Write the total after each input: 5, 7, 3.","Run the program and compare with your trace.","Swap roles and predict for 0, 4, 6.","Explain how the loop knows when to finish."],"minutes":12},{"title":"Which range gives four numbered passes?","strategy":"All-student hinge question","notes":"Correct B: range(1,5) produces 1,2,3,4. A produces three values and C produces five values 0 through 4. Collect simultaneous responses before discussion. If several choose A, list values explicitly and recheck range(1,3). Distinguish number of passes from the final stop argument.","body":["The passes must be numbered 1, 2, 3, 4.","A: range(1, 4)     B: range(1, 5)     C: range(5)","Choose alone, then list the values.","Explain why the two other choices do not fit."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Exit ticket: explain a fixed loop","strategy":"Independent exit ticket","notes":"Expected range(2,5) gives 2,3,4 and three passes. With donations 2,4,6, running totals are 2,6,12. Initialisation stays before the loop and final output after it. Collect individual traces to identify range-stop or accumulation support for Lesson 2.","body":["List the values in range(2, 5).","Trace totals for donations 2, 4 and 6.","Where should total start at zero?","Explain why the final print sits outside the loop."],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w04-l02","lesson":2,"title":"Condition-controlled while loops","chapter":"loops","prepare":"Python editor, paper trace grids and a known school-editor Stop command. Discuss non-termination on paper before running.","slides":[{"title":"Condition-controlled while loops","strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Success means trace a pre-test while loop, identify initialisation and update, and explain how the condition becomes False. Begin with a small bounded counter so learners can compare with for.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 4 · Lesson 2","Repeat while a condition remains True."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: the range boundary","strategy":"Individual retrieval and paired explanation","notes":"Expected range(1,4) produces 1,2,3 and excludes 4. A final print outside the loop runs once. Initialising a total inside the loop discards earlier accumulated values. Ask learners to retrieve before notes, then correct their explanation.","body":["List the values in range(1, 4).","Why does the value 4 stay out?","Which instruction normally prints the final total once?","Compare your answers with a partner."],"minutes":5},{"title":"A condition checked before every pass","strategy":"Teacher modelling and guided prediction","notes":"Trace count=1, condition True, print 1, update to 2. Continue through 3 then update to 4. At 4 the condition is False, so the body stops and Finished prints. Output 1,2,3,Finished on separate lines. Check the condition before entry and after each update. Ask a learner to narrate the last failed check.","body":["Predict the output before running.","What value makes the condition False?"],"code":"count = 1\nwhile count \u003c= 3:\n    print(count)\n    count = count + 1\nprint(\"Finished\")","minutes":6},{"title":"The final check belongs in the trace","strategy":"Worked trace with faded support","notes":"Model the first two rows, then learners complete rows for count 3 and 4. At 3, True prints 3 and updates to 4. At 4, False prints nothing inside the body and performs no update. Support: keep condition column headings. Stretch: explain why there are four condition checks but only three body executions.","table":[["Count at check","count \u003c= 3","Body and update"],["1","True","Print 1, count becomes 2"],["2","True","Print 2, count becomes 3"],["3","Predict first","Complete the trace"],["4","Predict first","Does the body run?"]],"minutes":10},{"title":"A while loop may run zero times","strategy":"Guided variation and peer explanation","notes":"Change the start value to 4. The first condition is False, so only Finished prints. For starts 3 and 1, outputs are 3 then Finished, and 1,2,3 then Finished. Ask learners to write predictions before editing. A pre-test loop can have zero body executions; reaching the while line does not guarantee a pass.","body":["Keep while count \u003c= 3.","Predict when count starts at 4, then at 3.","Run each version and compare.","Explain why reaching a loop can produce no passes."],"minutes":10},{"title":"Zero, one and several passes","strategy":"Paired investigation and role changes","notes":"Pairs run the same bounded loop with starts 4,3,1 after individual predictions. Driver enters the starting value in source, navigator checks the condition trace, then swap roles. Every learner records number of passes: 0,1,3. Support: circle the start and update. Stretch: choose a new upper limit and design zero/one/several-pass tests without adding input validation.","table":[["Starting count","Expected passes","Actual passes"],["4","Predict first","Record after run"],["3","Predict first","Record after run"],["1","Predict first","Record after run"]],"minutes":12},{"title":"What would stop this repetition?","strategy":"Paper debugging and all-student check","notes":"Discuss without executing an unbounded loop. If count starts at 1 and while count \u003c= 3 has no update, printing count never changes the condition. It remains True. Expected repair: count = count + 1 inside the body. Model the school editor Stop control before learners experiment, then run only the corrected bounded version. Recheck termination by tracing count=4.","body":["On paper: count starts at 1.","The body prints count but never changes it.","Will while count \u003c= 3 ever become False?","Write an update that makes this example finish."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Exit ticket: the stopping check","strategy":"Independent exit ticket","notes":"For count=1, while count \u003c 3 prints and increments, output is 1 and 2. Last update makes count 3, then condition False. Start at 3 gives zero passes. Ask for the final failed condition check rather than only the printed sequence. Use errors to revisit \u003c versus \u003c= next lesson.","body":["Start count at 1 and use while count \u003c 3.","Print count, then add 1 on each pass.","State the output and the value at the stopping check.","What happens if count starts at 3?"],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w04-l03","lesson":3,"title":"Counters, totals and loop faults","chapter":"loops","prepare":"Python editor, paper trace grids and a clean three-donation program.","slides":[{"title":"Counters, totals and loop faults","strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Success means distinguish counter from accumulator, trace both and explain a repair. Use the same donation example to reduce new context while learners reason about changing state.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 4 · Lesson 3","Track what changes and explain why a loop gives its result."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: the loop structure","strategy":"Individual retrieval and pair comparison","notes":"Expected initialise before repetition, update changing state inside the body and trace the failed condition for while. A fixed known number of passes often suits for/range. Ask learners to explain how an update changes termination, then compare with notes.","body":["Name something initialised before a loop.","What update can make a while condition become False?","When does a known pass count suit for/range?","Compare one explanation with a partner."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Counting passes and accumulating values","strategy":"Worked example and code annotation","notes":"Model inputs 5,7,3. count changes 0,1,2,3 while total changes 0,5,12,15. count increases by 1 per donation and total increases by the entered amount. final print displays 3 15. The name pass_number receives the values 0, 1 and 2; the separate count records completed inputs. Ask learners to mark both updates.","body":["Which variable counts passes?","Which variable combines the input amounts?"],"code":"count = 0\ntotal = 0\nfor pass_number in range(3):\n    donation = int(input(\"Donation: \"))\n    count = count + 1\n    total = total + donation\nprint(count, total)","minutes":6},{"title":"A trace of two changing values","strategy":"Guided practice with reduced support","notes":"Learners complete independently: after 5 count1 total5; after 7 count2 total12; after 3 count3 total15. Ask them to compare the update rules in pairs. Support: model only the first row and provide columns. Stretch: trace a zero donation and explain why count changes even when total does not.","table":[["Donation","Count after pass","Total after pass"],["5","Complete the trace","Complete the trace"],["7","Complete the trace","Complete the trace"],["3","Complete the trace","Complete the trace"]],"minutes":10},{"title":"A total that forgets earlier values","strategy":"Predict, run, investigate","notes":"This deliberate fault resets total to zero during every pass. With 5,7,3, final total is 3. Learners predict from the intended requirement as 15, then trace the code’s actual 3, preserving the distinction. Repair by moving total = 0 before the loop. Do not change the expected result to match the faulty program.","body":["Predict the final value for 5, 7 and 3.","Explain where earlier donations disappear."],"code":"for pass_number in range(3):\n    total = 0\n    donation = int(input(\"Donation: \"))\n    total = total + donation\nprint(total)","minutes":10},{"title":"A repair supported by tests","strategy":"Independent debugging and peer feedback","notes":"Learners repair reset placement and run input sets 5,7,3 ->15; 0,4,6 ->10; 0,0,0 ->0. Keep before/after traces. Ask why only testing zeros would hide the fault: both correct and faulty versions give 0. Support: show the indentation boundary. Stretch: add a counter and verify 3 even for all-zero inputs. Peer checks should challenge evidence without taking over code.","body":["Move the initialisation to the correct place.","Predict and test 5,7,3 and 0,4,6.","Also test three zeros.","Explain why one passing input set is weak evidence."],"minutes":12},{"title":"Which change affects which value?","strategy":"All-student misconception check","notes":"If donation=0, count still increases by 1 and total stays the same. Correct B. Collect simultaneous responses, then ask learners to point to the two update expressions. If several choose A, trace an initial count0,total0 with one zero donation. Counters track events and accumulators combine values, so they need not be equal.","body":["A donation is 0. What happens after that pass?","A: neither count nor total changes","B: count increases and total stays the same","Choose alone and justify from the update rules."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Exit ticket: a trace and a repair","strategy":"Independent exit ticket","notes":"For donations 2,0,4, final count3,total6. total starts at 0 before the loop, then becomes 2,2,6. Ask learners to explain why zero input still counts as a donation. A reset inside the loop would produce final total4. Use responses to decide whether to provide the score-task skeleton next lesson.","body":["Trace donations 2, 0 and 4.","State final count and total.","Explain why the zero donation still counts as a pass.","Where must total start at zero, and why?"],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w04-l04","lesson":4,"title":"A score total and mean","chapter":"loops","prepare":"Python editor and a blank expected/actual log. Assume four suitable whole-number scores.","slides":[{"title":"A score total and mean","strategy":"Learning intention and transfer","notes":"Success means plan and implement four inputs, accumulate their sum, calculate mean after repetition and justify tests. This is formative transfer practice from the chapter, not an extra summative assessment.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 4 · Lesson 4","Build and test a program that reads four scores."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: what repeats and what runs once?","strategy":"Individual retrieval and pair explanation","notes":"Expected input and total update repeat, total initialisation and final mean/output run once. For four passes range(4) gives 0,1,2,3. Allow individual answers before pairs discuss. Revisit accumulator placement if students put total=0 in the repeated body.","body":["Which donation instructions repeated each pass?","Which instructions ran once?","List the values produced by range(4).","Explain the accumulator’s starting value."],"minutes":5},{"title":"The score task requirements","strategy":"Requirement annotation and guided planning","notes":"Exactly four whole-number scores, suitable input assumed. The program must display sum and arithmetic mean. Learners underline four, total and mean. Use for/range because pass count is known. Choose a non-negative integer score assumption for all class tests and keep invalid text outside the core task.","body":["Read exactly four whole-number scores.","Display their total and arithmetic mean.","Assume suitable non-negative scores.","Choose a loop and explain why it fits."],"minutes":6},{"title":"Expected results before implementation","strategy":"Independent prediction and pair comparison","notes":"Calculate independently before showing answers: 8+10+6+12=36, mean36/4=9.0. For 0,0,0,0 expect total0 mean0.0. For 5,5,5,5 expect20 and5.0. Ask learners for working, not Python output. Support: add the first two scores together before the next two. Stretch: choose inputs with a non-integer mean, such as 1,2,3,5 ->11 and2.75.","table":[["Four scores","Expected total","Expected mean"],["8, 10, 6, 12","Calculate by hand","Total divided by 4"],["0, 0, 0, 0","Calculate by hand","Total divided by 4"],["5, 5, 5, 5","Calculate by hand","Total divided by 4"]],"minutes":10},{"title":"A plan with a clear loop body","strategy":"Teacher modelling with faded support","notes":"Model the algorithm verbally, leaving Python implementation to students. Initialise total once, repeat read/add four times, then divide by four and display both results. In Python range(4) repeats four times. Explain that += is shorthand for assigning old total plus score, if learners use the chapter’s solution. Support: offer a blank loop skeleton. Stretch: explain why / preserves a fractional mean while // would discard its fraction for these non-negative inputs.","body":["Set the running total to zero.","Repeat: read one score and add it, four times.","After the loop, calculate total / 4.","Display the total and mean."],"minutes":10},{"title":"Your implementation and test record","strategy":"Independent coding with optional scaffolds","notes":"Reference solution: total=0; for score_number in range(4): read score with int(input(\"Score: \")) then total=total+score; after the loop mean=total/4; print(total,mean). Use proper newlines and indentation. Learners run all three predicted sets, retaining expectations if actual differs. Support: provide the input line and blank loop boundaries. Stretch: use 1,2,3,5 to test a fractional mean. Ask each learner to explain a chosen line without partner help.","body":["Write the program from your plan.","Run each predicted input set and record actual results.","Trace the first wrong value if a result differs.","Save your code and evidence."],"minutes":12},{"title":"Three scores or four?","strategy":"Hinge question and debugging explanation","notes":"A loop using range(3) reads only three scores. With 8,10,6, it accumulates24, then dividing by4 gives6.0. The intended four-score result is36 and9.0. Correct the range to four passes; dividing by3 alone would still violate the four-input requirement. Collect simultaneous responses before discussion and ask learners to cite the requirement.","body":["A version reads scores with range(3).","It then calculates mean = total / 4.","What requirement does it miss?","Which change fixes the input count?"],"minutes":5},{"title":"Exit ticket: defend your solution","strategy":"Independent exit ticket and reflection","notes":"For 1,2,3,5, expected total11 and mean2.75. Learners identify exactly four passes and why mean runs afterwards. Ask what their tests can and cannot establish. Check understanding of counting, accumulation and integer versus real division before Week 5 functions. Save individual work for formative follow-up.","body":["Predict total and mean for 1, 2, 3 and 5.","Explain how your program reads exactly four scores.","Why calculate the final mean after the loop?","Name a test that helped you find or avoid a fault."],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w05-l01","lesson":1,"title":"Function definitions and calls","chapter":"functions","prepare":"Python editor, the functions chapter and the familiar badge-cost calculation.","slides":[{"title":"Function definitions and calls","strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Success means identify a definition, make a call and trace a returned result. Link to earlier badge programs so learners focus on function structure rather than a new calculation.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 5 · Lesson 1","Give a useful calculation a name and call it when needed."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: a familiar calculation","strategy":"Individual retrieval and pair comparison","notes":"Allow one minute without notes, then compare. Four badges at $3 cost $12. Quantity and price are inputs to the calculation; multiplication produces the result. Distinguish calculation from input prompts and printing. Revisit arithmetic if needed before introducing syntax.","body":["Four badges cost $3 each. What is the total?","Which values does the calculation need?","Which step calculates, and which step displays?","Compare your explanation with a partner."],"minutes":5},{"title":"A function’s agreed task","strategy":"Requirement annotation and teacher modelling","notes":"Explain contract as agreed inputs, result and assumptions. Use non-negative whole-number quantities and whole-dollar prices in these introductory examples. The function returns quantity × unit_price. It does not prompt or print. The caller decides where values come from and how to use the result.","table":[["Part of the contract","Badge example"],["Name","badge_cost"],["Inputs","quantity and unit_price"],["Returned result","quantity × unit_price"],["Responsibility","Calculate the cost"]],"minutes":6},{"title":"A definition waits for a call","strategy":"Teacher think-aloud and guided prediction","notes":"def creates the function, but its body runs when badge_cost(4,3) is called. Parameters receive 4 and 3, return sends 12 back, cost receives 12 and print displays it. Mark the indented body and unindented caller. Ask why a file containing only this definition prints nothing.","body":["Predict what prints and when the body runs.","Identify the definition and the call."],"code":"def badge_cost(quantity, unit_price):\n    return quantity * unit_price\n\ncost = badge_cost(4, 3)\nprint(cost)","minutes":10},{"title":"A trace across the call","strategy":"Worked example and faded support","notes":"Model 4,3 as arguments, local parameter bindings and returned 12 stored in cost. Learners independently trace badge_cost(6,3): result 18. Support: provide stage headings. Stretch: trace badge_cost(0,3) and explain why it needs no special branch.","table":[["Stage","For badge_cost(4, 3)"],["Caller supplies arguments","4 and 3"],["Parameters receive values","quantity = 4, unit_price = 3"],["Function returns","12"],["Caller stores result","cost = 12"]],"minutes":10},{"title":"Several calls, one definition","strategy":"Paired prediction and coding","notes":"Keep one definition and make separate calls (0,3), (4,3), (4,5). Expected 0,12,20. Driver runs, navigator checks prior predictions and explains a call, then swap. Each learner records results. Support: supply one example call. Stretch: use a returned result in a later addition without changing the function.","body":["Keep one badge_cost definition.","Predict calls with (0, 3), (4, 3) and (4, 5).","Run each call and record the returned result.","Swap roles and explain how the same body serves each call."],"minutes":12},{"title":"Which line calls the function?","strategy":"All-student hinge question","notes":"Correct B invokes the function. A defines it and C refers to the function object without calling it. Take simultaneous choices. If several choose A, run a file containing only the definition and ask what triggers its body.","body":["A: def badge_cost(quantity, unit_price):","B: badge_cost(4, 3)","C: badge_cost","Choose the call and explain what triggers execution."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Exit ticket: define, call and predict","strategy":"Independent exit ticket","notes":"badge_cost(5,2) returns 10, stored with cost = badge_cost(5,2). Definition creates the function; a call invokes it. Collect individual calls and explanations. Use errors to group definition-versus-call support next lesson.","body":["Write a call for five badges at $2 each.","Predict the returned result.","Show how the caller could store that result.","Explain how a definition differs from a call."],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w05-l02","lesson":2,"title":"Parameters, arguments and returns","chapter":"functions","prepare":"Python editor, badge_cost definition and a paper call-trace template.","slides":[{"title":"Parameters, arguments and returns","strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Success means map arguments to parameters, use a return value and distinguish returned results from displayed output.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 5 · Lesson 2","Trace values into a function and back to its caller."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: the first call","strategy":"Individual retrieval and peer explanation","notes":"Expected badge_cost(4,3) returns 12. The body runs on a call. A caller can assign its returned result to cost. Allow individual retrieval then pair comparison. Retrace the call if learners think def immediately computes a numeric result.","body":["Predict badge_cost(4, 3).","When does the indented function body run?","How can the caller keep the returned result?","Compare one answer with a partner."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Parameters and arguments","strategy":"Worked mapping and guided practice","notes":"Parameters width,height appear in def area(width,height). Arguments 5,2 appear in area(5,2). Positional binding maps first to first, second to second. Product 10 returns. Learners then map badge_cost(6,4): quantity 6, price 4, result 24. Support: align columns. Keyword and default arguments come later.","table":[["Definition or call","What to identify"],["def area(width, height):","Parameters: width and height"],["area(5, 2)","Arguments: 5 and 2"],["Inside this call","width = 5, height = 2"],["Returned product","10"]],"minutes":6},{"title":"The caller can use the result","strategy":"Teacher think-aloud and guided prediction","notes":"cost is 12, payable 14 and output 14. Trace entry into the function, return to assignment, then caller addition. Packaging is a separate caller calculation. Ask learners to predict badge_cost(2,5) plus 2:12. A result can be stored, displayed or combined.","body":["Track cost, then payable.","Which calculation happens in the caller?"],"code":"def badge_cost(quantity, unit_price):\n    return quantity * unit_price\n\ncost = badge_cost(4, 3)\npayable = cost + 2\nprint(payable)","minutes":10},{"title":"Displaying and returning differ","strategy":"Predict, run, investigate","notes":"This deliberately faulty calculation prints 12 inside the function, then caller prints None. Normal completion without an explicit return value returns None. Learners predict both lines, run and explain. Replace inner print with return quantity * unit_price. Corrected caller prints 12 once. A later numeric calculation needs a numeric returned result.","body":["Predict both lines of output.","What value does the caller store in cost?"],"code":"def badge_cost(quantity, unit_price):\n    print(quantity * unit_price)\n\ncost = badge_cost(4, 3)\nprint(cost)","minutes":10},{"title":"A result the caller can reuse","strategy":"Paired debugging with role changes","notes":"Repair print to return. Test (0,3)->0, (4,3)->12, (4,5)->20. The last result plus caller packaging 2 gives 22. Driver repairs while navigator traces the return, then swap. Input and printing stay outside. Support: annotate return-to-assignment. Stretch: explain how an extra print inside would add output without changing a numeric return.","body":["Repair the function so it returns the product.","Test calls with (0, 3), (4, 3) and (4, 5).","Use the last result in a caller calculation that adds 2.","Swap roles and explain the path back to the caller."],"minutes":12},{"title":"What does the call return?","strategy":"All-student misconception check","notes":"Correct B: normal completion with no explicit return value gives None. Displaying 12 does not return 12. Take responses before discussion. If several choose A, assign a call result and inspect it separately. return ends that call; print displays output.","body":["A function prints 12 and reaches the end.","It has no explicit return statement.","What does the call return? A: 12     B: None","Choose alone and explain the difference."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Exit ticket: map and use the values","strategy":"Independent exit ticket","notes":"Parameters width,height receive arguments 7,2 and product 14 returns. Adding 3 in caller gives 17. Collect a labelled mapping and result. Revisit vocabulary or return/None according to errors.","body":["For def area(width, height) and area(7, 2):","Name the parameters and arguments.","Predict the returned product.","If the caller adds 3, what is its new result?"],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w05-l03","lesson":3,"title":"Local names and program structure","chapter":"functions","prepare":"Python editor and separate caller/function trace columns.","slides":[{"title":"Local names and program structure","strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Success means distinguish local and module-level names, trace simple shadowing and refactor a calculation with explicit inputs and result. Global means module-level in these examples.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 5 · Lesson 3","Keep a function’s local work separate from its caller."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: a reusable result","strategy":"Individual retrieval and paired explanation","notes":"Parameters appear in definition, arguments in call, return sends a value to caller, print displays output. Retrieve individually then compare before a brief correction. This prepares scope without introducing a new calculation.","body":["What are parameters and arguments?","Why return a result that another calculation needs?","What does a call without an explicit return value return?","Compare and correct one explanation."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Where names belong","strategy":"Teacher think-aloud and guided trace","notes":"Output 12 then 2. Global quantity starts 2. Local parameter quantity receives 4 and unit_price 3; local result 12 returns and caller cost becomes 12. This does not rebind global quantity. Ask learners to annotate caller and local columns. Same spelling does not imply the same binding.","body":["Predict both printed values.","Which quantity belongs to the current call?"],"code":"quantity = 2\ndef badge_cost(quantity, unit_price):\n    result = quantity * unit_price\n    return result\ncost = badge_cost(4, 3)\nprint(cost)\nprint(quantity)","minutes":6},{"title":"A scope record for one call","strategy":"Worked example with reduced support","notes":"Model global quantity 2 and local quantity 4, then learners complete local result 12 and caller cost 12. A fresh call badge_cost(6,5) returns 30 while global quantity stays 2. Parameters and assigned names inside this function are local. Module-level code cannot directly use its local result name.","table":[["Name and location","Value for badge_cost(4, 3)"],["quantity at module level","2"],["quantity inside badge_cost","4"],["unit_price inside badge_cost","3"],["result inside badge_cost","Complete the trace"],["cost after return to caller","Complete the trace"]],"minutes":10},{"title":"A local assignment","strategy":"Predict, run, investigate","notes":"Output 9 then 2. x=9 creates a local binding; global x remains 2. The caller receives 9 through return. A function can read global names, but local assignment does not automatically change a global. Global declarations are outside the core task. Avoid saying globals cannot be read inside functions.","body":["Predict before running.","Explain why the two outputs differ."],"code":"x = 2\ndef local_demo():\n    x = 9\n    return x\nprint(local_demo())\nprint(x)","minutes":10},{"title":"Refactoring the badge calculation","strategy":"Independent refactoring and peer explanation","notes":"Write badge_cost(quantity,unit_price) returning product. Keep prompts and receipt output in caller. Test (0,3)->0, (4,3)->12, (4,5)->20 before interactive input. Explicit parameters make dependencies visible rather than relying on changing global prices. Support: provide a definition skeleton. Stretch: explain why a new price argument changes that call’s result. Peer checks compare behaviour before/after.","body":["Move the badge calculation into badge_cost.","Pass quantity and price as arguments.","Keep input prompts and receipt printing in the caller.","Test the calculation first, then the whole program."],"minutes":12},{"title":"Does the global value change?","strategy":"All-student hinge question","notes":"Correct A: module-level x stays 2 in local_demo. B is the returned local value 9. Take responses before discussion. If several choose B, use separate trace columns. Returning 9 differs from assigning a new global x.","body":["In local_demo, a local x is set to 9.","The module-level x started at 2.","After the call, module-level x is A: 2     B: 9","Choose alone and justify using scope."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Exit ticket: trace scope","strategy":"Independent exit ticket","notes":"With global quantity 2 and badge_cost(3,5), result 15 returns, local quantity 3 and global quantity 2. Caller receives the local calculation through return. Collect individual traces and use errors to plan support for the delivery function.","body":["Keep module-level quantity at 2.","Call badge_cost(3, 5) and predict its result.","State the local and module-level quantity values.","How can the caller receive a locally calculated result?"],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w05-l04","lesson":4,"title":"A delivery function with tests","chapter":"functions","prepare":"Python editor and input/expected/actual log. Assume suitable non-negative numeric order totals.","slides":[{"title":"A delivery function with tests","strategy":"Learning intention and transfer","notes":"Success means implement a contract, return the correct charge and test below/at/above 50. Formative transfer uses the chapter’s existing delivery rule.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 5 · Lesson 4","Test a focused function before adding input and output."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: the delivery boundary","strategy":"Individual retrieval and pair comparison","notes":"Totals 49.99,50,50.01 give charges 5,0,0. >=50 includes equality. return supplies a result, print displays output. Retrieve the Week 3 rule individually then compare before designing the function.","body":["Recall free delivery for orders of $50 or more.","Predict charges for 49.99, 50 and 50.01.","Which comparison includes exactly 50?","How does return differ from print?"],"minutes":5},{"title":"A small function contract","strategy":"Requirement annotation","notes":"Name delivery_charge, one parameter order_total, numeric returned charge. Assume suitable non-negative finite numbers. Invalid text/negative totals are outside this introductory contract. Input and print stay outside the function. Learners underline >=50 and identify caller operations.","table":[["Part","Requirement"],["Name","delivery_charge"],["Parameter","order_total"],["Return for total >= 50","0"],["Return for smaller suitable totals","5"],["Input prompts and output","Stay in the caller"]],"minutes":6},{"title":"An early return ends the call","strategy":"Teacher modelling and guided prediction","notes":"For 50, True returns 0 and ends the call, so return 5 does not run. For 49.99, False leads to return 5. print with 50 displays 0. Learners predict 50.01->0 before running. An explicit else returning 5 is also valid. Focus on returned charge rather than formatted receipt text.","body":["Trace totals 50 and 49.99.","Why does a successful call return only one charge?"],"code":"def delivery_charge(order_total):\n    if order_total >= 50:\n        return 0\n    return 5\n\nprint(delivery_charge(50))","minutes":10},{"title":"Expectations come from the contract","strategy":"Independent prediction with guided review","notes":"Before running, calculate 49.99->5,50->0,50.01->0. These distinguish > from >=. Add ordinary 10->5 and 100->0. Support: mark the inclusive boundary on a number line. Stretch: explain why 10 and 100 alone miss equality faults.","table":[["Order total","Expected charge","Actual / result"],["49.99","Predict from rule","Record after run"],["50.00","Predict from rule","Record after run"],["50.01","Predict from rule","Record after run"]],"minutes":10},{"title":"Your implementation and caller","strategy":"Independent coding with optional scaffolds","notes":"Re-create from the contract before consulting the model. Make separate calls for boundary totals and 10,100, recording expected/actual. Then add total = float(input(\"Order total: \")), charge = delivery_charge(total), and print(charge) in caller. Support: header and blank branches. Stretch: store and reuse returned charge in another calculation. No input validation is claimed.","body":["Write delivery_charge from the contract.","Test boundary totals and ordinary totals 10 and 100.","Then add input and display in the caller.","Record any correction and rerun the test set."],"minutes":12},{"title":"Which test detects the equality fault?","strategy":"Hinge question and debugging explanation","notes":"A faulty >50 condition returns 5 at 50 instead of 0. Correct B. 49.99 and 100 still pass. Take simultaneous responses and explain why the repair needs rerunning other tests. If several choose A, compare > and >= at exactly 50 in a trace.","body":["A faulty function uses order_total > 50.","Which input reveals the error?","A: 49.99     B: 50     C: 100","Choose alone, explain and propose the correction."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Exit ticket: defend the function","strategy":"Independent exit ticket and reflection","notes":"For 50 charge 0; for 25 charge 5. Returning a number supports reuse. Strong evidence includes independent predictions below/at/above 50 with observed results. Collect individual code/test explanations to check calls, parameter mapping, return and scope before Week 6 arrays.","body":["State the returned charges for totals 50 and 25.","Explain why input stays outside the function.","Name the test that checks equality at the boundary.","Show how the caller can store and display a result."],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w06-l01","lesson":1,"title":"Lists, indices and traversal","chapter":"arrays","prepare":"Python editor, paper index/value grids and the arrays chapter. Use suitable integer input.","slides":[{"title":"Lists, indices and traversal","strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Success means load a list, identify a value by index and display each index/value pair. Explain that Python lists implement these array-style tasks. Lists can change size and hold mixed types, while some arrays have a fixed size/type. Use consistent integer values in this week’s exercises.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 6 · Lesson 1","Store related values and visit them by position."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: repeated input","strategy":"Individual retrieval and pair explanation","notes":"Allow a minute without notes, then compare. range(3) gives 0,1,2 and three passes. A running total combines inputs but does not retain each original value. Ask why storing all readings might help later. This leads to a list without suggesting lists and fixed-size arrays are identical.","body":["List the values in range(3).","What does a running total retain?","How could keeping every original input help?","Compare your explanation with a partner."],"minutes":5},{"title":"A position differs from its value","strategy":"Worked example and guided prediction","notes":"Model scores=[12,7,19]. The first non-negative index is 0, scores[1] is 7 and len(scores) is 3. Ask for index of value 19:2. The last valid non-negative index is len(scores)-1. Python also supports negative indexing, which is outside this introductory convention. One-dimensional access uses one index.","table":[["Index","0","1","2"],["Value","12","7","19"]],"minutes":6},{"title":"Loading values with append","strategy":"Teacher think-aloud and code annotation","notes":"Trace suitable integer inputs 12,7,19. The empty list grows to [12], then [12,7], then [12,7,19]. append adds at the end. Initialising scores=[] happens once before repetition. int converts input text. Ask learners to mark repeated instructions and predict len(scores) after each pass:1,2,3.","body":["Trace inputs 12, 7 and 19.","Which instruction retains each new value?"],"code":"scores = []\nfor index in range(3):\n    score = int(input(\"Score: \"))\n    scores.append(score)\nprint(scores)","minutes":10},{"title":"Traversal with each index","strategy":"Worked example and faded support","notes":"Expected output pairs 0 12,1 7,2 19. range(len(scores)) produces 0,1,2, so every stored element is visited once. Learners identify which expression is an index and which accesses a value. Support: provide a three-column trace. Stretch: explain why range(len(scores)+1) would attempt an invalid final index.","body":["Predict each index/value pair.","Explain why len(scores) is the stop value."],"code":"scores = [12, 7, 19]\nfor index in range(len(scores)):\n    print(index, scores[index])","minutes":10},{"title":"Your loading and display program","strategy":"Paired programming with role changes","notes":"Pairs load four integers then print each index/value pair. Use 8,3,6,10: indices 0..3 and matching values. Driver types loading, navigator checks a trace, then swap for display. Every learner records predictions. Support: supply an empty list and loop headers. Stretch: replace a source value and explain why its index stays the same. Keep suitable input assumption explicit.","body":["Load four integers using append.","Display every index and its stored value.","Predict first for inputs 8, 3, 6 and 10.","Swap roles and check that all four values appear once."],"minutes":12},{"title":"Where is the last valid index?","strategy":"All-student hinge question","notes":"For [12,7,19], correct B is 2. A is last value 19, C is length 3. scores[3] raises IndexError. Collect simultaneous choices before discussion. If several choose C, label positions on paper and recheck a two-element list. Do not imply all out-of-range Python indices are positive.","body":["scores = [12, 7, 19]","Its last valid non-negative index is:","A: 19     B: 2     C: 3","Choose alone and explain index versus value."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Exit ticket: access and explain","strategy":"Independent exit ticket","notes":"For values=[8,3,6], values[1]=3, length 3 and last valid non-negative index 2. Access uses square brackets; append adds one value. Collect individual answers to identify indexing or length support for accumulator traversal next lesson.","body":["For values = [8, 3, 6], find values[1].","State the length and last non-negative index.","Explain what append does when loading input.","Describe how a traversal visits every value."],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w06-l02","lesson":2,"title":"Totals with list traversal","chapter":"arrays","prepare":"Python editor, paper accumulator traces and suitable integer lists.","slides":[{"title":"Totals with list traversal","strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Success means initialise an accumulator, add every list value once and test empty, negative and ordinary cases. Link to Week 4 running totals, now processing retained values.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 6 · Lesson 2","Accumulate a total by visiting every stored value."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: index or value?","strategy":"Individual retrieval and paired correction","notes":"For values=[4,-2,6], values[1]=-2, len 3 and non-negative indices 0,1,2. Ask learners to retrieve individually then compare. Revisit position versus value before a loop adds values rather than indices.","body":["values = [4, -2, 6]","Find values[1] and len(values).","List its non-negative indices.","Compare your answers with a partner."],"minutes":5},{"title":"A running total through the values","strategy":"Teacher think-aloud and guided prediction","notes":"Trace total 0, then 4, then 2, then 8. for value in values supplies each stored value, not each index. += in the chapter is shorthand for total=total+value; use expanded form here. Final print outside loop runs once. Ask learners to explain why total starts before traversal.","body":["Predict total after each pass.","Which name receives a stored value?"],"code":"values = [4, -2, 6]\ntotal = 0\nfor value in values:\n    total = total + value\nprint(total)","minutes":6},{"title":"The accumulator trace","strategy":"Guided practice with faded support","notes":"Model first row 0+4=4, learners complete total before/after:4 and 2 for -2, then 2 and 8 for 6. Pair explanations should identify negative addition reducing the running total. Support: give one completed row. Stretch: write an indexed traversal yielding the same total and explain why adding index would give a different result.","table":[["Value","Total before","Total after"],["4","0","4"],["-2","Complete","Complete"],["6","Complete","Complete"]],"minutes":10},{"title":"An empty traversal still has a total","strategy":"Guided variation and peer explanation","notes":"With values=[], body runs zero times and initial total 0 prints. With[-8,-3], total becomes-8 then-11. With[5], one pass gives 5. Learners predict before running. This total algorithm safely handles an empty list because it does not read values[0]. Distinguish this from finding an empty list’s maximum next lesson.","body":["Keep the same total algorithm.","Predict for [], [-8, -3] and [5].","Run each case and compare.","Explain why an empty list needs no index access here."],"minutes":10},{"title":"Repairing a reset inside the loop","strategy":"Paired debugging and role changes","notes":"This deliberately faulty code outputs 6 rather than intended 8 for[4,-2,6]. Each pass resets total, discarding previous values. Move total=0 before the loop. Swap roles and test[],[5],[-8,-3],[4,-2,6], expecting 0,5,-11,8. Faulty version with empty list would have no total binding in a fresh run; repair before testing it. Support: mark indentation. Stretch: explain why[0,0] alone hides the reset fault.","body":["Predict this version’s output.","Repair it and test empty, single and negative cases."],"code":"values = [4, -2, 6]\nfor value in values:\n    total = 0\n    total = total + value\nprint(total)","minutes":12},{"title":"Does the loop add values or indices?","strategy":"All-student misconception check","notes":"For values=[10,20,30], summing indices 0+1+2 gives 3. Correct B. The required value total is 60. Collect simultaneous responses; if several choose A, place index and value columns side by side. range(len(values)) supplies indices, so an indexed accumulator must add values[index].","body":["values = [10, 20, 30]","A loop adds index for index in range(len(values)).","Its total is A: 60     B: 3","Choose and explain how to total the stored values."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Exit ticket: totals and evidence","strategy":"Independent exit ticket","notes":"For[3,-1,2], running totals 3,2,4. Empty list total 0. Initialising before loop preserves earlier values. An unequal nonzero list exposes a reset error better than all zeros. Collect a trace and one justified test; use errors for a short accumulator refresher next lesson.","body":["Trace the total for [3, -1, 2].","State the expected total for an empty list.","Explain where initialisation belongs.","Choose a test that exposes a reset inside the loop."],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w06-l03","lesson":3,"title":"Minimum and maximum positions","chapter":"arrays","prepare":"Python editor and index/value/best-index trace grids. Establish non-empty input before inspecting index0.","slides":[{"title":"Minimum and maximum positions","strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Success means retain the position of a best value, explain first-occurrence ties and state empty-list assumptions. Use non-empty integer lists for the main algorithm, then explicitly discuss the empty case.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 6 · Lesson 3","Keep a best value’s index while traversing a list."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: location and value","strategy":"Individual retrieval and pair explanation","notes":"For values=[4,9,9], maximum value 9 appears at indices 1 and 2. Ask learners for both, then why saying 9 does not locate an occurrence. Retrieve zero-based indexing and length before deciding a tie rule.","body":["values = [4, 9, 9]","What is the maximum value?","At which indices does it occur?","Explain why a value alone does not identify its position."],"minutes":5},{"title":"The first maximum index","strategy":"Teacher modelling and guided trace","notes":"This example assumes non-empty values. Start best_index 0, comparing actual values via indexing. At index 1,9>4 updates best_index 1; at index 2,9>9 is False so retain 1. Output 1 9. State first-occurrence tie rule. best_index 0 means a valid position, not a guessed maximum value of zero.","body":["Assume the list is non-empty.","Trace which index the algorithm retains."],"code":"values = [4, 9, 9]\nbest_index = 0\nfor index in range(1, len(values)):\n    if values[index] > values[best_index]:\n        best_index = index\nprint(best_index, values[best_index])","minutes":6},{"title":"A trace of the best position","strategy":"Worked example with reduced support","notes":"Model initial best_index 0/value 4. For index 1 compare 9>4 True and update 1; index 2 compare 9>9 False and retain 1. Learners complete rows independently before pair checking. For a fresh[8,2,7], best_index stays 0. Support: write both compared values. Stretch: explain why a one-element list makes no loop-body passes but still has maximum index 0.","table":[["Current index","Comparison","Best index after"],["Start","Use first stored value","0"],["1","9 > 4","Complete"],["2","9 > 9","Complete"]],"minutes":10},{"title":"Negative values and ties","strategy":"Guided variation and all-student checks","notes":"For[-8,-3], first maximum index 1/value-3. A guessed maximum value 0 is wrong because 0 is not present; best_index 0 correctly refers to real value-8. For[4,9,9], > retains first index 1 while >= updates to last index 2. Ask learners to commit predictions then explain the equality check. Support: label index/value separately.","body":["Predict the first maximum position in [-8, -3].","Why could a guessed maximum value of 0 fail?","For [4, 9, 9], compare using > and using >=.","Which comparison keeps the first occurrence?"],"minutes":10},{"title":"Adapting the search for a minimum","strategy":"Independent coding with optional scaffolds","notes":"Change > to \u003c and keep non-empty assumption. For[-2,4,-5,-5], first minimum index 2/value-5. For[7], index 0/value 7. Learners code and trace independently then compare evidence in pairs. Strict \u003c preserves first equal minimum, \u003c= gives last. Support: supply header and index loop. Stretch: implement max_index as a Week 5 function returning an index for non-empty input.","body":["Adapt the algorithm to find the first minimum.","Predict for [-2, 4, -5, -5] and [7].","Implement and compare expected with actual.","Explain your tie rule."],"minutes":12},{"title":"What about an empty list?","strategy":"Hinge question and error analysis","notes":"Correct B: no maximum position exists. Accessing values[0] on[] raises IndexError. Check len(values)==0 first, reporting no maximum, and use the algorithm only in the non-empty branch. If several choose A, ask what element index 0 would refer to. A total of 0 for an empty list does not imply maximum index 0.","body":["values = []","What is its maximum position?","A: 0     B: no maximum position exists","Choose alone. What must happen before reading values[0]?"],"minutes":5},{"title":"Exit ticket: index, value and tie rule","strategy":"Independent exit ticket","notes":"For[-4,-1,-1], first maximum index 1/value-1; >= would retain 2. First minimum is index 0/value-4. Empty list needs a guard before accessing index 0. Collect individual explanations to identify tie-rule, sign or index confusion before the temperature task.","body":["Find the first maximum in [-4, -1, -1].","Give both its index and value.","Which comparison keeps the first maximum?","State the empty-list check needed before index 0."],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w06-l04","lesson":4,"title":"The temperature list task","chapter":"arrays","prepare":"Python editor and blank test log. Load exactly four suitable integer temperatures per run.","slides":[{"title":"The temperature list task","strategy":"Learning intention and transfer","notes":"Success means load four temperatures, display indices/values, find first minimum without min and defend test coverage. This is formative chapter transfer practice. Exact four-input contract guarantees non-empty data before the minimum search.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 6 · Lesson 4","Load temperatures and find the first minimum position."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: a first occurrence","strategy":"Individual retrieval and peer explanation","notes":"For[-2,4,-5,-5], minimum value-5 at indices 2 and 3, first index 2. Strict \u003c keeps the earlier equal value. Ask learners to retrieve individually then compare. Revisit position versus value if they answer only-5.","body":["For [-2, 4, -5, -5], identify the minimum.","Give its first index and the other equal index.","Which comparison preserves the first minimum?","Compare your explanation with a partner."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Loading and displaying four readings","strategy":"Teacher modelling and guided prediction","notes":"Trace input-2,4,-5,-5 into the list. The four accepted integer inputs guarantee length 4 and indices 0..3. Display is a separate traversal after loading. print pairs 0 -2,1 4,2 -5,3 -5. Input validation is outside this core task. Ask learners to mark the two distinct loops.","body":["Predict the four index/value pairs.","Why do loading and display use separate traversals?"],"code":"temperatures = []\nfor index in range(4):\n    temperatures.append(int(input(\"Temperature: \")))\nfor index in range(len(temperatures)):\n    print(index, temperatures[index])","minutes":6},{"title":"Tests before the minimum search","strategy":"Independent prediction and pair comparison","notes":"Expected first minimum indices/values: [-2,4,-5,-5] ->2/-5; [6,3,8,9] ->1/3; [2,2,2,2] ->0/2. Learners predict from requirements before running. Last tied minimum would be 3 in first case, so this catches \u003c=. Support: label indices on paper. Stretch: choose a four-value case whose minimum occurs last, e.g.[3,2,1,0] ->3/0.","table":[["Four temperatures","First minimum index","Minimum value"],["-2, 4, -5, -5","Predict first","Predict first"],["6, 3, 8, 9","Predict first","Predict first"],["2, 2, 2, 2","Predict first","Predict first"]],"minutes":10},{"title":"Finding the first minimum","strategy":"Teacher modelling with faded support","notes":"This fragment follows the four-input loading code. Start lowest_index 0 at a real element. Strict \u003c changes only for a smaller temperature. For main test, trace best indices 0,0,2,2. Final output 2 -5. If reusing this algorithm for a variable-length list, check empty input before index 0. Support: retain index/value trace columns.","body":["Use this after loading four temperatures.","Trace the repeated minimum before running."],"code":"lowest_index = 0\nfor index in range(1, len(temperatures)):\n    if temperatures[index] \u003c temperatures[lowest_index]:\n        lowest_index = index\nprint(lowest_index, temperatures[lowest_index])","minutes":10},{"title":"Your program and test record","strategy":"Independent implementation and peer feedback","notes":"Learners combine loading, display and minimum search without using min. Run all predicted four-reading cases and preserve expected results. Partner challenges a tie-rule or index explanation after individual coding. Support: provide a blank loop skeleton. Stretch: separately test a reusable search with a one-element list and an empty-list guard, distinguishing those from the exactly-four-input interface.","body":["Combine loading, display and first-minimum search.","Run each predicted test set without using min.","Record any fault, correction and rerun.","Ask a partner to challenge one index or tie-rule decision."],"minutes":12},{"title":"Index 2 or the third reading?","strategy":"All-student hinge question","notes":"Correct B: index 2 is the third reading for Python zero-based indexing. In[-2,4,-5,-5], first minimum is index 2/value-5. Displaying human position lowest_index+1 is fine, but keep zero-based index internally for access. If several choose A, label indices 0..3 above item numbers 1..4.","body":["The first minimum has Python index 2.","It is A: the second reading     B: the third reading","Choose alone and explain.","How could you display a human-friendly reading number?"],"minutes":5},{"title":"Exit ticket: defend the evidence","strategy":"Independent exit ticket and reflection","notes":"For[3,1,1,4], first minimum index 1/value 1. Strict \u003c keeps it, while \u003c= returns 2. Equal values test the tie rule and negative values expose an unsafe guessed best value 0. Fixed four inputs ensure non-empty data; reusable search needs a guard. Collect an individual explanation before Week 7 files and reliable input.","body":["For [3, 1, 1, 4], give first minimum index and value.","Explain which comparison your program uses.","Name a test that checks repeated equal values.","Why is the four-input list safe to inspect at index 0?"],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w07-l01","lesson":1,"title":"Text files, modes and paths","chapter":"files","prepare":"Python editor, a dedicated disposable practice folder and a text editor. Keep original class data separate.","slides":[{"title":"Text files, modes and paths","strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Success means choose read/write/append deliberately, explain relative paths and preserve one record per line. Use only disposable practice files when demonstrating write mode.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 7 · Lesson 1","Save records and read them in a later run."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: keeping values","strategy":"Individual retrieval and pair explanation","notes":"Allow one minute without notes then compare. A list retains values while that program is running; a saved text file can be read in a later run. Traversal visits each item. Ask what information must persist beyond program exit before naming the file format.","body":["What does a list retain during a program run?","What could preserve records after the program exits?","What does sequential traversal mean?","Compare your explanation with a partner."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Three file modes","strategy":"Worked comparison and guided prediction","notes":"Read r requires an existing file. Write w creates a missing file or truncates an existing file when opened. Append a creates a missing file or adds at its end. Use an existing practice file containing 2, then ask what w and a would do before executing. with closes the file when its block exits, including after an exception.","table":[["Mode","Purpose","Existing file"],["r","Read saved text","Contents stay unchanged"],["w","Write new text","Old contents are replaced"],["a","Append new text","Old contents remain"]],"minutes":6},{"title":"A path starts from a location","strategy":"Teacher demonstration and paired explanation","notes":"Explain a relative name is resolved from current working directory, which can differ from script location. Demonstrate the actual school editor setting and locate the practice folder. An absolute path specifies its full location; avoid machine-specific paths in shared examples. Learners point to their folder without editing important files. Missing file in r raises FileNotFoundError.","body":["A path identifies a file’s location.","\"practice.txt\" is relative to the working directory.","The script folder and working directory can differ.","Locate your disposable practice folder before running."],"minutes":10},{"title":"Two records, two lines","strategy":"Predict, run, investigate","notes":"In the practice folder, w writes 2 followed by newline, then a writes 5 followed by newline. The file contains two records, 2 and 5, not 25. UTF-8 makes text encoding explicit. Predict first, run, inspect with a text editor. Re-running the whole snippet resets the file first because w comes before a; running only append again adds another 5. Emphasise both cases.","body":["Predict the file contents before running.","What changes if only the append block runs again?"],"code":"with open(\"practice.txt\", \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as file:\n    file.write(\"2\\n\")\nwith open(\"practice.txt\", \"a\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as file:\n    file.write(\"5\\n\")","minutes":10},{"title":"Reading the records in order","strategy":"Paired coding with role changes","notes":"Expected displayed lines 2 and 5, with a third 5 if only append ran again. strip removes surrounding whitespace, including newline, from the string. Driver runs a read; navigator compares actual file contents, then swap and append 0 before reading again. Support: provide with header. Stretch: explain why a file with no final newline can join a later append onto the last record.","body":["Read and compare with the text editor.","Append a new record, then read again."],"code":"with open(\"practice.txt\", \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as file:\n    for line in file:\n        print(line.strip())","minutes":12},{"title":"Which mode preserves earlier records?","strategy":"All-student hinge question","notes":"Correct C:a. w replaces old text and r does not permit writing. Collect simultaneous choices then explain. If several choose w, compare opening w with opening a on two disposable copies. File mode choice matters before any write call.","body":["You need to retain old records and add one at the end.","A: r     B: w     C: a","Choose alone and justify your choice.","Why must the newline convention stay consistent?"],"minutes":5},{"title":"Exit ticket: explain the file contract","strategy":"Independent exit ticket","notes":"Expected one non-negative integer per line, consistent UTF-8, newline after each record, a to add and r to read. A relative file path uses working directory. Collect individual explanations; use errors for a short mode/path reminder before validation.","body":["State a format for one quantity per line.","Choose modes for adding and reading records.","Explain what w does to an existing file.","Where does a relative path begin?"],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w07-l02","lesson":2,"title":"Conversion, range and retry","chapter":"files","prepare":"Python editor and a response/test table. Use whole-number quantities of zero or more.","slides":[{"title":"Conversion, range and retry","strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Success means distinguish conversion failure from range failure, catch expected ValueError and retry until a suitable quantity is entered. Validation checks rules, not whether a plausible value is truthful.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 7 · Lesson 2","Convert input, check its value and respond clearly."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: text becoming a number","strategy":"Individual retrieval and paired explanation","notes":"Expected input returns str. int(\"12\") succeeds; int(\"2.5\") and int(\"three\") raise ValueError. int(\"-3\") succeeds as conversion but violates a non-negative quantity rule. Ask learners to commit before comparing.","body":["What type does input() return?","Predict int(\"12\") and int(\"2.5\").","Does int(\"-3\") satisfy a non-negative quantity rule?","Compare conversion and range in your explanation."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Different reasons to reject input","strategy":"Classification and think-pair-share","notes":"Students classify individually, compare in pairs then justify. \"0\" and \"12\" convert and satisfy range. \"-3\" converts but fails range. \"three\" and \"2.5\" fail integer-text conversion. Accept zero explicitly. Support: two stages on paper. Stretch: explain why conversion alone is insufficient.","table":[["Text entered","Integer conversion","Non-negative rule"],["\"0\"","Succeeds","Passes"],["\"-3\"","Succeeds","Fails"],["\"three\"","Fails","Not checked"],["\"2.5\"","Fails","Not checked"]],"minutes":6},{"title":"Catching a specific conversion error","strategy":"Teacher modelling and guided prediction","notes":"With input three, int raises ValueError, so message prints. With 12, conversion succeeds and except does not run. Catch the expected error rather than bare except. This isolated fragment demonstrates conversion only: it does not yet retry or check range. Do not use quantity after failed conversion without a successful retry.","body":["Predict for inputs three and 12.","Which expected error does this handler catch?"],"code":"try:\n    quantity = int(input(\"Quantity: \"))\nexcept ValueError:\n    print(\"Enter a whole number.\")","minutes":10},{"title":"A separate range check","strategy":"Guided variation and peer explanation","notes":"This fragment assumes conversion already succeeded. With-3 it prints Quantity cannot be negative. With 0 it prints Accepted:0. With 12 accepted 12. Learners change the literal, predict and run. A value can be the right type but outside the allowed range. Avoid claiming this literal example handles arbitrary input by itself.","body":["Predict again with quantity = 0.","Why does a successful conversion still need this check?"],"code":"quantity = -3\nif quantity \u003c 0:\n    print(\"Quantity cannot be negative.\")\nelse:\n    print(\"Accepted:\", quantity)","minutes":10},{"title":"A retry loop combines the checks","strategy":"Paired prediction and implementation","notes":"Trace inputs three, then-3, then 0. First ValueError prints whole-number message. Second converts but prints negative message. Third passes >=0 and break leaves the loop with quantity 0. while True makes retry explicit; break exits only after suitable input. Swap driver/navigator for 2.5 then 4. Catch ValueError specifically. Support: trace conversion, range and action columns.","body":["Trace three, then -3, then 0.","When does break allow the program to continue?"],"code":"while True:\n    try:\n        quantity = int(input(\"Quantity: \"))\n        if quantity >= 0:\n            break\n        print(\"Quantity cannot be negative.\")\n    except ValueError:\n        print(\"Enter a whole number.\")","minutes":12},{"title":"Does valid mean truthful?","strategy":"All-student misconception check","notes":"Correct B: accepted 12 fits the declared type/range but could still be a wrong real-world quantity. Validation does not establish truth. Collect responses before discussion. Ask for a value that is plausible but mistyped, then contrast conversion/range rejection.","body":["The program accepts a quantity of 12.","“This proves the user entered the true quantity.”","A: agree     B: disagree","Choose and explain what the checks actually establish."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Exit ticket: choose useful input tests","strategy":"Independent exit ticket","notes":"Expected zero accepted, -1 range rejected, textword and decimal text conversion rejected, ordinary integer accepted. Handler ValueError. Explain retry with an unsuitable value followed by a suitable one. Use answers to decide conversion/range support before file processing.","body":["Give tests for zero, a negative and unsuitable text.","State the expected response to each.","Name the exception for invalid integer text.","Explain why a retry test needs a later suitable input."],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w07-l03","lesson":3,"title":"Sequential file processing and tests","chapter":"files","prepare":"Python editor, disposable quantities.txt copies and a test table. Main format: one non-negative integer on each line.","slides":[{"title":"Sequential file processing and tests","strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Success means read records sequentially, calculate a total and test stated assumptions. Learners distinguish empty, missing and malformed files. The first model assumes all records are suitable; later activities add an explicit rejection policy.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 7 · Lesson 3","Test file contents as well as program calculations."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: two checks on each record","strategy":"Individual retrieval and paired correction","notes":"A line is text; strip removes whitespace; int converts suitable integer text; >=0 checks range. Invalid integer text raises ValueError. Ask learners to recall these steps individually then pair-check before processing multiple records.","body":["A record contains \"-3\\n\".","What do strip and int do to it?","Which check still rejects the resulting quantity?","Compare the steps with a partner."],"minutes":5},{"title":"A total from valid records","strategy":"Teacher think-aloud and guided trace","notes":"Assume file exists and every line is a suitable non-negative integer. With lines 2,0,5, total moves 0,2,2,7 and output Total:7. Empty file body runs zero times, output 0. with handles closing. This model intentionally does not yet handle missing/malformed files, so state its assumptions before running.","body":["Assume every record is suitable.","Trace lines 2, 0 and 5, then an empty file."],"code":"total = 0\nwith open(\"quantities.txt\", \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as file:\n    for line in file:\n        quantity = int(line.strip())\n        total = total + quantity\nprint(\"Total:\", total)","minutes":6},{"title":"A test table from the file contract","strategy":"Independent prediction and paired review","notes":"Expected one-record 5 total 5;2,0,5 total 7; empty total 0. Missing file raises FileNotFoundError. Under a reject-file policy, a negative or malformed record rejects whole file and no successful total is displayed. Learners distinguish expectations of the initial assumed-valid model from required behaviour of an improved version. Support: separate calculation and invalid-file tests.","table":[["File case","Expected under stated policy"],["One valid record: 5","Total 5"],["Valid records: 2, 0, 5","Predict total"],["Existing empty file","Predict total"],["Malformed or negative record","Reject file and report"]],"minutes":10},{"title":"A helper checks one record","strategy":"Worked example and guided prediction","notes":"This helper returns an integer 0 or more for a suitable record, otherwise None. Predict parse_quantity(\" 5\\n\")=5, \"-3\"=None, \"three\"=None and blank line=None. None is a distinct rejection signal because zero is valid. It does not itself read a file or decide how the caller reports failure. Strip/conversion/range combine Week 5 functions with Week 7 checks.","body":["Predict for \"5\", \"-3\" and \"three\".","Why is None a better rejection signal than 0 here?"],"code":"def parse_quantity(text):\n    try:\n        quantity = int(text.strip())\n    except ValueError:\n        return None\n    if quantity \u003c 0:\n        return None\n    return quantity","minutes":10},{"title":"An explicit policy for unsuitable records","strategy":"Independent coding with optional scaffolds","notes":"Policy for this exercise: stop at first unsuitable record, report file rejected and do not display a partial total as successful. Reference structure: initialise total 0 and file_valid=True; read each line; call parse_quantity; if value is None set file_valid=False and break, else accumulate; after traversal display total only if file_valid. Handle FileNotFoundError separately with a missing-file message. Support: provide this scaffold. Test valid, empty, malformed, negative and missing cases. Stretch: track and report the rejected line number. Partners review evidence after independent work.","body":["Use parse_quantity while reading each line.","Reject the file on the first unsuitable record.","Report failure instead of a successful partial total.","Run valid, empty, malformed, negative and missing cases."],"minutes":12},{"title":"Empty and missing are different","strategy":"All-student hinge question","notes":"Correct B: an existing empty file opens and produces total 0 in the model; a missing file raises FileNotFoundError. FileNotFoundError differs from conversion ValueError. If several choose A, use two practice paths, one to an empty file and one absent. Do not replace a missing input with a new empty file silently.","body":["One path refers to an empty file; another has no file.","“Reading both gives an empty traversal.”","A: agree     B: disagree","Choose and identify the missing-file exception."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Exit ticket: evidence for the policy","strategy":"Independent exit ticket","notes":"For lines 2,three,5, improved policy rejects on second line and displays no successful total; initial model would raise ValueError. Existing empty file total 0. Negative text converts but fails range. Ask learners to name a specific observed result for each policy test and retain it before controlled practical.","body":["Predict the required response to lines 2, three, 5.","Should a partial total count as a successful result?","Explain empty versus missing-file behaviour.","Save a test record with an expected and actual result."],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w07-l04","lesson":4,"title":"Controlled practical briefing","chapter":"files","prepare":"Teacher supplies the fresh task, permitted resources, assessment conditions, submission location and marking materials separately. Public deck contains process guidance only.","slides":[{"title":"Controlled practical briefing","strategy":"Teacher-led assessment orientation","notes":"This is the existing Week 7 planner practical slot. Use the teacher-issued task and school assessment arrangements; do not treat this public deck as a complete assessment instrument. Keep the fresh scenario and marking materials in the teacher-controlled distribution channel. Suggested timing is a 55-minute session; adjust to the issued task.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 7 · Lesson 4","Apply the skills from Weeks 1–7 to the teacher-issued task."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Task and conditions","strategy":"Teacher-led briefing","notes":"Issue the fresh problem separately. Confirm permitted resources, collaboration/help rules, time available and what evidence must be submitted. Explain how students can request clarification of wording without receiving a solution. Do not show a worked answer during the controlled attempt. This slide introduces no new marks or weighting.","body":["Read the task supplied by your teacher.","Confirm the time and permitted resources.","Check the rules for assistance and collaboration.","Identify the required files and evidence to submit."],"minutes":4},{"title":"Requirements before code","strategy":"Individual task reading","notes":"Students independently identify required inputs, processing, outputs and data/file formats from the issued brief. They should distinguish task requirements from assumptions and seek permitted clarification. Apply only the features actually required by the task, rather than adding unnecessary file operations or algorithms.","body":["Identify the required inputs and outputs.","Note any type, range and file-format rules.","Separate stated requirements from assumptions.","Request permitted clarification of unclear wording."],"minutes":4},{"title":"A plan you can explain","strategy":"Independent planning","notes":"Students prepare a brief plan or pseudocode using only conventions/resources allowed in the task. Ask them to decide where selection, repetition, list processing or functions serve the actual problem. They record independent expected results before implementation. Teacher observation follows the issued assessment conditions.","body":["Plan the main processing steps.","Choose suitable program structures for the task.","Select tests from the stated requirements.","Calculate expected results before running."],"minutes":7},{"title":"Independent implementation","strategy":"Controlled individual work","notes":"Main implementation interval:20 suggested minutes. Students use the teacher-issued resources and follow its help rules. Teacher handles environment failures in accordance with the task conditions and keeps assessment observations separately. No model code or task-specific answers appear in the public deck.","body":["Implement your plan under the issued conditions.","Use clear names and consistent indentation.","Save your work in the required location.","Use the agreed process for technical difficulties."],"minutes":20},{"title":"Testing against the requirements","strategy":"Independent verification","notes":"Suggested 10-minute testing interval. Students compare prior expectations with actual results, include applicable normal/boundary/invalid/file cases and record corrections honestly. Which tests are required comes from the issued task. Preserve evidence of a mismatch and rerun rather than replacing expectations to match an error.","body":["Compare actual results with your predictions.","Test relevant boundaries and unsuitable input.","Check file contents and repeat runs where required.","Record corrections and rerun the affected tests."],"minutes":10},{"title":"Submission check","strategy":"Individual submission review","notes":"Allow five suggested minutes for the issued submission procedure. Students verify their own final files open and the requested code/design/test evidence is included. Avoid adding criteria absent from the task. Keep original assessment evidence intact and follow the teacher’s submission directions.","body":["Follow the teacher’s submission instructions.","Check the final code and required data files.","Include the requested planning and test evidence.","Confirm the submitted version is the intended version."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Reflection after submission","strategy":"Individual reflection and teacher follow-up","notes":"After the controlled attempt ends, students reflect without changing submitted evidence. Teacher decides when task-specific feedback or solutions may be discussed, especially if other classes still need to complete it. Use observations to plan Week 8 design/testing support rather than adding another assessment.","body":["Which skill helped you solve the task?","Which test found or prevented a fault?","Name one skill to strengthen next.","Follow your teacher’s directions for later feedback."],"minutes":3}]},{"id":"u1-w08-l01","lesson":1,"title":"Requirements and acceptance criteria","chapter":"design","prepare":"Open the design chapter and project-workbook.md. Use invented order IDs and quantities only. This is the existing learning project, not a new assessment.","slides":[{"title":"Requirements and acceptance criteria","strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Success means identify the organiser and problem, write measurable requirements R1 to R5, and link each to a predicted test. Explain that weeks 8 to 10 provide ten project lessons. School assessment conditions, if applicable, come from the teacher separately.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 8 · Lesson 1","Agree what the club-order manager must do."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: evidence from last week","strategy":"Individual retrieval and partner explanation","notes":"Allow silent retrieval before pair discussion. Zero is a valid quantity. int conversion alone accepts a negative integer. Append preserves previous records. A prediction is a test plan until an actual result is recorded. Use responses to revisit a single misconception before the project brief.","body":["Why must the quantity check accept zero?","Which file mode keeps earlier records?","Does a predicted result prove that a program works?","Explain one answer to a partner."],"minutes":5},{"title":"The organiser’s problem","strategy":"Teacher brief and student questioning","notes":"The school club sells badges for 300 cents each. The organiser records orders and needs a session summary. Use synthetic IDs, never student names or payment details. Ask students to identify inputs, processing and outputs. Clarify console application scope and the separation between the current session list and the persistent file.","body":["Badges cost 300 cents each.","An order contains an invented ID and a quantity.","Save accepted orders and summarise this session.","What must we clarify before designing the program?"],"minutes":6},{"title":"Requirements R1 to R3","strategy":"Worked example with paired completion","notes":"Model R2: quantities 0, 1 and 4 must produce 0, 300 and 1200 cents. Pairs expand R1 with letters, decimal text, a negative, zero and a valid retry. R3 keeps zero as an accepted order and preserves entry order. Students record independent expected results and test IDs in workbook section 1. These are planned checks, not completed test evidence.","table":[["ID","Required behaviour","Example check"],["R1","Validate quantity and retry","three, then 0"],["R2","Cost = quantity × 300 cents","4 gives 1200 cents"],["R3","Keep accepted session quantities","2, 0, 5 stays in order"]],"minutes":10},{"title":"Requirements R4 and R5","strategy":"Guided scenario analysis","notes":"R4 appends ID, quantity and cost with separate records, retaining earlier runs. IDs must be non-empty invented labels with no commas or line breaks. R5 reports current-session total quantity and cost and the first largest zero-based index. For [2,0,5], expect 7, 2100 and index 2. For [5,5], first index 0. For empty, show a clear message without indexing. Ask pairs to write acceptance criteria for persistence, ties and empty sessions.","body":["R4: append each saved order without losing old records.","R5: report session totals and first largest-order index.","An empty session needs a clear message.","Write a check for two runs, a tie and an empty session."],"minutes":10},{"title":"A measurable brief","strategy":"Organiser interview and independent writing","notes":"Pairs alternate organiser and developer roles. Refine vague wording such as easy to use into observable invalid-input recovery. Retain all five chapter requirements. Include ID restrictions, integer cents, writable-folder assumption and handling a save failure before adding to the session list. Support: requirement, input/action, expected response sentence frames. Extension: clarify what happens after a failed save. Each student records their own decisions and test links.","body":["Interview your partner about one unclear behaviour.","Record R1 to R5 with observable acceptance criteria.","Link each requirement to a planned test ID.","State assumptions and unresolved questions."],"minutes":12},{"title":"Which criterion can we check?","strategy":"All-student hinge question","notes":"Correct B. It defines an action and observable response. A is vague and C concerns appearance of code rather than required behaviour. Collect simultaneous answers with reasons. If several choose A, ask how two observers could independently agree whether the test passed. Rework one vague criterion together.","body":["A: The program should be easy to use.","B: After three, show a message and accept a retry of 2.","C: The program should contain lots of code.","Which gives an observable acceptance check?"],"minutes":5},{"title":"Checkpoint: an agreed brief","strategy":"Independent exit ticket","notes":"Collect workbook section 1: five requirements, each linked to a planned input/action and expected result. Ask for one assumption and one question needing a decision. Feedback should distinguish a requirement from an implementation choice. Students retain the brief for scheduling and design next lesson.","body":["Show R1 to R5 and a planned check for each.","Explain why zero must remain a valid order.","Name one assumption that could affect the design.","Keep the agreed brief in your project workbook."],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w08-l02","lesson":2,"title":"Dependencies and the project schedule","chapter":"design","prepare":"Use the agreed R1 to R5 brief, project workbook section 2, and a blank ten-lesson timeline. Paper or an approved spreadsheet is suitable.","slides":[{"title":"Dependencies and the project schedule","strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Success means sequence tasks using real dependencies, sketch a Gantt chart for the ten project lessons, and reserve time for corrections and user acceptance. The sample schedule is a proposal students may justify and revise.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 8 · Lesson 2","Plan the ten project lessons and the evidence due."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: a task needs a reason","strategy":"Individual retrieval and think-pair-share","notes":"Expected R4 concerns retained records over two runs, R5 includes ties and empty sessions. A measurable check compares an actual result to a predetermined expectation. Ask which requirement informs the record format. Link last lesson’s decisions to practical tasks in the schedule.","body":["Which requirement needs a second program run?","Which requirement needs a tied maximum test?","What evidence will show an agreed requirement is met?","Compare answers before opening your workbook."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Dependencies determine the order","strategy":"Card sort and justification","notes":"Give pairs task labels: agree record format, implement append, calculate cost, test cost, integrate modules, trial with organiser. Record format precedes append implementation. Calculation implementation precedes an execution test, though expected results can be designed now. Integration depends on compatible interfaces and tested components. Permit overlapping planning and testing when justified.","table":[["Task","Needs first"],["Implement file append","Agreed record format"],["Run calculation tests","Working calculation module"],["Integrate modules","Compatible tested interfaces"],["Organiser trial","Usable tested program"]],"minutes":6},{"title":"Ten project lessons","strategy":"Worked timeline and calendar check","notes":"The chapter allocates four project lessons in week 8, two in week 9 and four in week 10. Week 9 lessons 1 and 2 teach APIs/attribution and debugging, so do not count them as extra project workshops. P1 to P10 are local planning labels, not official lesson numbers. Today is P2. Have students label each timeline slot with its actual week and lesson.","table":[["Project slots","Course lessons","Focus"],["P1–P4","Week 8, lessons 1–4","Brief and design review"],["P5–P6","Week 9, lessons 3–4","Modules and storage"],["P7–P10","Week 10, lessons 1–4","Integration to evaluation"]],"minutes":10},{"title":"A Gantt chart proposal","strategy":"Teacher modelling and paired critique","notes":"Each filled dot marks a planned task period. Ranges refer to project slots shown on the previous slide. In P1 to P4, plan and desk-check tests rather than claim executed code results. Build in P5 to P7 includes unit and integration tests. P8 reserves whole-system tests and corrections, P9 the organiser trial and P10 demonstration/evaluation. Students redraw at ten individual slot resolution and refine. This is one feasible plan, not proof every task will finish on time.","table":[["Task","P1–P4","P5–P7","P8","P9–P10"],["Brief and design","●","","",""],["Build and unit tests","","●","",""],["System tests and fixes","","","●",""],["Trial and evaluation","","","","●"]],"minutes":10},{"title":"Your schedule and milestones","strategy":"Paired planning with independent records","notes":"Students complete workbook section 2 and draw a ten-slot Gantt chart. Include requirements, interfaces/data, implementation, tests/corrections, acceptance and evaluation. Record dependencies and evidence for design review at end of week 8, build review at end of week 9 and final demonstration in week 10. Distinguish planned and actual completion. Support: supply task names. Extension: justify which tasks can overlap without creating conflicting interfaces.","body":["Place tasks on a ten-slot Gantt chart.","Show dependencies and time for corrections.","Mark the design, build and final review checkpoints.","Keep planned and actual completion separate."],"minutes":12},{"title":"A delay needs a revised plan","strategy":"Scenario discussion and all-student response","notes":"Scenario: file storage does not pass its tests by the build review. A justified response identifies affected integration/persistence tasks, records the reason, adjusts remaining work and preserves essential testing and acceptance evidence. Avoid inventing completed dates or deleting requirements silently. Students propose a concrete change within available lessons and seek teacher feedback if scope is no longer feasible.","body":["File storage still fails at the build review.","Which later task depends on working storage?","What could you change within the available lessons?","Record the reason for your revised plan."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Checkpoint: a feasible schedule","strategy":"Independent exit ticket","notes":"Ask each student to show a dependency with an explanation and locate time for correction/retest. Check ten project slots rather than twelve. Retain the Gantt chart and workbook schedule, with actual progress left honest. Use plans to identify unrealistic workloads before module design.","body":["Show your ten project slots.","Explain one dependency.","Point to correction and retest time.","Save the schedule for the design checkpoint."],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w08-l03","lesson":3,"title":"Modules, data and pseudocode","chapter":"design","prepare":"Open the brief, schedule, workbook section 3 and unfinished project-starter.py. Prepare paper for a structure chart. No finished project solution is required.","slides":[{"title":"Modules, data and pseudocode","strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Success means divide responsibilities between modules, specify their inputs/results/side effects, agree data representations and describe the mainline algorithm. Students design their own implementation using the existing chapter brief.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 8 · Lesson 3","Design parts that can work together and be tested."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: functions and interfaces","strategy":"Individual retrieval and partner explanation","notes":"A parameter receives a supplied argument. return provides a result to the caller, whereas print displays output. Isolating order_cost lets us test without keyboard input or file changes. A structure chart shows module calls rather than every branch and loop. Use this as a diagnostic before presenting the chapter module names.","body":["What does a parameter receive?","How does return differ from print?","Why separate a calculation from keyboard input?","What must two cooperating modules agree?"],"minutes":5},{"title":"A structure chart shows calls","strategy":"Worked structure chart and verbal rehearsal","notes":"The indented tree represents the chapter’s structure chart: main coordinates all five subordinate modules. Box and connect the names on paper if preferred. It shows call relationships, not chronological control flow or a flowchart. main repeats the order steps and later requests the summary. Ask students to explain one responsibility and add input/result labels to their own chart.","body":["Which module coordinates the others?","Add the data each module receives or returns."],"code":"main\n├── read_order_id\n├── read_quantity\n├── order_cost\n├── save_order\n└── summarise_orders","minutes":6},{"title":"An interface is an agreement","strategy":"Worked contract and paired completion","notes":"read_order_id returns a valid invented label; read_quantity returns a valid integer >= 0. order_cost(quantity) returns integer cents and does no file or keyboard I/O. save_order receives ID, quantity and cost and appends one record; agree a failure signal with main. summarise_orders receives the quantity list and reports or returns a documented summary; choose and record a consistent interface. Table contains selected examples, not every module. Students complete all interfaces including side effects.","table":[["Module","Receives","Produces / changes"],["order_cost","quantity","cost_cents"],["save_order","ID, quantity, cost","Appended record / failure"],["summarise_orders","quantities list","Documented session summary"]],"minutes":10},{"title":"The data contract","strategy":"Guided design and peer explanation","notes":"The record example represents synthetic ID A01, quantity 2 and cost 600 cents. Append a newline after each record. IDs must be non-empty and contain no commas or line breaks. Session list [2,0,5] contains quantities from this run only. Use integer cents to avoid floating-point currency calculations. Decide who validates each field. Discuss the dependency: save successfully before adding to the session list, otherwise session totals could include an unsaved order.","body":["Session list: [2, 0, 5] contains accepted quantities.","File record: A01,2,600 followed by a newline.","IDs are non-empty with no commas or line breaks.","A failed save must leave the session list unchanged."],"minutes":10},{"title":"Mainline pseudocode","strategy":"Paired algorithm design and role change","notes":"This is language-neutral pseudocode, not runnable Python. Fill the SAVE failure policy explicitly: display failure and offer retry/cancel, without claiming a saved order or adding a quantity prematurely. Do not silently skip validation. Students annotate the loop, selection, function calls and data movement then write their own detailed algorithm and interfaces. Swap explainer/checker roles halfway. Support: use chapter pseudocode. Extension: show how a cancelled retry returns to the organiser menu.","body":["Explain what happens after a failed save.","Where will your empty-session check belong?"],"code":"quantities = empty list\nWHILE organiser chooses another order\n    READ validated ID and quantity\n    cost = order_cost(quantity)\n    TRY to save the order\n    IF save succeeds: append quantity to list\n    ELSE: report failure and offer retry or cancel\nSUMMARISE the session, including the empty case","minutes":12},{"title":"A stub exposes unfinished work","strategy":"Predict and explain","notes":"Calling this stub raises NotImplementedError and saves no file. It marks a deliberate placeholder, not a working save operation. Students compare this to a pass stub that might silently do nothing. They can inspect project-starter.py but should not run the whole unfinished program expecting success. Replace and test stubs in later build lessons.","body":["What happens if the mainline calls this now?","What evidence would show that the real module works?"],"code":"def save_order(order_id, quantity, cost_cents):\n    raise NotImplementedError(\"File append unfinished\")","minutes":5},{"title":"Checkpoint: a connected design","strategy":"Independent exit ticket and partner handover","notes":"Students retain a structure chart, every interface contract, list/file data description and mainline pseudocode. A peer should identify which module supplies each value and how failure reaches the caller. Expected one clear job per function, explicit side effects, current-session versus persistent storage distinction. Collect any unresolved failure policy for lesson 4 review.","body":["Keep the structure chart and interface contracts.","Include the session list and file record format.","Show validation, saving and the empty-session branch.","Explain one module to a partner using only your design."],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w08-l04","lesson":4,"title":"Desk-check and design review","chapter":"design","prepare":"Bring workbook sections 1 to 3, draft algorithms and a blank trace/test table. Use the chapter’s synthetic values. This checkpoint supports learning, with no new weighting or assessment conditions.","slides":[{"title":"Desk-check and design review","strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Success means trace a design by hand, find and correct a design fault and submit the existing week 8 checkpoint evidence. Distinguish desk-check predictions from actual program test results. Today is design review, not a demand to finish the full program.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 8 · Lesson 4","Check the design before the build workshops."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: the design contract","strategy":"Individual retrieval and peer explanation","notes":"Expect structure chart shows calls, zero is accepted, saving precedes session addition and the empty branch prevents an invalid first-element access. Ask students to locate the relevant part of their own design. Resolve missing save-failure decisions before the first trace.","body":["What does a structure chart show?","When may an order enter the session list?","Which branch handles a session with no orders?","Point to each answer in your own design."],"minutes":5},{"title":"A trace records changing state","strategy":"Teacher think-aloud and guided completion","notes":"Assume each save succeeds. Initially total 0 and empty list. A01 with 2 gives cost 600, list [2], total quantity 2. A02 with 0 gives cost 0, list [2,0], total remains 2 but order count becomes 2. A03 with 5 gives cost 1500, list [2,0,5], total 7 and cost 2100. The table shows predicted state, not executed code evidence. Ask students to compute before revealing each row and attach requirement IDs R2/R3/R5.","table":[["Saved quantity","Session list","Quantity total","Cost total"],["2","[2]","2","600 cents"],["0","[2, 0]","2","600 cents"],["5","[2, 0, 5]","7","2100 cents"]],"minutes":6},{"title":"First largest order","strategy":"Worked trace and misconception discussion","notes":"For [5,5], initialise best_index 0 after confirming the list is non-empty. At index 1, strict greater-than is false, leaving best_index 0. A >= comparison would incorrectly select index 1 under the first-largest requirement. Show both traces by hand. Student records index/current value/best index. A desk-check can expose this design fault before a program exists.","body":["Trace the first-largest index.","What would change if > became >=?"],"code":"quantities = [5, 5]\nbest_index = 0\nFOR index FROM 1 TO length(quantities) - 1\n    IF quantities[index] > quantities[best_index]\n        best_index = index","minutes":10},{"title":"Edge cases reveal design gaps","strategy":"Paired challenge and trace comparison","notes":"These are anticipated results. For -1 reject/retry without saving. No orders produces empty message without index access. [5,5] selects index 0. On file failure, keep session unchanged, report failure and offer agreed retry/cancel. Learners add decimal text, letters, invalid IDs and a second-run persistence test to the workbook. Do not treat a mock or predicted second run as actual file evidence.","table":[["Setup","Expected behaviour"],["Quantity -1","Reject and allow retry"],["No accepted orders","Empty message, no indexing"],["Quantities 5, 5","First largest index 0"],["File save fails","Report failure, list unchanged"]],"minutes":10},{"title":"A peer design review","strategy":"Structured peer review and independent revision","notes":"Pairs exchange designs and trace an ordinary case plus a different edge case without the author filling gaps verbally. Reviewer identifies exact step and requirement for any gap. Author revises, reruns the hand trace and records why. Retain original and revised evidence. Support: trace table with input, step, list and output. Extension: trace invalid ID followed by valid retry and ensure no duplicate saved record. Teacher checks feasibility and unresolved dependencies.","body":["Trace a partner’s normal case and one edge case.","Identify a step that is unclear or gives a wrong result.","Revise your own algorithm and repeat the trace.","Record the change and its reason in the workbook."],"minutes":12},{"title":"Prediction or execution evidence?","strategy":"All-student hinge question","notes":"Correct B. A desk-check is useful evidence of design reasoning but does not demonstrate actual code behaviour. Actual results remain blank until executed in build/testing lessons. Collect votes and request a sentence explaining why both are useful. Students label today’s traces as desk-checks and keep planned expected results ready for later execution.","body":["Your hand trace predicts both saved records remain.","A: This proves the file code works across two runs.","B: This still needs an executed persistence test.","Choose and explain the evidence you still need."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Week 8 design checkpoint","strategy":"Independent evidence check and teacher conference","notes":"Retain requirements linked to predicted tests, ten-project-lesson schedule with dependencies, structure chart/interfaces, data formats, pseudocode and desk-checks including corrections. This is the chapter learning checkpoint, not a new weighted assessment. Teacher gives one actionable next step. Week 9 lessons 1 and 2 address API contracts/attribution and debugging before project build workshops in lessons 3 and 4. Carry forward honest unresolved questions.","body":["Brief and predicted tests linked to R1 to R5.","Ten-lesson schedule with dependencies.","Modules, data contracts and pseudocode.","Desk-checks, revisions and one next build action."],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w09-l01","lesson":1,"title":"API contracts and source attribution","chapter":"design","prepare":"Official Python open/csv documentation, Python editor, disposable practice folder, week 8 module design and project-workbook.md section 7. Use synthetic data only.","slides":[{"title":"API contracts and source attribution","strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Success means explain an API beyond web services, identify inputs, outputs, side effects and likely errors from documentation, then record a reused source and the learner’s own contribution. This is the scheduled API/attribution lesson, before two project build workshops later in week 9.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 9 · Lesson 1","Use a documented interface and explain reused work."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: module agreements","strategy":"Individual retrieval and paired explanation","notes":"Allow silent recall, then compare with the week 8 design. order_cost takes a valid quantity and returns integer cents. save_order changes persistent storage and needs an agreed failure signal. An unfinished stub deliberately raises NotImplementedError. Use responses to connect student-defined interfaces to library interfaces.","body":["What does order_cost receive and return?","Which module changes the saved file?","How should the caller learn that saving failed?","Compare your module contracts with a partner."],"minutes":5},{"title":"An API is a software interface","strategy":"Worked comparison and classification","notes":"API means application programming interface. Programs use documented operations supplied by another component. Python’s open and the csv library are local examples. A web API adds remote communication, but this project needs no account, key or network service. The operation contract describes allowed inputs, results, side effects and errors. Ask learners to classify their own function contract and a library call without assuming all APIs use the internet.","table":[["Interface","Caller supplies","Result or effect"],["order_cost(quantity)","Valid quantity","Returns integer cents"],["open(path, mode)","Path and file options","File object or exception"],["csv.writer(file)","Writable file object","CSV writer object"]],"minutes":6},{"title":"Documentation detective","strategy":"Paired documentation reading and jigsaw explanation","notes":"Open the official open and csv.writer documentation linked in the teacher notes. One partner finds file mode, encoding and errors, the other finds writer creation, writerow and newline guidance. Partners explain and complete a four-part contract. Expected: append mode retains existing records, newline=\"\" lets csv control record newlines, encoding=\"utf-8\" makes encoding explicit, writerow receives a row sequence and writes it. Creation of a writer alone does not write a row. open can raise OSError, including PermissionError. Do not copy the entire documentation or memorise every optional parameter. Sources: https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#open and https://docs.python.org/3/library/csv.html#csv.writer","body":["Find the required input and a useful option.","Identify the returned object and the file side effect.","Find a documented error or failure to handle.","Explain your findings to your partner."],"minutes":10},{"title":"A local API in use","strategy":"Predict, run, investigate and change","notes":"Use only a disposable practice folder. This original demonstration appends A01,2,600 as one CSV row. Predict before running, inspect the text file, then change the ID to A02 and quantity/cost to 0 and 0. Both rows should remain. Re-running the unchanged snippet appends a duplicate, so discuss who controls repeated calls. csv writes the record separator, so do not add a separate newline field. The API can quote commas, but the agreed project ID restrictions still apply. The snippet assumes valid values and a writable folder; it is not a complete save_order implementation and has no failure handler. Explain that validation, caller policy and exception handling remain project responsibilities. Sources: official csv and open documentation.","body":["Predict the saved row, then inspect the file.","Change the synthetic order and check both rows remain."],"code":"import csv\nwith open(\"api-practice.csv\", \"a\",\n          newline=\"\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as file:\n    writer = csv.writer(file)\n    writer.writerow([\"A01\", 2, 600])","minutes":10},{"title":"A useful source log","strategy":"Worked example and independent source review","notes":"Model a real log entry for this lesson: Source: CS ATAR textbook, Unit 1 Week 9 Lesson 1, site URL and access date. Reused section: original csv practice snippet. Code licence: MIT, retaining the linked copyright/permission notice when distributing reused code. Changes: A01 to A02 and quantity/cost to zero. Own contribution: prediction, run evidence and interface explanation. Then record official Python documentation separately as a consulted reference, with URL, section, access date and what it informed; distinguish consulting documentation from copying its examples. Students audit one actual source from their work and record borrowed section, relevant permission/conditions, changes, own work and permitted assistance in workbook section 7. Follow school rules if used for assessment. If permission is unclear, use an approved source or ask the teacher before reusing. Crediting a source alone does not establish reuse permission.","body":["Record the author/title, URL and date accessed.","Identify what you used and its licence or permission.","Describe your changes and your own contribution.","Log one real source in workbook section 7."],"minutes":12},{"title":"Credit and permission","strategy":"All-student hinge question and scenario discussion","notes":"Correct B. Being publicly visible does not itself establish permission to copy and distribute. Naming the author is attribution, while the licence/permission sets conditions. Avoid a blanket judgement about all classroom copyright exceptions: here the question concerns publishing copied code in the shared textbook/project. Students should use an approved licensed alternative or seek guidance, then record required credit. This is a practical source review, not a new legal or assessment policy.","body":["You want to publish copied code with your project.","The page names its author but gives no reuse terms.","A: Naming the author alone settles permission.","B: Check permission and conditions before reuse."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Exit ticket: an interface you can justify","strategy":"Independent exit ticket","notes":"Collect a short contract for csv.writer/ writerow or open: supplied input, result/effect and a relevant failure. Students explain why csv does not enforce the project’s non-negative quantity rule or determine its save-failure policy. Check a completed source entry distinguishes reused material from original work. Next lesson is error classification and debugging; leave full project storage implementation to the scheduled build workshop.","body":["Describe one API call’s input and result or effect.","Name a failure your program must respond to.","Explain one rule the project must still enforce.","Submit your source entry and own contribution."],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w09-l02","lesson":2,"title":"Error classification and debugging","chapter":"design","prepare":"Python editor, project workbook test table and week 8 predicted tests. Use the original small faulty snippets in the slides; no changes to live assessment tasks or important data.","slides":[{"title":"Error classification and debugging","strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Success means distinguish syntax errors, execution exceptions and incorrect logic, reproduce a fault with a small case, find the first incorrect state and verify a correction with retests. This is a guided debugging lesson before the week 9 project build workshops.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 9 · Lesson 2","Find the cause of a failure and verify the repair."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: a contract predicts behaviour","strategy":"Individual retrieval and partner explanation","notes":"Allow silent answers before comparison. A contract describes inputs, results/effects and errors. A successful run may still calculate the wrong result. Expected results must come from the requirement and independent calculation. A broad handler does not repair an incorrect algorithm. Connect this lesson to last lesson’s API contracts and the existing project tests.","body":["What should an operation’s contract tell its caller?","Can a program finish and still give a wrong answer?","Where should an expected result come from?","Compare one example with a partner."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Three kinds of failure","strategy":"Classification sort and teacher modelling","notes":"Give three original examples verbally or in the editor: if quantity >= 0 with no colon causes SyntaxError; [2,0,5][3] causes IndexError during execution; quantity + 300 instead of quantity * 300 produces a wrong cost without necessarily raising an exception. Learners classify independently, then justify with when and how the fault appears. SyntaxError is a Python exception class too, but distinguish parsing failure from exceptions during ordinary execution for this teaching classification. Source: https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/errors.html","table":[["Failure","What happens","Useful evidence"],["Syntax","Python cannot parse the code","Message and indicated location"],["Execution exception","An operation raises an exception","Exception type and traceback"],["Logic","Behaviour violates the requirement","Expected versus actual results"]],"minutes":6},{"title":"A traceback narrows the search","strategy":"Predict, run and inspect","notes":"Before running, ask for valid list indices: 0, 1 and 2. The call raises IndexError at quantities[3]. In the actual editor show the traceback’s final exception type and the source line, noting wording can vary by Python version. Do not fix the index by guessing or hide the fault with a blanket except. For a required final element, len(quantities)-1 or -1 is valid only after deciding how empty data should behave. Students reproduce with three items, then discuss the empty case. Source: Python errors tutorial.","body":["Predict the valid indices, then run.","Read the exception type and the source line."],"code":"quantities = [2, 0, 5]\nprint(quantities[3])","minutes":10},{"title":"The first incorrect state","strategy":"Teacher think-aloud and paired trace","notes":"The requirement is total quantity 7 for [2,0,5]. This faulty loop prints 5. Trace after each iteration: expected totals 2,2,7 versus actual 2,0,5. The first divergence is after quantity 0. total = quantity replaces the total rather than accumulating. Change only the cause to total += quantity, then rerun. Retest [], [0], [4] and [2,0,5] expecting 0,0,4,7. Learners predict before executing and retain the failed trace as evidence.","body":["Expected total: 7. Trace every iteration.","At which iteration does the total first go wrong?"],"code":"quantities = [2, 0, 5]\ntotal = 0\nfor quantity in quantities:\n    total = quantity\nprint(total)","minutes":10},{"title":"Paired debugging clinic","strategy":"Driver/navigator debugging with role change","notes":"Assume the function receives a validated non-negative integer. It should return quantity times 300 cents. This original faulty version adds 300. Predict results for 0,1,4 before running: expected 0,300,1200 and faulty 300,301,304. Navigator identifies the first mismatch and proposes one change; driver edits to quantity * 300, runs all cases and records results. Swap roles, then reuse the previous loop tests including empty data. Record symptom, smallest failing case, cause, correction and retest in the workbook. Support: supply a two-column expected/actual table. Extension: explain how a negative-input contract would need a separate explicit decision; do not silently expand this exercise’s assumptions.","body":["Expected cents for quantities 0, 1, 4: 0, 300, 1200.","Predict, reproduce, isolate, repair and retest."],"code":"def order_cost(quantity):\n    return quantity + 300","minutes":12},{"title":"A repair needs regression checks","strategy":"All-student hinge question","notes":"Correct B. The failed case must now pass, and earlier cases should still pass after the change. Re-running previous tests checks for regressions, though a finite set does not prove every possible input. Ask for an empty or zero case that could fail after an apparently sensible change. If students choose A, demonstrate a special-case patch returning 0 for every quantity: it passes quantity zero but fails quantities 1 and 4.","body":["You fixed the zero-quantity cost case. What next?","A: Submit because that case now passes.","B: Rerun it and the earlier ordinary/boundary tests.","Choose and justify the evidence a repair needs."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Exit ticket: explain a fresh fault","strategy":"Independent transfer and exit ticket","notes":"Requirement: accept every whole-number quantity >= 0. The condition > 0 wrongly rejects zero, so this is a logic error. >= 0 is the correction for this isolated range fragment, which assumes successful integer conversion. Tests -1,0,1 should reject, accept, accept. Ask students to distinguish this from int(\"three\") raising ValueError. Collect a precise cause and retest plan, not just a category name. Use gaps to plan support in the calculation/input workshop next lesson.","body":["Requirement: accept whole quantities of zero or more.","Classify the fault, correct it and propose three tests."],"code":"quantity = 0\nif quantity > 0:\n    print(\"Accepted\")\nelse:\n    print(\"Rejected\")","minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w09-l03","lesson":3,"title":"Calculation and input workshop","chapter":"design","source":"https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#design","prepare":"Open the design chapter and its learning resources. Use paper or a local editor as appropriate, with fictional data and disposable practice copies. The suggested 55-minute lesson is flexible.","slides":[{"title":"Calculation and input workshop","body":["Unit 1 · Week 9 · Lesson 3","Connect validated input to a tested cost calculation."],"minutes":2,"strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Explain the goal and the expected independent outcome. Connect validated input to a tested cost calculation. This is ungraded textbook learning. Suggested times can be adjusted to the class. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#design. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"Retrieval and explanation","body":["What should quantity zero mean?","Where should input conversion happen?"],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual retrieval, then partner comparison","notes":"Zero is valid and costs zero cents. Conversion and range checks belong at the input boundary. A calculation function receives a validated quantity. Allow silent thinking before discussion. Ask a learner to justify an answer before revealing the explanation. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#design. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"The order contract","table":[["Part","Expected behaviour"],["Input","Whole quantity of zero or more"],["Calculation","quantity × 300 cents"],["Rejected input","Explain and ask again"],["Accepted input","Return the validated quantity"]],"minutes":6,"strategy":"Worked table and teacher explanation","notes":"Use the project requirements. Keep prices in integer cents. No need to add accounts or a new interface. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#design. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"Predicted costs","body":["Predict costs for quantities 0, 1 and 4.","Identify the input and returned result."],"code":"def order_cost(quantity):\n    return quantity * 300","minutes":10,"strategy":"Predict, model and explain","notes":"Expected costs are 0, 300 and 1200 cents. Ask learners to predict before running. The calculation assumes successful validation. Pause for predictions before discussing the expected result. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#design. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"Retry trace","body":["Trace inputs \"three\", \"-1\", \"0\".","Which input reaches the calculation?"],"minutes":10,"strategy":"Guided paired practice","notes":"Conversion fails for three. Negative one converts but fails the range rule. Zero is accepted and costs zero. Use driver/navigator roles, then swap. Swap explaining and checking roles so both learners articulate the reasoning. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#design. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"Independent practice","body":["Complete the input and calculation modules.","Test invalid text, a negative value, zero and four.","Record predicted and actual results."],"minutes":12,"strategy":"Independent application with optional support","notes":"Use a separate project copy. Accept equivalent function names. Check the retry loop actually continues and the calculation returns a value. Support with the chapter example, then ask for an independent changed input. Offer the relevant worked example if needed, then use a changed case to check independent understanding. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#design. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"A choice to explain","body":["The function prints 1200 but returns no value.","A: The caller receives 1200.","B: The caller receives None."],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Whole-class response and justification","notes":"B. Printing displays text. A normally completed call without an explicit returned value gives None. Ask what cost = order_cost(4) would store. Give thinking time and collect reasons, without recording scores. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#design. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"Exit explanation","body":["Explain how zero travels through your modules.","Name one failed test and its correction."],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual reflection and fresh explanation","notes":"Look for successful conversion, accepted range, returned zero cost and actual retest evidence. Keep storage integration for the next workshop. Use the response to select the next explanation or practice activity. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#design. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."}]},{"id":"u1-w09-l04","lesson":4,"title":"Storage and recovery workshop","chapter":"design","source":"https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#design","prepare":"Open the design chapter and its learning resources. Use paper or a local editor as appropriate, with fictional data and disposable practice copies. The suggested 55-minute lesson is flexible.","slides":[{"title":"Storage and recovery workshop","body":["Unit 1 · Week 9 · Lesson 4","Save accepted orders and preserve earlier records."],"minutes":2,"strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Explain the goal and the expected independent outcome. Save accepted orders and preserve earlier records. This is ungraded textbook learning. Suggested times can be adjusted to the class. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#design. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"Retrieval and explanation","body":["What does append mode retain?","When should an order enter the session list?"],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual retrieval, then partner comparison","notes":"Append retains earlier file text. Follow the project contract: successful saving precedes adding the accepted order to the session list. Allow silent thinking before discussion. Ask a learner to justify an answer before revealing the explanation. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#design. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"Storage checks","table":[["Case","Expected result"],["First successful save","One complete record"],["Second run and save","Earlier record remains"],["Write failure","Clear failure and no false success"],["Empty session","No maximum-index access"]],"minutes":6,"strategy":"Worked table and teacher explanation","notes":"Use only disposable synthetic files. Check the agreed CSV fields and record separator in the starter. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#design. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"Two runs, two records","body":["Predict the file after saving quantities 2 and 5.","Compare append with write mode."],"minutes":10,"strategy":"Predict, model and explain","notes":"With the agreed newline format, append gives two records. Opening in w truncates existing contents. Demonstrate only in a disposable folder. Pause for predictions before discussing the expected result. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#design. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"A failed save","body":["A save function reports failure.","Trace whether the session list should change."],"minutes":10,"strategy":"Guided paired practice","notes":"It should remain unchanged under this project contract. Do not claim success or create a summary entry for an unsaved order. Swap explaining and checking roles so both learners articulate the reasoning. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#design. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"Independent practice","body":["Connect storage to your accepted-order workflow.","Test two runs and a simulated save failure.","Keep a safe copy before changing files."],"minutes":12,"strategy":"Independent application with optional support","notes":"Use a stub returning False or the supplied controlled failure fixture. Do not change OS permissions on important files. Check before/after session state and file contents. Offer the relevant worked example if needed, then use a changed case to check independent understanding. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#design. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"A choice to explain","body":["Only the newest order survives the second run.","A: Inspect the file mode and record-writing path.","B: Add more input prompts."],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Whole-class response and justification","notes":"A addresses the reported storage failure. Verify a correction with two runs rather than changing unrelated interface behaviour. Give thinking time and collect reasons, without recording scores. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#design. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."},{"title":"Exit explanation","body":["Show the retained records from two runs.","Explain the outcome when saving fails."],"minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual reflection and fresh explanation","notes":"Expected: both valid records remain and a failed save does not falsely enter session data. Unfinished work becomes a specific next action. Use the response to select the next explanation or practice activity. Teaching reference: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#design. Use the current chapter and its linked resources."}]},{"id":"u2-w01-l01","lesson":1,"title":"Data and information","chapter":"database-foundations","source":"https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#database-foundations","prepare":"Open the database foundations chapter, equipment-loans-flat.csv, database-foundations-workbook.md and database-foundations-answers.md. Use paper or a school-approved spreadsheet. No SQL installation or student account is needed. Use the fixed reporting date of 8 April 2026. Teacher notes and explained answers are public.","slides":[{"title":"Data and information","body":["Unit 2 · Week 1 · Lesson 1","Use loan records to explain which items are overdue."],"notes":"Introduce the equipment desk scenario. Use loan records to explain which items are overdue. Activities are ungraded. Use only the supplied fictional loan records. Suggested timings are flexible. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#database-foundations.","minutes":2,"strategy":"Learning intention"},{"title":"The equipment desk question","body":["Which items are still on loan?","What extra facts would show which loans are overdue?"],"notes":"Allow quiet thinking before discussion. Required facts include LoanID, DueDate, Returned and the reporting date. The item identifier is also needed to name the equipment. A due date alone cannot establish overdue status. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#database-foundations.","minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual recall, then partner explanation"},{"title":"A record needs context","table":[["Stored value","Meaning in this activity"],["L001","A particular loan"],["2026-04-06","Its due date"],["Returned = False","The item has not been returned"],["8 April 2026","Our fixed reporting date"]],"notes":"These individual values are data. The conclusion that L001 is overdue is information derived from them using a stated rule. False is a text representation in the CSV and must be interpreted or converted appropriately when importing. The reporting date is fixed for this exercise, regardless of the actual date. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#database-foundations.","minutes":6,"strategy":"Worked table and discussion"},{"title":"Data, information and the overdue rule","body":["Data are stored values. Information answers a question using them.","A loan is active when its item has not been returned.","It is overdue when it is active AND due before 8 April.","Under this rule, an item due on 8 April is not overdue."],"notes":"Model L001: Returned=False, due 6 April, so active and overdue. Then model L002: Returned=False, due 8 April, so active but not overdue. Emphasise strictly before. Dates alone do not supply the full answer. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#database-foundations.","minutes":10,"strategy":"Teacher model with student predictions"},{"title":"Four loans to compare","table":[["Loan","Due date","Returned","Overdue?"],["L001","6 April","False","Explain"],["L002","8 April","False","Explain"],["L003","7 April","True","Explain"],["L006","7 April","False","Explain"]],"notes":"Partners apply both checks before revealing results. L001 and L006 are overdue. L002 is due today. L003 has been returned. Swap the explaining role after two rows. This table is a four-row extract, not the complete six-loan dataset. All dates are in 2026. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#database-foundations.","minutes":10,"strategy":"Paired practice with exchanged roles"},{"title":"The full six-loan report","body":["Open the supplied CSV. Check all six loan records.","List the active LoanIDs, then the overdue LoanIDs.","Write one sentence explaining each count."],"notes":"Use workbook questions 1–4. Four active loans: L001, L002, L004 and L006. Two overdue loans: L001 and L006. Students should record the date and rule beside the counts. Do not remove L005 merely because E02 also occurs in L002. Offer the model as support, then ask for an independent explanation. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#database-foundations.","minutes":12,"strategy":"Independent application"},{"title":"A date is only one part","body":["L003 was due on 7 April and has been returned.","A: It is overdue because its due date has passed.","B: It is not overdue because it has been returned."],"notes":"B follows the stated rule. Ask learners to name both conditions: an unreturned item and a due date before the reporting date. Collect reasons without recording scores. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#database-foundations.","minutes":5,"strategy":"Choice and explanation"},{"title":"Data, rule and conclusion","body":["Name one stored value and one conclusion from today.","Explain how a wrong return status could change the report."],"notes":"Example data: L001 has Returned=False and DueDate=2026-04-06. Example information: two loans are overdue on 8 April under the stated rule. If L003 were incorrectly recorded as unreturned, the report would wrongly include it. Correct calculations still depend on accurate records. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#database-foundations.","minutes":5,"strategy":"Reflection and a fresh example"}]},{"id":"u2-w01-l02","lesson":2,"title":"Repeated facts in a flat file","chapter":"database-foundations","source":"https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#database-foundations","prepare":"Open the database foundations chapter, equipment-loans-flat.csv, database-foundations-workbook.md and database-foundations-answers.md. Use paper or a school-approved spreadsheet. No SQL installation or student account is needed. Use the fixed reporting date of 8 April 2026. Teacher notes and explained answers are public.","slides":[{"title":"Repeated facts in a flat file","body":["Unit 2 · Week 1 · Lesson 2","Explain how repeated facts can create maintenance problems."],"notes":"Introduce the equipment desk scenario. Explain how repeated facts can create maintenance problems. Activities are ungraded. Use only the supplied fictional loan records. Suggested timings are flexible. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#database-foundations.","minutes":2,"strategy":"Learning intention"},{"title":"Rows and borrowing events","body":["Our flat register stores every loan in one table.","The same item appears in two loan records.","Does that prove one row is a mistake? Explain."],"notes":"No. Each row represents a borrowing event. E02 occurs in L002 and L005, which have distinct LoanIDs and different return statuses. Ask what one row represents before judging repeated identifiers. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#database-foundations.","minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual recall, then partner explanation"},{"title":"Two loans, one borrower","table":[["LoanID","BorrowerID","Borrower label","Item"],["L002","B002","Learner B","E02"],["L005","B002","Learner B","E02"]],"notes":"This extract repeats borrower and item details. In the full CSV, L002 is an active loan due on 8 April and L005 is a returned loan due on 4 April. The rows are legitimate history. Repeated labels create a maintenance risk when the label changes, even though repeated IDs may be necessary. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#database-foundations.","minutes":6,"strategy":"Worked table and discussion"},{"title":"An incomplete update","body":["Change B002’s label to Learner Bee in L002 only.","L005 still says Learner B for the same borrower.","Now the register gives two labels for B002."],"notes":"Demonstrate on a disposable copy or annotate the two rows on paper. The unchanged BorrowerID identifies the same borrower. Updating only one repeated label leaves an inconsistency. In a suitable relational design, one Borrower record stores the label and loans reference that borrower. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#database-foundations.","minutes":10,"strategy":"Teacher model with student predictions"},{"title":"The final-loan deletion","body":["E05 is the microphone in loan L006.","Imagine deleting L006 from the flat register.","Which equipment fact would this register lose?"],"notes":"Partners inspect the complete CSV. L006 is the only row describing E05 as a Microphone. Removing it loses this register’s only description of that item. Ask students to distinguish losing the loan event from losing an equipment fact. This is a paper simulation, not an instruction to delete the source dataset. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#database-foundations.","minutes":10,"strategy":"Paired practice with exchanged roles"},{"title":"A separate home for each fact","table":[["Table","Fact it keeps","Starter records"],["Borrower","Who borrows","4"],["Equipment","Which item","5"],["Loan","Each borrowing event","6"]],"notes":"Use workbook questions 5–7. Learners allocate BorrowerID and label to Borrower, EquipmentID and description to Equipment, and LoanID, borrower/item references, DueDate and Returned to Loan. The three sets have 4 borrowers, 5 items and 6 events. This is a responsibility sketch, not a formal ER diagram or the final project schema. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#database-foundations.","minutes":12,"strategy":"Independent application"},{"title":"A repeated identifier","body":["Someone deletes L005 because E02 already appears in L002.","What history disappears? What should identify each loan?"],"notes":"The returned earlier E02 borrowing event disappears. LoanID distinguishes each event. BorrowerID and EquipmentID can legitimately recur in Loan. Separate tables reduce repeated descriptive facts when designed well, but poor links or constraints can still cause errors. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#database-foundations.","minutes":5,"strategy":"Choice and explanation"},{"title":"A change to explain","body":["A borrower changes their display label.","Explain what you would update in each design."],"notes":"Flat register: update every occurrence for that borrower consistently. Related design: update the borrower’s one descriptive record and keep the loans referencing its identifier. Ask why changing labels should not erase borrowing history. A CSV may remain adequate for a small temporary list with one editor and manageable checks. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#database-foundations.","minutes":5,"strategy":"Reflection and a fresh example"}]},{"id":"u2-w01-l03","lesson":3,"title":"Database software and related tables","chapter":"database-foundations","source":"https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#database-foundations","prepare":"Open the database foundations chapter, equipment-loans-flat.csv, database-foundations-workbook.md and database-foundations-answers.md. Use paper or a school-approved spreadsheet. No SQL installation or student account is needed. Use the fixed reporting date of 8 April 2026. Teacher notes and explained answers are public.","slides":[{"title":"Database software and related tables","body":["Unit 2 · Week 1 · Lesson 3","Distinguish stored records from the software that manages them."],"notes":"Introduce the equipment desk scenario. Distinguish stored records from the software that manages them. Activities are ungraded. Use only the supplied fictional loan records. Suggested timings are flexible. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#database-foundations.","minutes":2,"strategy":"Learning intention"},{"title":"A home for a fact","body":["Which table should hold an equipment description?","Which table should hold the due date of one loan?"],"notes":"Equipment stores the item description. Loan stores an event’s due date. Ask learners to explain why an item can have different due dates in different borrowing events. Recall the separate responsibilities from Lesson 2. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#database-foundations.","minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual recall, then partner explanation"},{"title":"Three terms, different roles","table":[["Term","Meaning"],["Database","An organised collection of stored data"],["DBMS","Database management system software"],["RDBMS","Relational DBMS: manages data in tables"]],"notes":"DBMS means database management system. RDBMS means relational database management system. The equipment desk’s records are the database. A DBMS is the software that defines, retrieves and changes managed data. An RDBMS is a type of DBMS that uses relations, commonly introduced as tables. PostgreSQL is an example. Source: https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial-concepts.html . Every RDBMS is a DBMS, but DBMS is the broader term. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#database-foundations.","minutes":6,"strategy":"Worked table and discussion"},{"title":"A request to the software","body":["A staff member asks to record a returned item.","The application passes the request to the DBMS.","The DBMS applies configured rules and changes the data."],"notes":"Describe the flow in words and ask students to label user/application, DBMS and stored database in a simple paper sketch. A system may check access and data constraints before an update. Facilities depend on the product and configuration. Do not imply that every DBMS automatically enforces the desk’s intended permissions. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#database-foundations.","minutes":10,"strategy":"Teacher model with student predictions"},{"title":"The three-table sketch","body":["Place these facts: borrower label, item description, due date.","Include identifiers so a loan can refer to its borrower and item.","Explain why Loan still needs its own identifier."],"notes":"Use workbook questions 7–9. Borrower holds the label, Equipment the description, Loan the due date and references. LoanID identifies an event because a borrower can borrow the same item again. Sketch labels and links only. Formal keys and relationship notation follow in later chapters. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#database-foundations.","minutes":10,"strategy":"Paired practice with exchanged roles"},{"title":"One file, several related tables","body":["SQLite can keep several tables in one database file.","Explain why this can still be a relational database.","Then label database, DBMS and RDBMS in your sketch."],"notes":"SQLite is an embedded SQL database engine and a single file can contain multiple tables. Source: https://www.sqlite.org/about.html . Flat-file organisation concerns the logical structure of records, not simply the number of physical files. Students should distinguish the SQLite software from the file of stored records. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#database-foundations.","minutes":12,"strategy":"Independent application"},{"title":"A file-count misconception","body":["A database file contains Borrower, Equipment and Loan tables.","A: One file means the design must be a flat register.","B: The logical organisation determines the design."],"notes":"B. The existence of related tables is compatible with one physical database file. A CSV loan register and a SQLite database file need not have the same logical organisation. Ask learners to explain the distinction rather than choosing by file extension alone. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#database-foundations.","minutes":5,"strategy":"Choice and explanation"},{"title":"A sentence for each term","body":["Explain database and DBMS using the equipment desk.","Then explain how RDBMS fits within the broader term DBMS."],"notes":"Example: the organised equipment and loan records form the database. The DBMS software manages requests to those records. An RDBMS is a DBMS using the relational model. Accept equivalent explanations that keep data and software distinct. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#database-foundations.","minutes":5,"strategy":"Reflection and a fresh example"}]},{"id":"u2-w01-l04","lesson":4,"title":"Data independence and access","chapter":"database-foundations","source":"https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#database-foundations","prepare":"Open the database foundations chapter, equipment-loans-flat.csv, database-foundations-workbook.md and database-foundations-answers.md. Use paper or a school-approved spreadsheet. No SQL installation or student account is needed. Use the fixed reporting date of 8 April 2026. Teacher notes and explained answers are public.","slides":[{"title":"Data independence and access","body":["Unit 2 · Week 1 · Lesson 4","Explain which changes affect a report and who may change records."],"notes":"Introduce the equipment desk scenario. Explain which changes affect a report and who may change records. Activities are ungraded. Use only the supplied fictional loan records. Suggested timings are flexible. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#database-foundations.","minutes":2,"strategy":"Learning intention"},{"title":"A report that stays the same","body":["The DBMS adds an index to find loans faster.","Must a report now ask for different fields? Explain.","Physical data independence separates storage from requests."],"notes":"In this example, no. The report can still request LoanID and DueDate while the DBMS changes how it finds stored records. Introduce physical data independence as separation between storage details and application requests. Do not claim all changes preserve compatibility. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#database-foundations.","minutes":5,"strategy":"Individual recall, then partner explanation"},{"title":"Changes and report compatibility","table":[["Change","Effect on the report"],["Add a storage index","The same field request can still work"],["Rename DueDate to DueOn","A request using DueDate may need editing"],["Move to another DBMS","Conversion or query changes may be needed"]],"notes":"An index illustrates physical data independence. Renaming a column changes the schema that the request uses. Moving systems may need conversion even when both are relational. A copied file does not guarantee compatibility with every DBMS. Use workbook question 10 to explain the limit. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#database-foundations.","minutes":6,"strategy":"Worked table and discussion"},{"title":"Access follows a requirement","body":["Learners may view permitted equipment availability.","Authorised desk staff may record returns.","The system needs controls that enforce those permissions."],"notes":"Discuss the minimum access needed for each role. A read-only availability report should not permit arbitrary edits or expose private borrower details. Product capabilities and configuration matter. SQLite relies on application and operating-system controls rather than server-style user accounts. Use the chapter’s stated scope. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#database-foundations.","minutes":10,"strategy":"Teacher model with student predictions"},{"title":"A check for each permission","table":[["Role","Attempt","Required result"],["Learner","View permitted availability","Allowed"],["Learner","Alter a loan’s return status","Refused"],["Authorised staff","Record an authorised return","Allowed"]],"notes":"These are proposed paper checks for the stated requirements, not a live system test. Partners explain what evidence would establish each result in an implemented system. Merely hiding an edit button is insufficient if another route still permits the edit. Never change live school records for this activity. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#database-foundations.","minutes":10,"strategy":"Paired practice with exchanged roles"},{"title":"Workbook practice and corrections","body":["Complete questions 10–13, then revisit one earlier weak answer.","Compare with the public explained answers after your attempt.","Write the corrected rule and try a changed example."],"notes":"Use database-foundations-workbook.md and database-foundations-answers.md linked from the chapter. Q10: storage changes can preserve requests, schema changes may require edits. Q11: appropriately configured learner read access and staff update access. Q12: a small temporary list may suit CSV; reusable related facts and growing history favour relational management. Q13: counts depend on accurate return status and the fixed date/rule. No scores or assessment records are required. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#database-foundations.","minutes":12,"strategy":"Independent application"},{"title":"A changed overdue rule","body":["New rule: unreturned items due TODAY also count as overdue.","Using 8 April 2026, which extra loan qualifies?","Explain whether the stored data or the rule changed."],"notes":"L002 now also qualifies. The original records stay the same and the rule changes from DueDate \u003c reporting date to DueDate \u003c= reporting date, with Returned=False still required. There are now three overdue loans: L001, L002 and L006. Four loans remain active. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#database-foundations.","minutes":5,"strategy":"Choice and explanation"},{"title":"A final explanation","body":["Give one storage change that can leave a report unchanged.","Explain why owning a DBMS does not prove access is restricted."],"notes":"An added index can change storage access without changing the report’s field request. Access restrictions need suitable controls and correct configuration, with checks against the requirements. Invite a fresh example: if Returned is renamed to ReturnStatus, a request naming Returned may need updating. Students record one idea to revisit. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#database-foundations.","minutes":5,"strategy":"Reflection and a fresh example"}]},{"id":"u2-w02-l01","lesson":1,"title":"Entities, attributes and records","chapter":"entities-relationships","source":"https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#entities-relationships","prepare":"Open the entities and relationships chapter, relationships-workbook.md, relationships-answers.md, equipment-model.svg and the four model CSVs (Borrower, Category, Equipment, Loan). Use fictional data and a disposable copy or paper. No SQL setup or student account is required. Explained answers are public.","slides":[{"title":"Entities, attributes and records","body":["Unit 2 · Week 2 · Lesson 1","Explain what each table, row and field represents."],"notes":"Use the equipment desk scenario from Week 1. Explain what each table, row and field represents. All activities are ungraded. Suggested timings can be adjusted. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#entities-relationships.","minutes":2,"strategy":"Learning intention"},{"title":"The facts that need a home","body":["Why did repeating a borrower’s label cause a problem?","What does one row in the Loan table represent?"],"notes":"A label change could leave inconsistent labels for the same borrower in the flat register. A Loan row represents one borrowing event. Recall the separate homes for borrower, item and loan facts before introducing Category. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#entities-relationships.","minutes":5,"strategy":"Recall and partner explanation"},{"title":"Type, instance and attribute","table":[["Term","Equipment desk example"],["Entity type","Loan: the kind of event we record"],["Entity instance","L001: one particular loan"],["Attribute","DueDate: a property of a loan"],["Attribute value","2026-04-06: L001’s due date"]],"notes":"An entity is one distinguishable thing or event. An entity type groups similar entities. An attribute is a property. In this relational model, a table represents the entity type, a record represents an instance, and a column or field represents an attribute. The value 2026-04-06 is not the field definition itself. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#entities-relationships.","minutes":6,"strategy":"Worked example with teacher explanation"},{"title":"A row and its fields","body":["Loan L001 records one event for borrower B001 and item E01.","DueDate names a field. 2026-04-06 is L001’s value in it.","Another loan has its own row and its own values."],"notes":"Show L001 in model-loans.csv and compare it with L002. Ask learners to point to an entire record, a column heading and one field value. Keep DueDate as a date property of an event, rather than a permanent property of an equipment item. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#entities-relationships.","minutes":10,"strategy":"Prediction and teacher modelling"},{"title":"The new Category table","table":[["Table","What one record represents"],["Borrower","One borrower"],["Category","One item category, such as Audio"],["Equipment","One physical item"],["Loan","One borrowing event"]],"notes":"Category extends Week 1’s three-table sketch. Category assignments are a new design decision: Camera is Imaging, Tripod is Support, Recorder and Microphone are Audio. Model categories has three records, borrowers four, equipment five and loans six. A category may exist before any equipment refers to it. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#entities-relationships.","minutes":10,"strategy":"Paired practice with exchanged roles"},{"title":"Annotated table definitions","body":["Open the four model CSVs. Label a table, row and field.","Choose one attribute and give a stored value for it.","Explain why an equipment description cannot identify every item."],"notes":"Use workbook questions 1–2. E01 and E04 both have description Camera, so that description is not unique. EquipmentID distinguishes each physical item under the agreed rules. Ask for labelled examples rather than definitions copied without context. No SQL creation is needed. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#entities-relationships.","minutes":12,"strategy":"Independent application"},{"title":"The column and the value","body":["A classmate says “2026-04-06 is an attribute name.”","Explain the difference between DueDate and that date."],"notes":"DueDate is the attribute or column name. 2026-04-06 is the value of that attribute for L001. A record includes the values for one instance across its defined fields. Ask students to use a different Loan row as their fresh example. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#entities-relationships.","minutes":5,"strategy":"Misconception check with reasons"},{"title":"A fresh record","body":["A new item E06 also has the description Camera.","Explain why it needs its own record and identifier."],"notes":"E06 represents another physical item even if its description matches E01 and E04. It requires a unique EquipmentID and a valid required CategoryID. No new loan is implied simply by registering an item. A sample’s unique-looking description does not establish a permanent uniqueness rule. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#entities-relationships.","minutes":5,"strategy":"Reflection and a fresh example"}]},{"id":"u2-w02-l02","lesson":2,"title":"Primary, foreign and composite keys","chapter":"entities-relationships","source":"https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#entities-relationships","prepare":"Open the entities and relationships chapter, relationships-workbook.md, relationships-answers.md, equipment-model.svg and the four model CSVs (Borrower, Category, Equipment, Loan). Use fictional data and a disposable copy or paper. No SQL setup or student account is required. Explained answers are public.","slides":[{"title":"Primary, foreign and composite keys","body":["Unit 2 · Week 2 · Lesson 2","Choose keys that preserve valid borrowing history."],"notes":"Use the equipment desk scenario from Week 1. Choose keys that preserve valid borrowing history. All activities are ungraded. Suggested timings can be adjusted. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#entities-relationships.","minutes":2,"strategy":"Learning intention"},{"title":"Matching descriptions","body":["E01 and E04 are both Camera.","Which field distinguishes the items? What rule must it follow?"],"notes":"EquipmentID identifies each item. The chosen primary key must be unique and required. Descriptions and borrower display labels can repeat. A primary key may use one field or a group of fields. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#entities-relationships.","minutes":5,"strategy":"Recall and partner explanation"},{"title":"Keys and their jobs","table":[["Key","Job in this model"],["Candidate key","A minimal set that uniquely identifies a row"],["Primary key (PK)","The candidate key chosen for identification"],["Foreign key (FK)","A reference to a permitted key"],["Composite key","A key containing more than one attribute"]],"notes":"Minimal means no attribute can be removed without losing uniqueness under the agreed rules. Minimal does not mean shortest text. A composite key can be a primary key. A foreign key may refer to another table or the same table and its values may repeat. Our required foreign keys refer to existing parent primary keys. Source: https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/ddl-constraints.html . Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#entities-relationships.","minutes":6,"strategy":"Worked example with teacher explanation"},{"title":"A loan uses three identifiers","table":[["Loan field","Role","Example"],["LoanID","PK: identifies this event","L003"],["BorrowerID","FK to Borrower.BorrowerID","B001"],["EquipmentID","FK to Equipment.EquipmentID","E03"]],"notes":"Trace L003 to Borrower B001 (Learner A), Equipment E03 (Recorder), then Equipment.CategoryID C03 to Category C03 (Audio). Equipment.CategoryID is another required FK. The foreign keys identify the referenced borrower and item, not this loan event. BorrowerID repeats in Loan because borrowers can have multiple events. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#entities-relationships.","minutes":10,"strategy":"Prediction and teacher modelling"},{"title":"A pair that can repeat","body":["B002 borrowed E02 in L005 and again in L002.","The pair (BorrowerID, EquipmentID) repeats legitimately.","LoanID distinguishes these two borrowing events."],"notes":"These are the two existing events in the dataset. L005 is returned, L002 is active. Do not use the borrower/item pair alone as Loan’s key. Adding a new future event needs a new LoanID and separate checks against overlapping active loans. An FK alone does not enforce that business rule. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#entities-relationships.","minutes":10,"strategy":"Paired practice with exchanged roles"},{"title":"Keys that reject the right errors","body":["Find every PK and FK in the four model tables.","Explain duplicate LoanID L001 and borrower reference B999.","Workshop rule: one enrolment per borrower/workshop pair.","Which fields together could identify an enrolment?"],"notes":"Use workbook questions 2, 3, 6, 7 and 9 as selected practice. Duplicate L001 violates PK uniqueness. B999 is absent from Borrower, so a required FK must reject it. For the separate WorkshopEnrolment example, assume one enrolment per borrower/workshop pair: (BorrowerID, WorkshopID) can be a composite PK. Either field alone may repeat. Example pairs B001/W01, B001/W02 and B002/W01 are permitted, while another B001/W01 is not. The extension is not a fifth table in the equipment model. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#entities-relationships.","minutes":12,"strategy":"Independent application"},{"title":"A repeated foreign key","body":["B001 appears in the BorrowerID field of two Loan rows.","Does this break key uniqueness? Explain which key matters."],"notes":"No. BorrowerID is a foreign key in Loan and may repeat. LoanID is the chosen primary key of Loan and must remain unique. B001 must exist in Borrower for both references. A field can have different key roles in different tables. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#entities-relationships.","minutes":5,"strategy":"Misconception check with reasons"},{"title":"Rules beyond the sample","body":["A small dataset has no repeated borrower labels.","Explain why this alone does not make the label a candidate key."],"notes":"Candidate-key status depends on the intended rules for all valid data, including future records. The school may register two borrowers with the same label. A sample can expose a failed uniqueness claim but cannot establish a permanent uniqueness requirement by itself. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#entities-relationships.","minutes":5,"strategy":"Reflection and a fresh example"}]},{"id":"u2-w02-l03","lesson":3,"title":"Reading and drawing relationships","chapter":"entities-relationships","source":"https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#entities-relationships","prepare":"Open the entities and relationships chapter, relationships-workbook.md, relationships-answers.md, equipment-model.svg and the four model CSVs (Borrower, Category, Equipment, Loan). Use fictional data and a disposable copy or paper. No SQL setup or student account is required. Explained answers are public.","slides":[{"title":"Reading and drawing relationships","body":["Unit 2 · Week 2 · Lesson 3","Read relationship limits in both directions."],"notes":"Use the equipment desk scenario from Week 1. Read relationship limits in both directions. All activities are ungraded. Suggested timings can be adjusted. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#entities-relationships.","minutes":2,"strategy":"Learning intention"},{"title":"One borrower, several events","body":["Borrower B001 appears in loans L001 and L003.","Does each loan still refer to exactly one borrower?"],"notes":"Yes. The two directions have different counts. One borrower can have many Loan rows. Each Loan row has one required borrower reference. Ask students to state the direction before naming the relationship. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#entities-relationships.","minutes":5,"strategy":"Recall and partner explanation"},{"title":"Minimum and maximum participation","table":[["Direction","Minimum","Maximum"],["One borrower: how many loans?","0","Many"],["One loan: how many borrowers?","1","1"],["One category: how many items?","0","Many"],["One item: how many categories?","1","1"]],"notes":"Zero means optional participation. Exactly one means minimum one and maximum one. The Equipment–Loan rule follows the same pattern: an item can have zero or many loans over time, and each loan has exactly one item. Read the symbols at one end as the number there for one instance at the other end. Rules come from requirements, not only the observed sample. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#entities-relationships.","minutes":6,"strategy":"Worked example with teacher explanation"},{"title":"The four-table equipment model","body":[],"notes":"Two bars mark exactly one. A circle and a crow’s foot mark zero or many. Read Category–Equipment, Borrower–Loan and Equipment–Loan in both directions. Each parent permits zero or many children; each child requires exactly one parent. PK means primary key and FK means foreign key. Primary keys are underlined. Original diagram source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/equipment-model.svg . Notation reference: SCSA support booklet, printed page 15 (PDF page 19). The diagram’s placements can change without changing its meaning. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#entities-relationships.","minutes":10,"strategy":"Prediction and teacher modelling","diagram":{"kind":"equipment-er","source":"equipment-model-slide.svg","alt":"Four-table equipment ER model. Category to Equipment, Equipment to Loan, and Borrower to Loan each allow zero or many children. Every child requires exactly one parent. PKs are underlined and FKs labelled.","labels":["Category","CategoryID (PK)","CategoryName","Equipment","EquipmentID (PK)","EquipmentDescription","CategoryID (FK)","Borrower","BorrowerID (PK)","BorrowerLabel","Loan","LoanID (PK)","BorrowerID (FK)","EquipmentID (FK)","DueDate","Returned"]}},{"title":"Three relationship patterns","table":[["Pattern","Rule in an example"],["One to one","Each borrower has at most one locker, and vice versa"],["One to many","One item can have many loan events over time"],["Many to many","Borrowers can use many items, and vice versa"]],"notes":"The locker example states maximum participation only. Whether a borrower must have a locker is a separate minimum-participation rule. Borrowing across time is many to many between Borrower and Equipment; Loan resolves it into two one-to-many relationships and stores event facts. This does not permit two active loans for the same physical item simultaneously. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#entities-relationships.","minutes":10,"strategy":"Paired practice with exchanged roles"},{"title":"An independent four-table diagram","body":["Redraw the model with PKs underlined and FKs labelled.","State each connection in both directions.","Check the zero-or-many and exactly-one ends."],"notes":"Use workbook questions 4, 5 and 8. Hide the worked model while learners draw, then compare. Category–Equipment, Equipment–Loan and Borrower–Loan all have optional-many at the child end and exactly-one at the parent end. Preserve all four tables and their fields. Ask partners to explain how the required FK is on the many side. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#entities-relationships.","minutes":12,"strategy":"Independent application"},{"title":"A category with no items","body":["New category C04 has no equipment yet.","Does this break the model? What about an item with no category?"],"notes":"C04 is allowed because a category may have zero items. An Equipment row with no CategoryID breaks the required category rule. Students should point to the appropriate end of the relationship instead of assuming all participation is mandatory. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#entities-relationships.","minutes":5,"strategy":"Misconception check with reasons"},{"title":"History and current availability","body":["One item has several Loan rows across its history.","Explain why this does not mean it can be on two active loans."],"notes":"History records events over time. The minimum/maximum relationship does not by itself enforce the separate no-overlap rule for active loans. The application or database needs an appropriate additional check. Ask for one unreturned and one returned example rather than assuming every Loan row is current. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#entities-relationships.","minutes":5,"strategy":"Reflection and a fresh example"}]},{"id":"u2-w02-l04","lesson":4,"title":"Data dictionaries and model checks","chapter":"entities-relationships","source":"https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#entities-relationships","prepare":"Open the entities and relationships chapter, relationships-workbook.md, relationships-answers.md, equipment-model.svg and the four model CSVs (Borrower, Category, Equipment, Loan). Use fictional data and a disposable copy or paper. No SQL setup or student account is required. Explained answers are public.","slides":[{"title":"Data dictionaries and model checks","body":["Unit 2 · Week 2 · Lesson 4","Document each field and test the design against examples."],"notes":"Use the equipment desk scenario from Week 1. Document each field and test the design against examples. All activities are ungraded. Suggested timings can be adjusted. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#entities-relationships.","minutes":2,"strategy":"Learning intention"},{"title":"A diagram and a dictionary","body":["The diagram shows tables and their connections.","What else must a developer know before creating the fields?","A data dictionary defines each field’s type, size and rules."],"notes":"A data dictionary records field names, types, sizes, meanings and constraints. It complements the diagram. Include required/optional status, key roles, formats and defaults where relevant. The model uses conceptual types before mapping to a chosen DBMS. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#entities-relationships.","minutes":5,"strategy":"Recall and partner explanation"},{"title":"Loan identifiers in the dictionary","table":[["Field","Conceptual type / size","Constraint"],["LoanID","Text / max 4 characters","PK; required; L + 3 digits"],["BorrowerID","Text / max 4 characters","Required FK to Borrower"],["EquipmentID","Text / max 3 characters","Required FK to Equipment"]],"notes":"The dictionary gives maximum text lengths chosen for this exercise, not universal limits. BorrowerID follows B plus three digits and EquipmentID E plus two digits. Text preserves letters and leading zeros. Each FK refers to the matching identifier field in its parent table. The exact DBMS type and enforcement come later. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#entities-relationships.","minutes":6,"strategy":"Worked example with teacher explanation"},{"title":"Dates, return status and defaults","table":[["Field","Conceptual type","Rule"],["DueDate","Date","Required; display YYYY-MM-DD"],["Returned","Boolean","Required; default False for a new loan"]],"notes":"Date and Boolean storage sizes depend on the DBMS. A displayed date format is not necessarily the storage format. The False default applies when a new loan omits a status value; it does not reset historical loans. CSV values are text encodings until converted. Choose integer for counts, float for approximate measured mass, text for labels, date for due date and Boolean for return status. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#entities-relationships.","minutes":10,"strategy":"Prediction and teacher modelling"},{"title":"Counterexamples for a design","table":[["Proposed data change","What to check"],["Another LoanID L001","Primary-key uniqueness"],["Loan with BorrowerID B999","Required valid borrower reference"],["Delete referenced borrower B001","Policy preserving referenced history"],["New category C04 with no items","Optional-many participation"]],"notes":"Partners state allowed/refused and explain the specific rule. Duplicate L001 and nonexistent B999 are invalid. For this learning model, restrict deletion of B001 while loans refer to it until references are resolved deliberately. C04 with no equipment is allowed. Format checks alone cannot prove a stored fact is true. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#entities-relationships.","minutes":10,"strategy":"Paired practice with exchanged roles"},{"title":"Dictionary and workbook practice","body":["Write all five Loan field definitions, including constraints.","Complete the relevant workbook questions independently.","Compare the explained answers, then correct one weak rule."],"notes":"Use workbook questions 10–12 and revisit question 7. Loan has LoanID, BorrowerID, EquipmentID, DueDate and Returned. Include type, maximum text size or DBMS-dependent size, meaning, key/reference, required status and relevant format/default. Identifiers are text. Default False applies to new loans. Ask for a justified deletion policy that keeps loan history. Support learners with the worked tables before a fresh independent example. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#entities-relationships.","minutes":12,"strategy":"Independent application"},{"title":"A default is not a bulk update","body":["A returned historical loan has Returned=True.","The design specifies default False for new loans.","Does the default change that old loan? Explain."],"notes":"No. A default supplies a value for a new record when omitted in the appropriate operation. It does not rewrite existing records. Changing an existing record needs an explicit update, which must also meet the configured constraints. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#entities-relationships.","minutes":5,"strategy":"Misconception check with reasons"},{"title":"A new requirement changes the model","body":["New rule: one loan event may contain several equipment items.","Can one EquipmentID field represent every item in that event?"],"notes":"A single EquipmentID stores one item reference. Do not overwrite the field or store a comma-separated list while claiming the existing design is unchanged. Further modelling could separate the loan event from its item lines in a linking/detail table, with agreed item and return rules. Ask which facts belong to the whole event and which belong to each item. This is an extension discussion, not a silent change to the four-table dataset. Teaching source: https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#entities-relationships.","minutes":5,"strategy":"Reflection and a fresh example"}]}]};
